window.__SEARCH__=[{"u":"tutorials/01-first-render.html","t":"Render your first image","q":"Tutorials","h":"Install, render the Cornell box, and watch noise fall as you raise the sample count. Tutorials Render your first image Install, render the Cornell box, and watch noise fall as you raise the sample count. Source: docs/tutorials/01-first-render.md learning-oriented We are going to install pt , render the Cornell box, and change one number to watch Monte Carlo noise fall away. By the end you will have three images on disk and a feel for what a sample buys you. You will need Python 3.10 or newer, and about ten minutes. No GPU. No display. Step 1 \u2014 Install it # From the root of the repository: python -m venv .venv .venv/bin/pip install -e \".[dev]\" That pulls in NumPy, Taichi and DearPyGui. It takes a minute or two the first time. Check that the command exists: .venv/bin/pt --help You should see a list of seven subcommands \u2014 render , gui , describe , edn , dump , flip , validate . We will use the first one now and meet three more in the next tutorial. Step 2 \u2014 Render the Cornell box # .venv/bin/pt render cornell --res 200 200 --spp 32 -o out/cornell.png The first two lines Taichi prints are its own banner and are not ours. Then: cornell-di: 200x200, 32 spp, 8 prims, 1 lights 1/32 spp ( 3.1%) 2.0 spp/s 32/32 spp (100.0%) 62.7 spp/s done in 0.51s png: out/cornell.png radiance: out/cornell_linear.npy meta: out/cornell.json Open out/cornell.png . We are looking at the Cornell box: a white room with one red wall and one green wall, a small bright lamp in the ceiling, and two spheres on the floor. Three things are worth noticing before we move on. The shadows are soft. Their edges fade rather than stopping dead, because the lamp has area : some points on the floor can see all of it, some can see part of it, and some can see none. The white walls are tinted. Near the red wall the floor is faintly red, and near the green wall faintly green. That is light bouncing off a coloured wall and onto a white one. The image is grainy , especially in the shadows. That is the part we are about to fix. Notice also that three files were written, not one. cornell.png is the picture; cornell_linear.npy is the same image before tone mapping, in linear radiance, which is what every measurement in this project is actually made against; cornell.json records the settings that produced them. Step 3 \u2014 Change one number # The grain is noise , and noise is what you get from estimating an integral with too few random samples. --spp is samples per pixel. Render the same scene with a quarter of them, then with eight times as many: .venv/bin/pt render cornell --res 200 200 --spp 8 -o out/cornell_8.png .venv/bin/pt render cornell --res 200 200 --spp 256 -o out/cornell_256.png Both finish in about a second. Open the three PNGs side by side \u2014 cornell_8.png , cornell.png , cornell_256.png . At 8 spp the shadow under the spheres is a mess of speckle. At 32 it is grainy but readable. At 256 it is smooth. The image has not changed at all between them; only our confidence in it has. You will also notice the improvement slows down. Going 8 \u2192 32 helps far more than 32 \u2192 256, even though the second step adds seven times as many samples. Monte Carlo error falls with the square root of the sample count: to halve the noise you need four times the work. That single fact is why the rest of this project exists \u2014 every estimator here is an attempt to get more out of each sample rather than to take more samples. Step 4 \u2014 Render a different estimator # The scene we just rendered samples the light uniformly. Here is the same geometry with ReSTIR DI, which reuses samples across neighbouring pixels and across frames: .venv/bin/pt render cornell_restir --res 200 200 --spp 32 -o out/restir.png Compare out/restir.png with out/cornell.png \u2014 same sample count, same resolution. The shadows are noticeably cleaner. That is the whole promise of resampling, and also the whole danger: an estimator that reuses samples can be made cleaner and wrong , and the difference is not something you can see. We will do exactly that in Catch a biased estimator . What we did # We rendered a scene, watched noise fall as the sample count rose, and saw a different estimator produce a cleaner image at the same cost. Next: Watch a scene lower \u2014 the same scene, seen at every stage between the text file and the kernels. It takes a few seconds and needs no GPU. If you would rather read about why resampling works before doing anything else, the interactive explainer starts from \"what is a reservoir\". Up Section overview Next Watch a scene lower"},{"u":"tutorials/02-watch-a-scene-lower.html","t":"Watch a scene lower","q":"Tutorials","h":"Follow one scene through every level of the pipeline, as text you can diff. No GPU, no display, a few seconds. Tutorials Watch a scene lower Follow one scene through every level of the pipeline, as text you can diff. No GPU, no display, a few seconds. Source: docs/tutorials/02-watch-a-scene-lower.md learning-oriented A scene here is not a config file that a renderer reads. It is a program that gets lowered \u2014 transformed step by step into more concrete forms until it reaches executable kernels \u2014 and every one of those intermediate forms is something you can print, save and diff. In this tutorial we will look at one scene at three different levels, and then diff two scenes against each other to find the exact point where the estimator changes. You will need the venv from the previous tutorial , and about ten minutes. Nothing here renders an image, so there is no GPU and no waiting. Step 1 \u2014 Read the scene as its author wrote it # .venv/bin/pt edn cornell This prints the scene as canonical EDN \u2014 the surface syntax, cleaned up. The first lines look like this: ( scene { :name \"cornell-di\" :settings { :seed 1234 :tonemap :reinhard :exposure 0 } :integrator { :type :direct-lighting :light-sampling :uniform :spp 256 :max-bounces 1 } :camera { :type :pinhole :eye [ 278 273 - 800 ] :look-at [ 278 273 - 799 ] :up [ 0 1 0 ] :fov 39.3 :resolution [ 512 512 ] :near 0.1 :far 5000 } Two things here will matter later. :type says which transport we are simulating \u2014 one bounce of light, or many. :light-sampling says how the light is sampled . They are separate axes, and they are separate on purpose: you can hold one fixed and vary the other. :seed is not decoration. Every random number this renderer draws is a pure hash of (pixel, sample, bounce, dimension, seed) \u2014 there is no random number generator with hidden state anywhere in the system. Change the seed and you get a different but equally valid image; keep it and you get the same image, every time, on any machine, no matter how the work was scheduled. But notice what you did not see. Compare the output above with the actual source in pt/scenes/cornell.edn and you will find the file is not the same text. The printed version has resolved names to indices, filled in defaults you never wrote, computed the camera basis from look-at and up , and connected each light to its geometry in both directions. What pt edn prints is the scene after five jobs have been done to it \u2014 resolve, default, normalize, stitch, expand \u2014 which is the level the rest of the system consumes. Step 2 \u2014 Read the schedule that will run # Two levels further down, the scene has become a graph of passes and then a flat schedule. Print it: .venv/bin/pt describe cornell ExecutionPlan: 4 steps, 8 resources -> 8 physical buffers schedule: 0: GeneratePrimaryRays reads[-] writes[primary_rays] dirty[camera,spp] 1: Intersect reads[primary_rays] writes[hits, aov_normal, aov_albedo, aov_depth] dirty[camera,scene,spp] 2: ShadeDI[uniform] reads[hits] writes[radiance_accum, moment2] dirty[camera,scene,spp] 3: ToneMap reads[radiance_accum, aov_normal, aov_albedo, aov_depth] writes[framebuffer] dirty[aov,camera,exposure,scene,spp] resources: slot 0 aov_normal [1,3] slot 1 aov_albedo [1,3] slot 2 aov_depth [1,3] slot 3 framebuffer [3,3] slot 4 hits [1,2] slot 5 moment2 persistent slot 6 radiance_accum persistent slot 7 primary_rays [0,1] Read it as four sentences. Shoot a ray through every pixel. Find what each ray hits. Shade the hits. Tone-map the result to a framebuffer. Each pass states what it reads and what it writes , and that declaration is enforced \u2014 a pass that reaches for a buffer it did not declare fails when the graph is built, not on some later frame. The dirty[\u2026] list is what makes the interactive shell cheap: drag the exposure slider and only the passes that declared exposure re-run, so every accumulated sample survives. The numbers in brackets next to each resource are its lifetime \u2014 the first and last step that needs it. hits is [1,2] : written at step 1, last read at step 2, dead afterwards. persistent means the buffer outlives the frame. Step 3 \u2014 Diff two estimators # Here is where it pays off. Print the schedule for the ReSTIR version of the same room and compare: diff <(.venv/bin/pt describe cornell 2>/dev/null) \\ <(.venv/bin/pt describe cornell_restir 2>/dev/null) < 2: ShadeDI[uniform] reads[hits] writes[radiance_accum, moment2] \u2026 --- > 2: ReSTIR.InitialAndTemporal reads[hits, reservoir_prev, hits_prev] writes[reservoir_temporal] \u2026 > 3: ReSTIR.Spatial reads[hits, reservoir_temporal] writes[reservoir_curr] \u2026 > 4: ReSTIR.Resolve reads[hits, reservoir_curr] writes[radiance_accum, moment2, aov_reservoir] \u2026 Steps 0 and 1 are identical. Step 3's tone map is identical. One shading node has expanded into three passes and some persistent reservoirs, and nothing else in the renderer changed. That is the claim the architecture makes about how these estimators relate to one another, and here it is as a diff rather than as a paragraph. Look at the two reuse passes closely: ReSTIR.Spatial reads[hits, reservoir_temporal] writes[reservoir_curr] It reads one buffer and writes a different one. That is not an accident and not a style choice. Spatial reuse means every pixel looks at its neighbours, so if the pass read and wrote the same buffer while running in parallel, each pixel would see an arbitrary mixture of already-updated and not-yet-updated neighbours. Two buffers cost memory and make the race impossible. Step 4 \u2014 Look at the buffers themselves # The last level is the data. Render a small image and write every cooked buffer to disk: .venv/bin/pt dump cornell --res 64 64 --spp 8 --dir out/artifacts wrote 17 arrays to out/artifacts aov_albedo aov_depth aov_normal framebuffer hits.light_id hits.material_id hits.n hits.p \u2026 These are plain .npy files. Load one and look at it: .venv/bin/python -c \" import numpy as np n = np.load('out/artifacts/hits.n.npy') print(n.shape, n.dtype) print('surface normal at the centre pixel:', n[32, 32]) \" Every intermediate the renderer produced is on disk, in a format anything can read. Nothing is hidden inside the engine. What we did # We looked at one scene at three levels \u2014 elaborated source, compiled schedule, cooked buffers \u2014 and used a diff between two of those levels to locate the single point where two estimators differ. The pipeline has more levels than the three we printed. In full it is: cornell.edn \u2192 forms \u2192 SceneAST \u2192 SemanticIR \u2192 RawGraph \u2192 ExecutionPlan \u2192 kernels read macro-expand analyze elaborate declare compile execute pt edn printed the SemanticIR, pt describe printed the ExecutionPlan, and pt dump printed what the kernels produced. The two levels with no CLI of their own are dumped by docs/explainer/capture_levels.py , and the interactive explainer has a scrubber that steps through all seven. Next: Catch a biased estimator \u2014 we make a renderer produce a clean, beautiful, wrong image, and then prove it is wrong. Previous Render your first image Next Catch a biased estimator"},{"u":"tutorials/03-catch-a-biased-estimator.html","t":"Catch a biased estimator","q":"Tutorials","h":"Render the same scene two ways, fail to tell them apart by eye, then measure the one that is wrong. Tutorials Catch a biased estimator Render the same scene two ways, fail to tell them apart by eye, then measure the one that is wrong. Source: docs/tutorials/03-catch-a-biased-estimator.md learning-oriented Noise and bias are different failures. Noise you fix with more samples; bias you fix with mathematics. The trouble is that a biased renderer does not look broken \u2014 it looks good , often better than the correct one, because whatever it is doing wrong is usually also smoothing. In this tutorial we will render the same scene two ways, fail to tell them apart by eye, and then catch the wrong one three separate ways with measurements. You will need the venv, and about ten minutes \u2014 one command in this tutorial runs for roughly two of them. No display needed. Step 1 \u2014 Render the same scene twice # bias_demo is a scene built for this purpose. Render it under two different reuse weightings: .venv/bin/pt render bias_demo --bias naive --spp 300 -o out/bias_naive.png .venv/bin/pt render bias_demo --bias mis --spp 300 -o out/bias_mis.png Each takes a few seconds. --bias chooses how ReSTIR combines samples borrowed from neighbouring pixels: naive weights each contributing stream by M_i / \u03a3M_j , and mis uses the generalized balance heuristic. Open the two PNGs and put them side by side. They are hard to tell apart. Look closely at the pools of light on the floor and you may notice that naive has narrower, brighter cores where mis has broader, softer ones \u2014 but if you did not already know which was which, you would not be able to say which one was wrong. You might well prefer the wrong one; it is visibly less noisy. Step 2 \u2014 Ask the harness # .venv/bin/pt validate bias_demo --res 128 128 --spp 400 --ref-spp 1500 --no-flip This runs for about two minutes. It builds a converged reference by brute force, then measures every estimator against it. Let us read the report a section at a time. The verdict # [bias] each estimator at 400 spp vs the reference (resampling M=8, sign-test stride 38px) uniform unbiased (within noise) mse=1.321e+00 expected_noise=3.273e+00 excess=-0.60x \u2026 RIS M=8 unbiased (within noise) mse=1.030e+00 expected_noise=1.328e+00 excess=-0.22x \u2026 ReSTIR mis unbiased (within noise) mse=1.287e+00 expected_noise=9.958e-01 excess=+0.29x \u2026 ReSTIR pairwise unbiased (within noise) mse=1.292e+00 expected_noise=9.705e-01 excess=+0.33x \u2026 ReSTIR naive BIASED mse=2.901e+00 expected_noise=6.242e-01 excess=+3.65x \u2026 1/M vs MIS: naive is 0.569x the energy of mis, excess +3.65x vs +0.29x -> the MIS weighting is doing necessary work here excess is the part of the error that both estimators' noise cannot account for. E[MSE] = var(estimator) + var(reference) + bias\u00b2 , so subtracting the two variances leaves the bias squared \u2014 which is why the report needs to know how noisy the reference is, not just the estimator. naive is missing 43% of the scene's energy ( 0.569x ). That is not a subtle defect. And you could not see it. The convergence curves # [convergence] MSE vs spp. A curve that FLATTENS has hit its bias ^ 2 floor; one still falling at ~ -1 is converging to the truth. uniform slope=-0.74 tail=-0.57 plateau= 1.1x floor ok 8:2.46e+01 32:7.94e+00 128:2.62e+00 512:1.19e+00 ReSTIR mis slope=-0.80 tail=-0.39 plateau= 1.0x floor ok 8:2.74e+01 32:7.73e+00 128:1.85e+00 512:1.08e+00 ReSTIR naive slope=-0.22 tail=+0.01 plateau= 2.6x floor BIASED 8:7.66e+00 32:3.24e+00 128:2.81e+00 512:2.84e+00 Read the four numbers on the right of the naive row: 7.66, 3.24, 2.81, 2.84 . The error falls, and then it stops falling \u2014 at 512 samples it is no better than at 128. It has hit a floor, and the floor is the bias squared. The correct estimators keep descending toward the reference's own noise ( plateau = 1.0x floor ); naive flattens 2.6\u00d7 above it. This is a second, independent detection. It does not need the run to converge, only for the curve to have turned over. The joke at the end # [variance] per-pixel variance of a SINGLE frame, 6 seeds, 90th percentile. uniform var(p90)=7.583e+00 (the baseline) RIS M=8 var(p90)=6.862e+00 1.11x better than uniform ReSTIR mis var(p90)=3.925e+00 1.93x better than uniform ReSTIR pairwise var(p90)=2.913e+00 2.60x better than uniform ReSTIR naive var(p90)=4.819e-01 15.74x better than uniform The wrong estimator is the cleanest image in the report \u2014 sixteen times less noisy than uniform sampling, four times less noisy than the correct weighting. That is the entire lesson. If you were choosing an estimator by looking at renders, or by measuring noise, you would choose naive without hesitating. It throws away 43% of the light and it is beautiful. Step 3 \u2014 Notice the row that does not fit # You will also have seen this in the convergence section: RIS M=8 slope=+0.13 tail=+1.16 plateau= 7.7x floor BIASED RIS is not biased \u2014 it is flagged here because its error on this scene is heavy-tailed: a handful of very bright pixels dominate the mean squared error, so one realization can land far above its own expectation and the fitted slope goes the wrong way. The [bias] section, which compares against both variances, correctly reports it as unbiased on the same run. This is worth seeing rather than hiding. MSE is the fragile statistic in this harness, and the way it fails is by producing a confident wrong answer in either direction. The project has a recorded case where the same effect made the detector report \"unbiased\" for an estimator losing a tenth of the scene's light \u2014 see A scene can be pathological in a way that breaks the statistics in the evidence log, along with the fix, which was to change the scene rather than the statistic. Step 4 \u2014 Understand what makes this scene special # Try the same measurement on the ordinary Cornell box: .venv/bin/pt validate cornell_restir --res 96 96 --spp 200 --ref-spp 600 --no-flip naive and mis come out indistinguishable. The bias is not there. Spatial reuse borrows samples from neighbouring pixels, so the intuitive place to look for its failure is a geometry discontinuity \u2014 an edge, a corner, a shadow boundary. That is the wrong place: the geometric similarity gate already rejects those neighbours, so it is doing, crudely, the job the MIS weighting does properly. The bias needs neighbours that pass the gate \u2014 same surface, same normal, same depth \u2014 and yet disagree about which samples are possible at all . bias_demo gets that with lights standing perpendicular to a flat floor: cos_light flips sign along a razor-sharp line across the plane, so two adjacent pixels are geometrically identical while one assigns zero probability to samples the other can produce. Building that scene was harder than writing the detector. What we did # We produced a clean, beautiful, wrong image, and then established that it was wrong three independent ways: excess error over the combined noise floors, a convergence curve that flattened above the reference's noise instead of at it, and a stable energy deficit that did not shrink with more samples. Two things to carry forward. Low variance is not correctness. It is the reason this project has a statistical harness at all. Every other kind of bug here can be found by looking at a render; this one cannot. Which statistic you measure decides whether you can see the effect. MSE found the defect on this scene. One bounce further out \u2014 on the indirect term, which is about 11% of a Cornell box \u2014 it does not: the MSE verdict reads \"unbiased (within noise)\" for estimators that the energy ratio resolves as broken at tens of sigma. Where next: Measure a change for bias is this tutorial turned into a working procedure, including the extra steps a GI change needs. The evidence log records every measurement this project has made, including the hypotheses that were eliminated. Inspect a single pixel if you have a display, to see what the estimator is doing rather than what it produced. Previous Watch a scene lower Next Inspect a single pixel"},{"u":"tutorials/04-inspect-a-pixel.html","t":"Inspect a single pixel","q":"Tutorials","h":"Click a pixel in the GUI and read the exact path that produced it. Tutorials Inspect a single pixel Click a pixel in the GUI and read the exact path that produced it. Source: docs/tutorials/04-inspect-a-pixel.md Requires a display learning-oriented Most renderers can tell you what colour a pixel came out. This one can tell you why : which light samples were considered, what each was worth, which one survived, and whether its shadow ray was blocked. In this tutorial we will click a pixel and read the estimator's actual working out. You will need the venv, and a display \u2014 this is the one tutorial that needs a graphical session. Everything else in these guides runs headless. About fifteen minutes. Step 1 \u2014 Open the shell # .venv/bin/pt gui cornell_restir Five windows appear: Render , Controls , Path Tree , Candidates and Debug 3D . The render starts accumulating immediately and gets cleaner as it goes; the status line in Controls tracks the sample count. Let it run for a few seconds until the image settles. Step 2 \u2014 Click a pixel # Click somewhere in the Render window \u2014 a spot on the floor in partial shadow is the most interesting, near the edge of a sphere's shadow. Path Tree fills in. Instead of \"Click a pixel in the Render view.\" you now have a tree: a primary ray, the surface it hit, and beneath it the light-sample candidates that were drawn for this pixel at the current sample index. Expand one of the candidate nodes and you get its details \u2014 which light it sampled, cos_light , its target value, its resampling weight w , its MIS weight m , and whether it was occluded . Candidates shows the same thing as a table. The column to look at is w / sum(w) : that fraction is the probability that candidate had of being selected. One row is marked chosen \u2014 that is the sample this pixel actually used. Here is the part worth pausing on. This is not a re-roll. The renderer did not save a log of what it did and play it back; it re-derived the path from scratch using the same hash of (pixel, sample, bounce, dimension, seed) the kernel used. That is why the numbers you are reading are the ones that produced the pixel, and not a plausible-looking second sample from the same distribution. Step 3 \u2014 Watch one sample at a time # In Controls , find the spp cap slider and drag it slowly. The primary ray jitters around inside the pixel, and the light sample wanders across the surface of the lamp. You are watching the Monte Carlo process happen one draw at a time \u2014 the very thing that averages into a smooth image. Now look at the Debug 3D window while you do it. The same information is drawn in space: the render camera as a frustum you can fly around, the primary ray in blue, rejected candidates dim, the survivor bright green. Temporally reused samples are pink and spatially borrowed ones are orange, with an arrow pointing back to the pixel they were borrowed from. Step 4 \u2014 Find the number the whole algorithm is about # In the Candidates table, look at M \u2014 the effective sample count for this pixel. With reuse warmed up it will read in the hundreds, even though only a handful of rays were traced for this pixel this frame. That number is the entire ReSTIR argument, made concrete: temporal reuse carries samples forward from previous frames and spatial reuse borrows from neighbours, so each pixel ends up summarising far more candidates than it ever paid for. Click a pixel right at a shadow boundary and compare. M will be lower there \u2014 the geometric similarity gate rejects neighbours that are not similar enough to borrow from, which is what stops reuse from smearing across an edge. Step 5 \u2014 Change the estimator while it runs # Back in Controls : strategy switches between uniform, RIS, ReSTIR DI. Watch the noise change. ReSTIR combine switches the reuse weighting between naive , mis and pairwise-mis . On this scene you will see essentially no difference \u2014 remember that; it is the subject of the next tutorial , and the reason it needs a purpose-built scene. AOV routes a cooked buffer straight to the display: normals, albedo, depth, reservoir weight. variance heatmap shows where the renderer is still uncertain, which is usually not where you would guess. temporal reprojection is the interesting one. Tick it, then drag the camera with the mouse. With it off, the image restarts from noise on every movement. With it on, the reuse follows the surfaces through the motion. What we did # We read a single pixel's estimate as evidence rather than as a number: the candidates it drew, what each was worth, which survived, and how many samples it was effectively summarising. Where next: Debug a single pixel is the same ground as a reference rather than a lesson \u2014 reach for it when you have a specific pixel misbehaving. pt/shell/ in the reference explains why click-to-inspect runs the pure Python core on the UI thread and never calls a kernel. Previous Catch a biased estimator"},{"u":"how-to/measure-a-change-for-bias.html","t":"Measure a change for bias","q":"How-to guides","h":"The protocol that turns \u201cthe tests pass\u201d into a correctness claim. How-to guides Measure a change for bias The protocol that turns \u201cthe tests pass\u201d into a correctness claim. Source: docs/how-to/measure-a-change-for-bias.md task-oriented You changed the estimator. Green tests are not a correctness claim and neither is a render that looks right \u2014 a biased ReSTIR produces a clean, beautiful, wrong image. This is the procedure that turns \"it works\" into a number. Work through it in order. The early steps are seconds and catch most mistakes; the later ones are minutes and catch the rest. 1. Change both twins # Every estimator lives twice \u2014 a pure-f64 reference in pt/core/ and a Taichi kernel in pt/kernels/ \u2014 and they are held to agreeing per sample , not statistically. concern reference kernel reservoir algebra, MIS weights core/reservoir.py inlined in kernels/restir*.py ReSTIR DI passes core/integrators/di_restir.py kernels/restir.py ReSTIR GI passes core/integrators/gi_restir.py kernels/restir_gi.py frame loop, history rollover core/render._render_restir Engine._make_advance + graph/builder.py If you added a random draw, you must also allocate a dimension in both core/rng.py and kernels/_common.py , or two \"independent\" draws silently become the same number \u2014 which looks exactly like plausible noise. See Add a draw . 2. Run the twin tests # The fastest useful signal in the repository: .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_kernels.py::TestRestirGiKernels -q .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_core.py -k reconnection -q They compare the reference and the kernel per pixel under all three weightings \u2014 including naive , precisely because it is the wrong estimator. The two implementations must agree about what the wrong answer is, or a later bias measurement would be measuring a kernel bug. Know this test's blind spot. The two implementations are supposed to compute the same thing, so a wrong pdf, a double-converted Jacobian or a doubled cosine satisfies the diff exactly as well as being right does. It proves transcription, not measure. That is what steps 3 and 4 are for. 3. Get a verdict # .venv/bin/pt validate <scene> --spp 400 Read three sections: [bias] \u2014 excess error over both estimators' noise. E[MSE] = var(est) + var(ref) + bias\u00b2 , so comparing against the reference's noise floor alone charges the estimator's own variance to bias and reports honest estimators as broken. [convergence] \u2014 an unbiased curve keeps falling near -1 ; a biased one flattens onto its bias-squared plateau. This fires earlier than the excess test, because it needs only the curve to have turned over. [variance] \u2014 measured per frame , never accumulated. Consecutive ReSTIR frames are correlated through the reservoirs, so averaging N of them is worth far fewer than N independent samples, and comparing accumulated means makes ReSTIR look worse than plain RIS. Converged references are cached by scene fingerprint in .ref_cache/ , so a repeat run is cheap \u2014 see validation/reference_cache.py . 4. If the change touches GI, measure the indirect term # Whole-image statistics cannot see a GI estimator. The indirect term is about 11% of a Cornell box, so a defect in the only quantity ReSTIR GI is responsible for arrives at a whole-image test divided by nine. A 25% spatial-reuse bias, once measured, read 0.9953 on total energy. .venv/bin/pt validate cornell_restir_gi --res 96 96 --spp 200 --ref-spp 1200 .venv/bin/pt validate gi_bias_demo --res 128 128 Four rules govern this measurement. Violating any of them produces a confident wrong answer. Difference against a one-bounce render. Use validation/gi.render_indirect and indirect_verdict rather than rolling the subtraction by hand. Vertex 0 keeps its RNG stream, so the direct term cancels exactly rather than averaging away. It is valid only for :uniform and :restir-gi , which share vertex 0's draws; require_shared_direct_term refuses the rest rather than returning a number nobody can interpret. Discard 64 warm-up frames. A ReSTIR run becomes the estimator under test over roughly m_cap frames, so an accumulation started at frame 0 averages in the path tracer it began as. Measured, that dilution turns 0.987 into 1.004 and hides the deficit completely. The reading settles by 64 frames and does not move through 256. Warm the no-reuse control identically. Both passes off at M = 1 is the two-bounce path tracer sample for sample; it must read 1.000 . That is what keeps the discard honest \u2014 otherwise the discard itself is a suspect. Read energy before MSE. On a term this small, bias\u00b2 never clears both estimators' variances, so the MSE-excess verdict stays inside its margin for an estimator losing a sixth of the indirect term. The energy ratio over the whole frame resolves the same defect at tens of sigma. pt validate prints energy in the first column and the MSE verdict beside it for exactly this reason. 5. Do not reach for these # Per-pixel probes. A single pixel's estimate is heavy-tailed enough to show fake bias on a direct-lighting control. Aggregate over the frame. FLIP. validation/flip.py ranks and localizes; it does not detect bias. A converged biased estimator is perceptually smooth and scores well. Use it for \"which of these looks closest\" and \"where do they differ\" \u2014 see Compare two renders perceptually . A render. Tone-mapped, the correct and the 40%-dark images of bias_demo are hard to tell apart, and the biased one is 15\u00d7 less noisy. 6. Write down what you measured # When a measurement settles something, add it to ARCHITECTURE.md 's Findings worth keeping and reference it from the code that depends on it. Record the hypotheses you eliminated. They are worth as much as the answer \u2014 one finding there records three eliminations and a non-finding, and every one of them is a test somebody else now does not have to repeat. See also # Invariants \u2014 the full checklist, with which test guards each contract. validation/ \u2014 the chain of trust and every statistic hanging off it. Catch a biased estimator \u2014 this procedure as a worked example, if you have not seen the harness fire. Up Section overview Next Compare two renders perceptually"},{"u":"how-to/compare-renders-perceptually.html","t":"Compare two renders perceptually","q":"How-to guides","h":"Run FLIP, read the map, and know the three questions it cannot answer. How-to guides Compare two renders perceptually Run FLIP, read the map, and know the three questions it cannot answer. Source: docs/how-to/compare-renders-perceptually.md task-oriented Every other statistic in this harness is a number about radiance. FLIP answers a different question: would a person see the difference, and where. Compare two images # .venv/bin/pt flip out/reference_linear.npy out/restir_linear.npy -o out/flip.png Both inputs are the linear .npy buffers, not the PNGs \u2014 pt render writes <name>_linear.npy beside every image for this reason. The output is a magma error map and a mean FLIP score on stdout. Get a map per estimator during validation # .venv/bin/pt validate cornell_restir_gi --flip-maps out/flipmaps pt validate reports mean FLIP per estimator in its own column and writes one map per estimator into the directory you name. In the GUI there is a FLIP vs reference view that lights up once a converged reference is in the cache. Read the map # Bright regions are where a viewer would notice a difference; dark regions are where they would not. FLIP is bounded per pixel , which is the property that makes it useful here: unlike MSE \u2014 which a single firefly has been measured to own at 25,000\u00d7 the median \u2014 nothing can dominate it. A map that is uniformly dim with one bright patch is telling you the truth about where your change landed. Know what it will not tell you # It is not a bias detector. A converged biased estimator is perceptually smooth and scores well. This is not a limitation to work around; it is the reason the energy and MSE sections of pt validate still carry the verdicts. If your question is \"is this correct\", go to Measure a change for bias instead. Do not quote the number against a published one. This implementation is independent and anchored against NVlabs' flip-evaluator . The achromatic path is exact \u2014 0.031368 against 0.031369 on a one-pixel edge shift, through the spatial CSF filter, both feature detectors, CIELab, HyAB and the redistribution. A residual sits in the two chromatic channels and reads high: about +0.01 of mean FLIP on this renderer's images, with the ordering between estimators preserved. That is what a column ranking estimators needs and is not enough to cite. tests/test_flip.py pins the exact half to 1e-4 and the chromatic half to the measured gap, so either improving or worsening the agreement fails a test rather than silently changing what the column means. Why the anchor exists # Implementing FLIP from the paper left it 7% high on every achromatic difference. What found that was not review \u2014 it was running NVlabs' evaluator once in a throwaway environment and diffing against it. The bug was in cmax , the distance the colour difference is normalized by. The natural derivation is the largest HyAB distance the sRGB gamut admits, green to blue, which comes to 308.12; the reference behaves as though it were 203.29. The value is now measured rather than derived. The general form is the same move validation/analytic.py makes one level down by re-implementing its own vector math: an anchor that shares code \u2014 or a derivation \u2014 with the thing it validates can be corrupted by the same mistake and agree anyway. See also # A metric worth having is worth anchoring, and the anchor found the bug \u2014 the measured table and the two constraints that pin the constant. validation/ \u2014 where FLIP sits in the chain of trust. Previous Measure a change for bias Next Dump and diff an intermediate"},{"u":"how-to/dump-and-diff-an-intermediate.html","t":"Dump and diff an intermediate","q":"How-to guides","h":"You changed the front end. Did the IR change the way you meant? How-to guides Dump and diff an intermediate You changed the front end. Did the IR change the way you meant? Source: docs/how-to/dump-and-diff-an-intermediate.md task-oriented Every level between the scene text and the kernels is a first-class artifact you can print and diff. Use that instead of guessing what a change did. Did the elaborated scene change? # .venv/bin/pt edn cornell > /tmp/before.edn # \u2026 make your change \u2026 .venv/bin/pt edn cornell > /tmp/after.edn diff /tmp/before.edn /tmp/after.edn pt edn prints the SemanticIR \u2014 the scene after resolve, default, normalize, stitch and expand. It is the level everything downstream consumes, so if this file is unchanged, no front-end change you made can have reached the renderer. This also round-trips: elaborate(read(to_edn(ir))) == ir is asserted for every bundled scene, so anything pt edn prints is a scene you can feed back in. Did the schedule change? # diff <(.venv/bin/pt describe cornell 2>/dev/null) \\ <(.venv/bin/pt describe cornell_restir 2>/dev/null) pt describe prints the compiled ExecutionPlan : the pass order, each pass's declared reads and writes, its dirty domains, and every resource with its lifetime interval and physical slot. This output is a stronger acceptance test than it looks. Because it prints slot numbers, requiring it character-for-character transitively pins declaration order, the topological tie-break, culling, the lifetime intervals and the greedy colouring's first-fit rule \u2014 five things with no numeric output of their own. The Racket port is held to it verbatim for exactly that reason. If you changed the graph and describe is byte-identical, you changed nothing the scheduler can see. Did the buffers change? # .venv/bin/pt dump cornell --res 64 64 --spp 8 --dir out/before # \u2026 make your change \u2026 .venv/bin/pt dump cornell --res 64 64 --spp 8 --dir out/after .venv/bin/python - <<'EOF' import numpy as np, glob, os for a in sorted(glob.glob( \"out/before/*.npy\" )): name = os.path.basename(a) x, y = np.load(a), np.load( \"out/after/\" + name) if x.shape != y.shape: print(f \"{name}: shape {x.shape} -> {y.shape}\" ) elif not np.array_equal(x, y): d = np.abs(x.astype(np.float64) - y) print(f \"{name}: {(d > 0).mean():.2%} of values differ, max {d.max():.4g}\" ) EOF Keep the resolution and sample count small and identical between the two runs. Because every draw is a hash of (pixel, sample, bounce, dimension, seed) rather than a stateful generator, two runs of unchanged code produce byte-identical buffers \u2014 so any difference at all is your change, not scheduling noise. That property is also the trap: if your change added a random draw without allocating a new dimension, the buffers will differ for a reason that has nothing to do with what you intended, and it will look like plausible noise. See Add a random draw . The two levels with no CLI # forms and SceneAST have no pt subcommand. Dump them with: .venv/bin/python docs/explainer/capture_levels.py which writes every stage for cornell.edn into docs/explainer/levels/ in one pass, shelling out to pt describe for the compiled plans so the output is the real CLI's rather than a reimplementation. Re-run it after changing the DSL front end or the graph builder, or the explainer goes stale. See also # Watch a scene lower \u2014 the same commands as a lesson, if you have not used them before. pt/graph/ \u2014 what the five compile stages do to produce that plan. Previous Compare two renders perceptually Next Debug a single pixel"},{"u":"how-to/debug-a-single-pixel.html","t":"Debug a single pixel","q":"How-to guides","h":"Path tree, candidate table, AOVs and the variance heatmap. How-to guides Debug a single pixel Path tree, candidate table, AOVs and the variance heatmap. Source: docs/how-to/debug-a-single-pixel.md task-oriented A pixel is too bright, too dark, or noisy where nothing else is. The shell can show you the estimator's actual working out for it. .venv/bin/pt gui cornell_restir Needs a display. Everything else in these guides runs headless. Read the estimate as evidence # Click the pixel in the Render window. Path Tree shows the primary ray, the surface it hit, and every light-sample candidate drawn for that pixel at the current sample index \u2014 each with the light it sampled, cos_light , its target value p\u0302 , its resampling weight w , its MIS weight m , its Jacobian, and whether it was occluded . Candidates shows the same as a table. w / sum(w) is the selection probability. M is the effective sample count. None of this is a replay. The path is re-derived from the same hash(pixel, sample, bounce, dimension, seed) the kernel used, so you are reading the sample that produced the pixel rather than a fresh draw from the same distribution. Click-to-inspect runs the pure Python core on the UI thread \u2014 no Taichi call ever happens there. What to look for # Symptom Look at Likely Pixel far too bright the chosen candidate's w and p\u0302 a target function evaluating non-zero where the contribution is zero, or a Jacobian blowing up at a grazing angle Pixel too dark, neighbours fine M , and whether streams were admitted a stream dropped instead of contributing its confidence \u2014 see empty streams Noisy only near an edge M on both sides of the edge the geometric gate rejecting neighbours, which is usually correct behaviour Fine still, wrong when the camera moves tick temporal reprojection , then drag the reprojection lookup, not the estimator Step one sample at a time # Drag spp cap in Controls. The primary ray jitters within the pixel and the light sample moves across the lamp \u2014 the Monte Carlo process, one draw at a time. If a pixel is wrong at one specific sample index and right at others, that index is the bug. See it in space # Debug 3D draws the same data geometrically: the render camera as a frustum, primary rays blue, rejected candidates dim, the survivor bright green, temporally reused samples pink, spatially borrowed ones orange with an arrow back to the pixel they came from. For a suspected reuse problem this is the fastest view \u2014 a sample being borrowed from somewhere it should not be is obvious as a line in space and invisible in a table. Look at the buffers instead of the image # The AOV combo routes a cooked buffer straight to the display: normals, albedo, depth, reservoir weight. A pixel whose normal is wrong is a geometry bug, not an estimator bug, and this tells you which in one click. variance heatmap shows where the renderer is still uncertain, which is often not where it looks noisiest. Dump cooked artifacts writes everything to .npy for offline comparison \u2014 see Dump and diff an intermediate . Compare estimators live # strategy switches between uniform, RIS and ReSTIR DI. ReSTIR combine switches the reuse weighting between naive , mis and pairwise-mis . If a pixel is wrong under all three weightings, the problem is not the weighting. Headless alternative # With no display, drive the pure reference directly \u2014 it is the same code the inspector runs: .venv/bin/python -c \" from pt.dsl import load_scene_file, scene_path from pt.core.pathtree import regenerate_path ir = load_scene_file(scene_path('cornell_restir')) tree = regenerate_path(ir, (128, 200), sample=0) print(tree.kind, len(tree.children), 'children') for c in tree.children[:3]: print(' ', c.kind, c.payload) \" primary 33 children candidate {'candidate_id': 0, 'light': 0, 'p_light': (319.0006\u2026, 548.7, 323.0381\u2026), \u2026} With no live reservoir state this shows the initial RIS candidates only \u2014 temporal and spatial reuse are omitted, not approximated. The GUI says so in a note when it is in the same position. To inspect a pixel under warmed reuse you need a ReservoirProvider carrying real buffers, which is what the shell supplies from its render worker. See also # Inspect a single pixel \u2014 the guided version. Engine, shell and CLI \u2014 the threading rules that shape the shell. Previous Dump and diff an intermediate Next Run the test suites"},{"u":"how-to/run-the-test-suites.html","t":"Run the test suites","q":"How-to guides","h":"Python, Racket, the markers that skip the slow and the graphical. How-to guides Run the test suites Python, Racket, the markers that skip the slow and the graphical. Source: docs/how-to/run-the-test-suites.md task-oriented There are two of them \u2014 Python for the renderer, Racket for the port \u2014 plus a mutation run that tests the tests. Set up # python -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -e \".[dev]\" Python 3.10 or newer. The GUI needs a display; everything else runs headless. Python # .venv/bin/python -m pytest # everything, ~6 min .venv/bin/python -m pytest -m \"not slow\" # skip the high-spp statistical tests .venv/bin/python -m pytest -m \"not gui\" # no display available Two markers are declared in pyproject.toml : marker what it selects slow high-spp convergence and bias tests \u2014 minutes each, and the reason a full run is ~6 minutes gui anything needing a DearPyGui viewport Combine them when you are on a headless box and in a hurry: .venv/bin/python -m pytest -m \"not slow and not gui\" The ones worth running while you work # .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_kernels.py::TestRestirGiKernels -q .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_core.py -k reconnection -q .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_graph.py -q The first is the reference-versus-kernel per-pixel diff \u2014 the fastest useful signal in the repository if you touched an estimator. tests/test_graph.py runs the whole graph suite against a FakeField with no GPU, so it is seconds rather than minutes; add graph tests there first. Building an Engine costs about 5 seconds \u2014 Taichi recompiles every kernel per engine, because each owns its own FieldsBuilder . Never construct engines in a loop. Use Engine.set_seed() , set_camera() and set_bias_mode() to vary a run. Racket # raco test port/racket # everything No installation needed \u2014 the tests require by relative path. To make (require restir/rng) work from anywhere: raco pkg install --link port/racket Mutation testing # port/racket/test/mutations.sh # ~3 min This deliberately breaks the port and checks that the conformance suite notices. Seventy mutations are caught; six are documented as not caught, with reasons. It is not optional, and it is not decoration. It has already caught a broken harness \u2014 the script grepped for \"failed\" where raco test prints \"failures\", so eight real mutations were reading as passes \u2014 and on step 3 it found two defects a 55,184-value fixture could not see: EPS is unreachable through any render, and every quad in every bundled scene is a rectangle, so the dot(u, v) terms of the quad solve vanish identically. The general form is worth keeping: a fixture generated from an implementation tests only the branches that implementation happens to take , and that set is smaller than it looks. See Verify the Racket port for the full workflow, including how to record a mutation that is expected not to be caught. The conformance guard # python docs/architecture/conformance/verify_conformance.py Checks this repository against its own frozen RNG vectors. Stdlib only \u2014 it is the one check that runs without a venv, because everything else depends on it. The documentation # python docs/site/build.py Also stdlib only. It fails on a dangling cross-reference, an unsupported Markdown construct, or drift between docs/site/findings.json and ARCHITECTURE.md . See Build the documentation . No linter # There is none configured, and no formatter. Match the surrounding code. Previous Debug a single pixel Next Verify the Racket port"},{"u":"how-to/verify-the-racket-port.html","t":"Verify the Racket port","q":"How-to guides","h":"Generate a fixture, check the port against it, then mutation-test the checker \u2014 the third step is not optional. How-to guides Verify the Racket port Generate a fixture, check the port against it, then mutation-test the checker \u2014 the third step is not optional. Source: docs/how-to/verify-the-racket-port.md task-oriented port/racket/ reimplements the thinking layers \u2014 pt/dsl/ , pt/graph/ , pt/core/ \u2014 as #lang restir/estimator . Kernels and the converged-reference oracle stay in Python. All eight steps are done. Verification has three parts, and the third is not optional. 1. Generate a fixture from Python # python docs/architecture/conformance/gen_conformance.py # step 1 \u2014 RNG python docs/architecture/conformance/gen_scene_conformance.py # step 2 python docs/architecture/conformance/gen_geometry_conformance.py # step 3 python docs/architecture/conformance/gen_reservoir_conformance.py # step 4 python docs/architecture/conformance/gen_reconnect_conformance.py # step 5 python docs/architecture/conformance/gen_graph_conformance.py # step 6 Each writes a JSON of golden values into docs/architecture/conformance/ . What each file pins is tabulated in Conformance vectors . Fixtures must be language-neutral JSON. Python's json.dump emits a bare Infinity , which is a Python extension that conforming readers reject. Encode non-finite values as strings and pass allow_nan=False . To check the Python against its own frozen vectors without regenerating anything: python docs/architecture/conformance/verify_conformance.py 2. Check the Racket against it # raco test port/racket Two claims are asserted separately , and the split matters: every compared value is within a 1e-12 relative tolerance, allowed for the three libm calls in the path ( tan in the camera basis, sqrt in every length, sin / cos in the sphere light); every compared value is exactly equal. Measured, the tolerance has never been used: all 55,184 values agree bit for bit. It stays because a different platform libm is a real possibility and the alternative is a suite that fails for a reason it cannot explain. But only the exactness check catches a one-ulp defect \u2014 and there has been one. Reverting the compensated sum below is invisible to the tolerance and immediately visible to exactness. Keeping them separate also makes failure legible: if the exactness check is the only one failing, the port is fine and the platform's libm differs. 3. Mutation-test the checker # port/racket/test/mutations.sh # ~3 min Deliberately break the port and confirm the suite notices. A fixture generated from an implementation tests only the branches that implementation happens to take , and that set is consistently smaller than it looks. What this has actually caught: A broken harness. The script grepped for \"failed\" where raco test prints \"failures\", so eight real mutations read as passes. EPS is unreachable through a render. Every ray a scene traces starts either at the camera or at a shading point already offset by shadow_epsilon , which is 1e-3 at minimum \u2014 an order of magnitude above EPS = 1e-4 . Deleting the guard changes no pixel of any scene. It still has to be right, because bounce rays are what it exists for. Every quad in every bundled scene is a rectangle. intersect_quad solves against a 2\u00d72 Gram matrix whose off-diagonal is dot(u, v) ; with rectangles that is exactly zero, so the two terms carrying it vanish identically. Corrupting one is a no-op on all nine scenes. Two acceptance gates that agree trivially on near neighbours. Only distant partners on the same plane separate them. Both of the first two are now covered by calling the intersection routines directly with hand-chosen rays, rather than by hoping a scene reaches them. Recording a mutation that is not caught # Some mutations should not be caught, and the reason is the useful artifact. Add it to the expected-uncaught list in mutations.sh with the reason written out \u2014 six are recorded there now. An unexplained entry is indistinguishable from a gap. Two host differences that will bite any port # CPython's sum() over floats is Neumaier-compensated since 3.12 , not a left fold: sum([0.1]*10) is exactly 1.0 where folding left gives 0.9999999999999999 . core/reservoir.py uses it in the balance heuristic's denominator and in NAIVE 's \u03a3 M_j . The disagreement appears only with three or more live streams , because compensated and naive summation coincide for two \u2014 which means it is invisible in the temporal merge and shows up only under spatial reuse. The port implements Neumaier explicitly rather than inheriting it: port/racket/vecmath.neumaier-sum . Python's negative list indexing has no equivalent in a conforming host. materials[-1] returns the last material and looks plausible; a port that raises is right, and the fixture is what has to change. Do not reorder the steps # Every step's acceptance is a per-sample diff against the Python, which is only possible because step 1 makes both draw the same numbers. Falling back to statistics is not an option: statistics on ~11% of an image will not see a defect losing a sixth of the indirect term. See also # Porting to Racket \u2014 the plan, the essential-versus-incidental split, and the traps already hit at cost. What the Racket port showed \u2014 status and the arguments the port settled by being written. Run the test suites . Previous Run the test suites Next Add a draw, a pass, a strategy, a scene"},{"u":"how-to/add-to-the-renderer.html","t":"Add a draw, a pass, a strategy, a scene","q":"How-to guides","h":"Recipes that list every file which must change together. How-to guides Add a draw, a pass, a strategy, a scene Recipes that list every file which must change together. Source: docs/architecture/reference/08-extending.md task-oriented This page renders docs/architecture/reference/08-extending.md , which lives in the reference directory for historical reasons. It is a how-to guide, so it is filed as one. Recipes. Each lists every file that must change together \u2014 the failure mode in this codebase is changing one half of a twin. Add a random draw # The most dangerous change in the repo, because getting it wrong looks like noise. Allocate a dimension in pt/core/rng.py : either a new *_STRIDE /slot pair under an existing bounce, or a new BOUNCE_* id. Mirror it in pt/kernels/_common.py . The constants are duplicated verbatim on purpose. Check it collides with nothing: dimensions are stride -indexed and some strides are runtime-scaled ( M \u00b7 CAND_STRIDE ). If your offset is fixed and the block is variable-width, it will collide for some M , invisibly. Run tests/test_kernels.py::TestRngParity and the per-pixel twin tests. Do not reuse an existing (bounce, dim) \"because that draw is unused here\". The GI candidate dimensions deliberately alias bsdf_dim(0, \u00b7) , and that alias is load bearing and documented; a second, undocumented one is a silent correlation. Add a target mode # pt/dsl/ir.py \u2014 extend TargetMode ; TARGET_NAMES derives automatically. pt/dsl/analyze.py \u2014 add the surface name to _TARGETS . pt/core/shading.py::target_phat \u2014 the DI branch. pt/core/reconnect.py::gi_phat_at \u2014 the GI branch, same three-way shape. pt/kernels/_common.py \u2014 target_phat and gi_target_phat . Twin tests, then a bias verdict \u2014 a new target changes the estimator , so it owes a pt validate run, not just green tests. FULL already exists as an enum member and is reserved: it includes visibility, so it traces a ray per candidate. Add a pass # Declare it in pt/graph/builder.py::assemble_di_graph : a PassDecl with reads , writes , params , build , and invalidated_by \u2286 DIRTY_DOMAINS . Create or import its resources \u2014 create for transient, import_persistent for anything crossing a frame. Write build_<pass>(cctx) -> execute(ctx) in pt/kernels/ . Capture only declared fields and compile-time params; everything shell-mutable is a kernel argument . If it participates in reuse, add the reference twin in pt/core/integrators/ and wire it into core/render._render_restir . Checklist: Reading and writing the same buffer is a race. Split it. Omitting spp from invalidated_by does not mean the pass is skipped when the sample advances \u2014 dirtiness also flows along data edges. A new persistent resource must be in zero_persistent 's reach (it is, via desc.transient ) and will never be aliased or culled. tests/test_graph.py runs the whole graph suite with FakeField and no GPU. Add there first; it is seconds rather than minutes. Add a strategy # The heaviest change; the existing four are the template. pt/dsl/ir.py \u2014 extend Strategy . pt/dsl/analyze.py \u2014 add to _STRATEGIES . pt/dsl/elaborate.py \u2014 the transport \u00d7 strategy matrix, with a new ERROR_CATALOG code for each illegal pairing. Do not let an invalid combination reach the graph. pt/graph/builder.py \u2014 a branch in assemble_di_graph . If it is a payload variation of an existing strategy, reuse the branch as ReSTIR GI does rather than writing a second graph. pt/core/integrators/<name>.py \u2014 the reference, exposing shade(...) , plus the four pass functions if it participates in reuse. pt/core/integrators/__init__.py \u2014 register in _BY_STRATEGY . pt/kernels/ \u2014 the twin. pt/scenes/<name>.edn \u2014 a scene that exercises it. If it resamples a new payload , the reservoir algebra should not grow a second copy. Add a ReuseDomain \u2014 a phat hook and a jacobian hook \u2014 and a device struct. See semantics.md . core/reservoir.py growing a second copy of the combination algebra is the outcome to avoid; the project's subtlest bugs have all lived in that one. Add a scene key # pt/dsl/analyze.py \u2014 the closed key set, and a parser in _PRIM_FIELDS if it is primitive-specific. pt/dsl/ast.py \u2014 the field. pt/dsl/elaborate.py \u2014 lower it; add an ERROR_CATALOG code if it can be wrong. pt/dsl/ir.py \u2014 the IR field, plus a with_* edit if anything varies it. pt/dsl/writer.py \u2014 emit it, or the round-trip law breaks. pt/kernels/scene_device.py \u2014 upload it, if the kernels need it. The round-trip test will catch step 5 for you. Nothing catches a missing step 3. Add a scene # Drop an .edn in pt/scenes/ . It loads by bare name everywhere. A scene intended to exercise an estimator needs design, not just geometry: \"Many lights\" is not a light count. Sixteen lamps in a ceiling grid look like the resampling regime but are not: every floor point sees them at similar distance and angle, target values differ by under 2\u00d7, and RIS beat uniform by ~2%. Scattering the same sixteen through the volume at varied heights, sizes and intensities is what creates the condition. Reuse bias needs neighbours that pass the gate and still disagree. Geometry discontinuities are the wrong place to look \u2014 the similarity gate already rejects those. On an ordinary Cornell box :naive and :mis differ by 0.2%, under the noise floor. bias_demo uses lights perpendicular to a flat floor, so cos_light flips sign along a razor line across a plane and two adjacent pixels are geometrically identical while disagreeing about which samples are possible . Avoid fireflies, or the statistics break. bias_demo v1 used small intense emitters; error went heavy-tailed, one realization landed 15\u00d7 above its own expectation, and the harness reported uniform MC as biased against a uniform reference with a convergence slope of +0.71 . reproject_demo v1 had posts through the back wall, putting wall points at distance ~0 from a samplable light point; MSE ran 25000\u00d7 the median and the detector read +0.14\u00d7 excess on an estimator losing a tenth of the scene's light. Moving the posts inside dropped MSE 37\u00d7 and the same test read +2.57\u00d7 and fired. Change the estimator at all # Read ARCHITECTURE.md 's \"Findings worth keeping\" first \u2014 the relevant entries will save you rediscovering a dead end already written down. Then: Change the reference and the kernel. Run the per-pixel twin tests. They are the fastest signal. Run pt validate <scene> for a verdict. Green tests are not a correctness claim; low variance is not correctness. For a GI change, measure the indirect term with 64 warm-up frames discarded , and read energy before MSE. See 07-invariants . Add what you measured to \"Findings worth keeping\", including the hypotheses you eliminated. Previous Verify the Racket port Next Commit with the signing agent"},{"u":"how-to/commit-with-signing.html","t":"Commit with the signing agent","q":"How-to guides","h":"Two failures that look alike and have different fixes. How-to guides Commit with the signing agent Two failures that look alike and have different fixes. Source: docs/how-to/commit-with-signing.md task-oriented Commits here are SSH-signed through the 1Password agent ( op-ssh-sign ). Two failures look alike and have different fixes. Your work stays staged either way, and --no-gpg-sign is never the answer. Could not connect to socket. Is the agent running? # Usually the agent is running fine and SSH_AUTH_SOCK is simply pointing somewhere else \u2014 a GNOME keyring, say. Point it at the right socket for the one command: SSH_AUTH_SOCK=~/.1password/agent.sock git commit -m \"\u2026\" Signing stays on. failed to fill whole buffer # The agent is genuinely locked. Unlock it, then retry the commit. If you are driving this through an assistant, ask \u2014 there is nothing to work around. git log --format=%G? prints N for everything # That is not a signing failure, and it is true of old commits here too. There is no gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile configured, so git cannot verify SSH signatures locally \u2014 it has no list of which keys to trust. Verification and signing are different operations. To confirm a signature actually attached, inspect the object rather than asking git to verify it: git cat-file commit HEAD | grep -c gpgsig 1 means the signature is there. Never do this # git commit --no-gpg-sign # no It produces a commit that looks fine locally and is unsigned in the history permanently. Both failures above are configuration problems with real fixes. Previous Add a draw, a pass, a strategy, a scene Next Build the documentation"},{"u":"how-to/build-the-docs.html","t":"Build the documentation","q":"How-to guides","h":"This site and the explainer, from a clean checkout. How-to guides Build the documentation This site and the explainer, from a clean checkout. Source: docs/how-to/build-the-docs.md task-oriented There are two built documents: this site, and the interactive explainer. Both are stdlib-only Python and need no venv. Build the site # python docs/site/build.py 41 files, 1128 KB 27 document pages \u00b7 4 section indexes \u00b7 40 findings \u00b7 71 search entries explainer: 262 KB open file:///\u2026/docs/site/dist/index.html Output lands in docs/site/dist/ , which is gitignored. Open index.html directly with file:// \u2014 nothing is fetched at runtime, so there is no server to run. The search index ships as a <script> assigning a global, because fetch() is blocked on file:// . --no-explainer skips folding in the interactive page, which is the only step that shells out. Build only the explainer # python docs/explainer/build.py # then open docs/explainer/dist/index.html It emits two files: index.html (standalone, with a theme toggle) and artifact.html (the bare fragment, for publishing as a hosted Artifact). The fragment carries no <!doctype> / <html> / <head> / <body> because the host supplies them; index.html wraps that identical fragment in the same skeleton, so what you read locally is what gets published. What the build refuses to do # It fails, loudly and with a line number, on: a dangling cross-reference \u2014 a Markdown link that resolves to neither a page of the site nor a file in the repository; a Markdown construct the renderer does not implement \u2014 images, reference-style links, setext headings, raw HTML blocks. Dropping a construct silently would produce a page that looks fine and is missing a paragraph; drift between docs/site/findings.json and ARCHITECTURE.md \u2014 the sidecar must name every finding and no others. Rename a finding and the build tells you which entry to update. Regenerating the inputs # Two steps need the venv and are run by hand, because their output is committed. This is deliberate: it keeps the build dependency-free. .venv/bin/python docs/site/capture_cli.py # pt --help transcripts, scene catalogue .venv/bin/python docs/explainer/capture_levels.py # every pipeline level for cornell.edn .venv/bin/python docs/explainer/prep_figures.py # explainer figures \u2014 needs out/*.png Run capture_cli.py after changing pt/cli.py or anything in pt/scenes/ , and capture_levels.py after changing the DSL front end or the graph builder. Neither is checked automatically; a stale capture produces a correct-looking page. prep_figures.py needs renders that are gitignored: .venv/bin/pt render bias_demo --bias naive -o out/bias_naive.png --spp 300 .venv/bin/pt render bias_demo --bias mis -o out/bias_mis.png --spp 300 Where to edit # Not in dist/ , and not in the HTML. Every page of the site renders a Markdown file that lives in the repository; each page names its source in the header line under the title. Edit that file. Adding a page means adding it to docs/site/nav.py , which is where the Di\u00e1taxis classification lives \u2014 the site does not infer which mode a document belongs to, because that judgement is the whole point. See also # Run the test suites \u2014 tests/test_docs.py covers the build's own guarantees. docs/site/README.md \u2014 what is committed and why. Previous Commit with the signing agent"},{"u":"reference/00-overview.html","t":"Overview: the lowering pipeline","q":"Reference","h":"The pipeline, the layer map, the estimator matrix, where everything lives. Reference Overview: the lowering pipeline The pipeline, the layer map, the estimator matrix, where everything lives. Source: docs/architecture/reference/00-overview.md information-oriented The pipeline # cornell.edn text \u2502 read pt/dsl/reader.py spans attached here \u25bc forms List/Vec/Map/Sym/Kw/Num/Str/Bool/Nil \u2502 macro-expand pt/dsl/macro.py applicative order, fixpoint \u25bc forms\u2032 \u2502 analyze pt/dsl/analyze.py shape checking, closed key sets \u25bc SceneAST level 2 pt/dsl/ast.py near-verbatim surface, spans kept \u2502 elaborate pt/dsl/elaborate.py resolve\u00b7default\u00b7normalize\u00b7stitch\u00b7expand \u25bc SemanticIR level 2.5 pt/dsl/ir.py frozen, resolved, SoA-friendly \u2502 declare pt/graph/builder.py passes state reads and writes \u25bc RawGraph level 3 pt/graph/builder.py no memory exists yet \u2502 compile pt/graph/compile.py order\u00b7cull\u00b7lifetimes\u00b7alias\u00b7guard \u25bc ExecutionPlan level 4 pt/graph/plan.py a schedule; no reasoning left \u2502 execute pt/kernels/ build_* thunks, then run \u25bc artifacts radiance \u00b7 AOVs \u00b7 reservoirs \u00b7 path trees Every arrow is a pure transform. Every level is dumpable: Level Command SemanticIR pt edn cornell ExecutionPlan pt describe cornell cooked buffers pt dump cornell --dir out/artifacts forms, SceneAST no CLI \u2014 docs/explainer/capture_levels.py Layer map # Path Lines Role pt/dsl/ ~2,020 The front end. Text to SemanticIR . pt/core/ ~2,284 Pure f64 reference. No Taichi, no I/O, no mutation. The spec. pt/graph/ ~862 Declare / compile / execute. Backend-injected, testable with no GPU. pt/kernels/ ~2,403 Taichi. Mutates fields freely; the contract lives at the pass boundary. pt/shell/ ~1,808 DearPyGui app, render worker thread, viewports. pt/engine.py 365 The object everything headless goes through. pt/cli.py 637 render \u00b7 gui \u00b7 describe \u00b7 edn \u00b7 dump \u00b7 flip \u00b7 validate validation/ ~2,204 The correctness harness. Ships as a top-level package. tests/ ~4,492 308 tests. Outweighs either implementation. The mass distribution is deliberate: the reference implementation is nearly as large as the fast one, because they are diffed against each other. The two load-bearing ideas # 1. Functional core, imperative shell \u2014 with the boundary at the pass. pt/core/ is genuinely pure. pt/kernels/ mutates freely, because pretending otherwise would make it slow and awkward for no benefit. The contract sits at the pass boundary instead: a pass may only touch resources it declared, and its declared outputs are a deterministic function of its declared inputs, params and seed. pt/graph/compile.py enforces this by handing each pass a scoped CompileContext \u2014 reaching for an undeclared buffer raises when the graph is built, not on some later frame. 2. Counter-based RNG. Every stochastic decision is hash(pixel, sample, bounce, dim, seed) . ti.random() is banned. Three consequences the design leans on: the pure reference and the Taichi kernel draw the same numbers , so they can be diffed per sample rather than statistically; clicking a pixel re-derives the path that was actually traced, so the inspector shows evidence rather than a plausible re-roll; the worker can be interrupted and restarted and still produce identical images. See 02-core for the dimension layout, which is a documented contract between pt/core/rng.py and pt/kernels/_common.py . The estimator matrix # Two orthogonal axes \u2014 what transport and how it is sampled : :uniform :ris :restir-di :restir-gi :direct-lighting (1 bounce) di_uniform di_ris di_restir rejected :global-illumination (\u22652) gi_uniform rejected rejected gi_restir (exactly 2) Rejections are elaboration errors with codes \u2014 GI_STRATEGY_UNSUPPORTED , GI_BOUNCE_EXACTLY_TWO , DI_BOUNCE_CONSTRAINT . See 01-dsl . integrator_for treats bounce count as the second axis: UNIFORM with max_bounces > 1 selects gi_uniform , not a fifth strategy. Every estimator lives twice # Concern Reference Kernel reservoir algebra, MIS weights core/reservoir.py inlined in kernels/restir*.py ReSTIR DI passes core/integrators/di_restir.py kernels/restir.py ReSTIR GI passes core/integrators/gi_restir.py kernels/restir_gi.py frame loop, history rollover core/render._render_restir Engine._make_advance + graph/builder.py Changing one without the other breaks tests/test_kernels.py::TestRestirGiKernels::test_agrees_with_the_reference_per_pixel and its DI siblings \u2014 which is the point of them. The algebra itself is not duplicated. core/reservoir.py holds one copy for both payloads, parameterized by a ReuseDomain . See semantics.md . Where to start reading # Changing the estimator \u2192 02-core , then semantics.md , then ARCHITECTURE.md 's findings. Adding a pass or resource \u2192 03-graph , then 08-extending . Adding scene syntax \u2192 01-dsl . Trusting a number \u2192 06-validation . Porting \u2192 porting-to-racket.md . Up Section overview Next pt/dsl/ \u2014 the front end"},{"u":"reference/01-dsl.html","t":"pt/dsl/ \u2014 the front end","q":"Reference","h":"Read, macro-expand, analyze, elaborate, the IR, the writer, the error catalog. Reference pt/dsl/ \u2014 the front end Read, macro-expand, analyze, elaborate, the IR, the writer, the error catalog. Source: docs/architecture/reference/01-dsl.md information-oriented Text to SemanticIR in four transforms. ~2,020 lines, the largest single layer, and roughly the part a Lisp host would hand you free \u2014 see porting-to-racket.md . Module Lines Role reader.py 283 text \u2192 spanned forms macro.py 251 form \u2192 form analyze.py 392 forms \u2192 SceneAST , shape checking ast.py 131 the SceneAST dataclasses elaborate.py 387 SceneAST \u2192 SemanticIR ir.py 231 SemanticIR + the enums errors.py 107 ElabError , Diagnostics , ERROR_CATALOG writer.py 176 SemanticIR \u2192 canonical EDN __init__.py 62 load_scene , load_scene_file , scene_path , analyze , elaborate Note: pt/dsl/__init__.py re-exports analyze and elaborate as functions , shadowing the submodules of the same name. import pt.dsl.analyze gives you the function, not the module. read \u2014 reader.py # Hand-written, not a library. One _TOKEN regex (ws / comment / open / close / str / kw / num / sym) and a recursive-descent _parse . read_all(source: str, src_name: str = \"<string>\" ) -> list read_one(source: str, src_name: str = \"<string>\" ) Form types, each carrying a Span : Form Fields Surface List items, span (...) \u2014 a call or tag Vec items, span [...] \u2014 a tuple Map pairs, span {...} \u2014 a record Sym value, span bare symbol Kw value, span :keyword Num value, is_int, span number Str , Bool , Nil value, span literal @dataclass(frozen= True ) class Span: src: str; start: int; end: int; line: int; col: int Span is a half-open [start, end) with a 1-based line and 0-based column, and .merge() / .render() \u2014 the latter prints the source line with a caret run. Contract. Commas are whitespace. ; starts a comment. Duplicate map keys and odd-length maps are read errors ( ReadError ). Why it is hand-written. A stock EDN reader gives you no spans, and spans are the whole basis of the diagnostics below. This is the single sharpest argument for the Racket port: syntax/parse provides exactly this for free. macro-expand \u2014 macro.py # expand_all(forms: list, source: str) -> list Collects defmacro forms, then expands every remaining top-level form to a fixpoint , applicative order, depth-capped at MAX_EXPANSION_DEPTH = 64 . Builtins ( BUILTIN_MACROS ): Form Expands to (cornell-walls) / (cornell-walls :white :red :green) 5 wall quad maps (cornell-light) / (cornell-light :lamp) the emissive ceiling quad (cornell-box) walls + light, as one geometry vector (concat [a b] [c]) [a b c] \u2014 lets macro output compose with literals Box dimensions are module constants: CORNELL_W = 556.0 , CORNELL_H = 548.8 , CORNELL_D = 559.2 (millimetres; the room spans x\u2208[0,556], y\u2208[0,548.8], z\u2208[0,559.2] and opens toward \u2212z, where the camera sits). User macros: (defmacro name [params] body) . Contract \u2014 span inheritance. Synthesized forms inherit the call site's span (the Clojure default); substituted arguments keep their own . This is what makes an error inside a macro expansion point at the user's code rather than at the macro body. analyze \u2014 analyze.py # analyze(forms: list, source: str, src_name: str = \"<string>\" ) -> SceneAST Shape checking against closed key sets . An unknown key is a hard error ( UNKNOWN_KEY ), not a warning \u2014 with a did_you_mean suggestion. Schema # Section Keys Notes (scene {\u2026}) :name :settings :integrator :camera :materials :geometry :lights exactly one scene form per file; :integrator :camera :materials :geometry required :settings :seed :tonemap :exposure tonemap \u2208 _TONEMAPS :integrator :type :light-sampling :spp :max-bounces :ris :restir see the matrix in 00-overview :ris :M (required, positive int) :target :source :restir :temporal :spatial :spatial-count :spatial-radius :M-cap :bias :reproject defaults true true 3 30.0 20 \"mis\" false :camera :type :eye :look-at :up :fov :resolution :near :far :up defaults [0 1 0] , :near 0.1, :far 5000 :materials {:name \u2192 {:bsdf :albedo :emission}} a scalar spectrum broadcasts to RGB :geometry [{:id :prim :material :emitter \u2026}] plus prim-specific fields :lights [{:id :type :geometry :emission :two-sided}] :geometry binds to a geometry :id Closed vocabularies ( analyze.py:155-160 ): _TRANSPORTS = ( \"direct-lighting\" , \"global-illumination\" ) _STRATEGIES = ( \"uniform\" , \"ris\" , \"restir-di\" , \"restir-gi\" ) _TARGETS = ( \"unshadowed\" , \"unshadowed-cosine\" , \"full\" ) _SOURCES = ( \"uniform-light-area\" , \"power\" ) _BIASES = ( \"naive\" , \"mis\" , \"pairwise-mis\" ) _TONEMAPS = ( \"reinhard\" , \"aces\" , \"linear\" ) _PRIM_FIELDS gives the per-primitive required fields: :prim Fields :plane :point :normal :sphere :center :radius :quad :origin :u :v Common to all: :id :prim :material :emitter . elaborate \u2014 elaborate.py # elaborate(ast: SceneAST, source: str = None ) -> SemanticIR Five named jobs: Job What it does RESOLVE Names \u2192 dense integer ids, in source order DEFAULT Fill omitted keys NORMALIZE eye / look-at / up / fov \u2192 an orthonormal basis + tan_half_fov + aspect STITCH Light \u2194 prim mutual back-references: prims[gi].light_id = len(lights) EXPAND :light-sampling \u2192 the Strategy enum Contract \u2014 source-ordered indices. _build_symbol_tables assigns ids in source order, and its docstring says the ordering is load-bearing: buffer layout and RNG stream assignment are a deterministic function of the source text. Reordering :geometry in a scene file changes the image. This is intended \u2014 it is what makes a render reproducible from the text alone \u2014 but it means index assignment is not an implementation detail. Contract \u2014 camera basis. right = normalize(cross(forward, up)) , then up is re-orthogonalized. :up parallel to the view direction is DEGENERATE_CAMERA . Diagnostics \u2014 errors.py # @dataclass(frozen= True ) class ElabError: code: str; message: str; path: tuple = (); span: object = None ; hint: str = None def render( self , source: str = None ) -> str class Diagnostics: def err( self , code, message, path=(), span= None , hint= None ) -> None def check( self ) -> None # raises ElaborationFailed if any @property def codes( self ) did_you_mean(name: str, candidates) -> str Contract \u2014 accumulate, then raise once. Errors collect in a Diagnostics and surface together as a single ElaborationFailed , every one rendered with a caret. A scene with four mistakes reports four, not the first. Error catalog # 23 codes, grouped by elaboration phase ( errors.py::ERROR_CATALOG ): Phase Codes 0 \u2014 symbol tables DUP_GEO_ID DUP_LIGHT_ID DUP_MATERIAL_ID 1 \u2014 leaf validation ZERO_VECTOR DEGENERATE_QUAD NONPOS_RADIUS 2 \u2014 reference resolution UNKNOWN_MATERIAL UNKNOWN_GEOMETRY 3/4 \u2014 geometry + lights INFINITE_AREA_LIGHT NON_EMISSIVE_LIGHT_GEO EMITTER_WITHOUT_EMISSION LIGHT_GEO_REUSED 5 \u2014 integrator + camera MISSING_RIS_CONFIG MISSING_RESTIR_CONFIG DI_BOUNCE_CONSTRAINT GI_BOUNCE_CONSTRAINT GI_STRATEGY_UNSUPPORTED GI_BOUNCE_EXACTLY_TWO BAD_RESOLUTION DEGENERATE_CAMERA top level NO_LIGHTS NO_GEOMETRY Three carry real semantics worth knowing: INFINITE_AREA_LIGHT \u2014 an area light bound to a :plane has infinite area, so its pdf is zero everywhere. Caught at elaboration rather than producing NaNs. LIGHT_GEO_REUSED \u2014 two lights on one geometry would make the emitter you can hit with a camera ray disagree with the one you sample for NEE. GI_BOUNCE_EXACTLY_TWO \u2014 a path reservoir holds one reconnection vertex, and the GI candidate dimensions deliberately alias the path tracer's per-vertex draws so that M=1 without reuse reproduces the uniform two-bounce estimator sample for sample. Deeper paths turn that alias into a silent correlation. See 02-core . SemanticIR \u2014 ir.py # Frozen, resolved, SoA-friendly. Everything downstream consumes this and nothing else. @dataclass(frozen= True ) class SemanticIR: name: str; seed: int; tonemap: str; exposure: float camera: CameraIR; integrator: IntegratorIR materials: tuple; prims: tuple; lights: tuple debug_material_names: tuple = (); debug_prim_names: tuple = (); debug_light_names: tuple = () Record Fields MaterialIR albedo, emission, is_emitter PrimIR kind, a, b, c, radius, normal, area, material_id, light_id \u2014 a tagged union flattened into fixed slots so it uploads to SoA fields AreaLightIR prim_id, emission, two_sided, area, inv_area CameraIR eye, forward, right, up, tan_half_fov, aspect, width, height, near, far + .n_pixels IntegratorIR strategy, spp, max_bounces, ris_M, ris_target, ris_source, temporal, spatial, spatial_count, spatial_radius, m_cap, bias_mode, reproject Enums, all IntEnum so they cross into Taichi as plain ints: PrimKind: PLANE= 0 SPHERE= 1 QUAD= 2 Strategy: UNIFORM= 0 RIS= 1 RESTIR_DI= 2 RESTIR_GI= 3 TargetMode: UNSHADOWED= 0 UNSHADOWED_COSINE= 1 FULL= 2 SourcePdf: UNIFORM_LIGHT_AREA= 0 POWER= 1 BiasMode: NAIVE= 0 MIS= 1 PAIRWISE_MIS= 2 Immutable edits # The only supported way to vary a scene. Used by the CLI's _load funnel, the shell, and the whole validation harness: with_camera(cam) with_exposure(ev) wit"},{"u":"reference/02-core.html","t":"pt/core/ \u2014 the pure reference","q":"Reference","h":"Types, the RNG dimension layout, geometry, the reuse algebra, the integrators. Reference pt/core/ \u2014 the pure reference Types, the RNG dimension layout, geometry, the reuse algebra, the integrators. Source: docs/architecture/reference/02-core.md information-oriented Genuinely pure: no I/O, no globals, no mutation of inputs, f64 throughout . This is the denotational spec. The Taichi kernels are f32 and must agree with it per sample, so a disagreement points at structure , not float noise. Module Lines Role types.py 126 frozen value types, mirroring kernels/runtime.py field for field vecmath.py 97 f64 vector helpers on plain tuples rng.py 191 the counter-based RNG and its dimension contract geometry.py 262 analytic intersection + the two epsilons camera.py 144 ray generation, projection, reprojection lights.py 123 area-light sampling; pdfs stay in area measure bsdf.py 58 Lambertian only, behind a stable interface shading.py 129 the direct-lighting estimator, stated once reservoir.py 372 the reuse algebra and ReuseDomain reconnect.py 282 the GI shift mapping and the only real Jacobian integrators/ 1,015 five integrators render.py 246 the reference frame loop pathtree.py + pathtree_types.py 263 on-demand per-pixel path reconstruction For the mathematics rather than the API, read semantics.md . Value types \u2014 types.py # Ray(origin, dir, pixel=- 1 , sample= 0 ) Hit(valid= False , t=inf, p, n, wo, material_id=- 1 , prim_id=- 1 , light_id=- 1 , pixel=- 1 ) LightSample(p, wi, dist, radiance, pdf_area, pdf_solid_angle, light_id, cos_light= 0.0 , valid= True ) PathSample(p, n, radiance, pdf_solid_angle, prim_id=- 1 , valid= True ) Projected(sx, sy, depth, visible) PathNode(kind, payload={}, children=()) # .walk() .find(kind) .with_children(\u2026) All frozen dataclasses. LightSample and PathSample are the two reservoir payloads, and the difference that matters is which measure their density is in : pdf_area for a light point, pdf_solid_angle for a path suffix. That single fact is why DI needs no Jacobian and GI does. RNG \u2014 rng.py # Every stochastic decision is a pure hash. There is no stream state anywhere. pcg(v: int) -> int hash4(pixel, sample, bounce, dim, seed= 0 ) -> int # argument order is part of the contract uniform_from(h: int) -> float # (h >> 8) * 2^-24 rand1(pixel, sample, bounce, dim, seed= 0 ) -> float rand2(pixel, sample, bounce, dim, seed= 0 ) -> (float, float) Mixing constants ( _PCG_MUL , _PCG_INC , _PCG_XSH , _K_PIXEL , _K_SAMPLE , _K_BOUNCE , _K_SEED , _INV_2_24 ) are duplicated verbatim in kernels/_common.py . tests/test_kernels.py::TestRngParity pins the pair bit for bit. The bounce axis \u2014 one stream per path vertex # BOUNCE_CAMERA = 0 # primary-ray generation BOUNCE_SHADE = 1 # direct lighting / initial RIS candidates, at the primary hit BOUNCE_TEMPORAL = 2 # ReSTIR temporal reuse BOUNCE_SPATIAL = 3 # ReSTIR spatial reuse BOUNCE_BSDF = 4 # path extension, all vertices BOUNCE_SHADE_INDIRECT = 5 # NEE at path vertex 1; vertex k uses 5 + (k - 1) shade_bounce(depth) -> BOUNCE_SHADE if depth == 0 else BOUNCE_SHADE_INDIRECT + depth - 1 Contract \u2014 vertex 0 keeps BOUNCE_SHADE at any depth. A two-bounce render draws exactly the numbers a one-bounce render draws at its first vertex. This is what makes L(2 bounces) \u2212 L(1 bounce) the indirect term per sample , exactly, rather than statistically \u2014 the basis of the entire GI validation path ( 06-validation ). Deeper vertices get their own bounce id rather than an offset inside BOUNCE_SHADE , because the candidate block there is M \u00b7 CAND_STRIDE wide with M a runtime parameter \u2014 any fixed offset would collide for some M , invisibly. The dim axis # Bounce Stride Slots Accessor 0 camera \u2014 DIM_JITTER = 0 (+1 for the second axis) \u2014 1 shade CAND_STRIDE = 4 CAND_PICK=0 CAND_U=1 CAND_V=2 CAND_ACCEPT=3 cand_dim(i, which) 2 temporal \u2014 DIM_TEMPORAL_ACCEPT = 0 \u2014 3 spatial TAP_STRIDE = 4 TAP_ANGLE=0 TAP_RADIUS=1 TAP_ACCEPT=2 tap_dim(k, which) 4 bsdf BSDF_STRIDE = 2 BSDF_U=0 BSDF_V=1 bsdf_dim(depth, which) 4 bsdf (GI ReSTIR) GI_CAND_STRIDE = 3 GI_CAND_U=0 GI_CAND_V=1 GI_CAND_ACCEPT=2 gi_cand_dim(i, which) Contract \u2014 the GI alias. GI candidates share bounce 4 with path extension, aligned so candidate 0's (u, v) are dimensions 0 and 1 \u2014 exactly bsdf_dim(0, \u00b7) . That is what lets ReSTIR GI at M=1 with reuse off be compared to the two-bounce uniform path tracer per sample \u2014 they draw the same numbers. The outputs are not bit-identical: the bounce ray leaves from x1 + eps * n , so reuse re-derives a direction differing from the sampled one by about eps / d . See core/integrators/gi_restir.py . The price: candidate i > 0 overlaps vertex i 's BSDF dimensions, so a path reservoir cannot coexist with a >2-bounce path. Enforced in elaborate.py as GI_BOUNCE_EXACTLY_TWO . Trap. Adding a draw means allocating a dimension in both core/rng.py and kernels/_common.py , or two \"independent\" draws silently become the same number \u2014 which looks exactly like plausible noise. See 08-extending . Geometry and the two epsilons \u2014 geometry.py # intersect_plane / intersect_sphere / intersect_quad / intersect_prim intersect_scene(scene, ray) -> Hit # closest hit, linear scan intersect_scene_any(scene, o, d, max_dist) -> bool # any-hit in [EPS, max_dist) prim_normal_at(prim, p) shadow_epsilon(dist) -> float # SHADOW_EPS + dist * SHADOW_REL scatter_epsilon(dist) -> float # SCATTER_EPS + dist * SCATTER_REL EPS = 1e-4 # minimum accepted intersection distance SHADOW_EPS = 1e-3 SHADOW_REL = 1e-4 SCATTER_EPS = 1e-4 SCATTER_REL = 1e-5 Contract \u2014 the two epsilons must not share a scale, and neither may cancel. Two findings live here, both in ARCHITECTURE.md : \"A shadow-ray epsilon that cancels itself\" \u2014 offsetting the origin forward by eps and shortening the far end by eps makes the two cancel, putting the light's own surface exactly on the t < limit boundary, decided by rounding, which f64 and f32 resolve differently. It caused 31% of pixels to disagree reference-vs-kernel; shortening by twice the offset took that to 0.2%. \"A bounce ray's epsilon and a shadow ray's solve different problems\" \u2014 a shadow ray's margin scales by its own length; a bounce ray has no far endpoint yet, so reusing the shadow formula scales it by the primary ray's length (~1300 units in the Cornell box), putting the origin 0.13 units off the surface. Worst-sample identity error: 169% . SCATTER_REL = 1e-5 was measured, not argued. Lights and shading # # lights.py \u2014 pdfs stay in AREA measure, never converted pick_light(scene, u) -> (light_id, pdf_pick) light_pdf_area(scene, light_id) -> float # (1/n_lights) * (1/area) sample_area_light(scene, light_id, x, u, v) -> LightSample light_sample_at(scene, light_id, world_p, x) -> LightSample # rebuild for an existing point cos_at_light(scene, light_id, p, wi) -> float # shading.py \u2014 the direct-lighting estimator, stated once eval_contribution(scene, hit, ls) -> (rgb, cos_shade, cos_light, G) # unshadowed f * Le * G target_phat(scene, hit, ls, mode=UNSHADOWED_COSINE) -> float occluded(scene, hit, ls) -> bool shade_from_sample(scene, hit, ls, W) # f * Le * G * W emitted_radiance(scene, hit) Contract \u2014 light_sample_at is exact. Re-deriving wi and dist from a stored world-space point loses nothing, which is precisely why DI_DOMAIN pairs phat_at with a Jacobian that is identically 1. Target modes # Mode p\u0302 UNSHADOWED Le \u00b7 cos_light / d\u00b2 \u2014 no BRDF, no receiver cosine UNSHADOWED_COSINE full f \u00b7 Le \u00b7 G , unshadowed ( the default ) FULL includes visibility \u2014 one ray per candidate; reserved p\u0302 is unshadowed by default because tracing a shadow ray per candidate would defeat the point of resampling. Resample against the cheap target, then trace exactly one shadow ray for the survivor. The reuse algebra \u2014 reservoir.py # The module the project's subtlest bugs have all lived in. Full treatment in semantics.md ; this is the API. @dataclass(frozen= True ) class Reservoir: y: LightSample = None # the surviving sample; None when empty w_sum: float = 0.0 # running sum of resampling weights M: float = 0.0 # confidence \u2014 float, so capping can be fractional W: float = 0.0 # contribution weight the shading pass multiplies by @property valid -> bool # y is not None and W > 0 @dataclass(frozen= True ) class Stream: res: Reservoir; hit: Hit; kind: str = \"canonical\" ; pixel: int = - 1 @dataclass(frozen= True ) class ReuseDomain: name: str phat: Callable # (scene, hit, y, target_mode) -> float jacobian: Callable = unit_jacobian # (scene, y, from_hit, to_hit) -> |d(to)/d(from)| empty() -> Reservoir update(r, cand, w, u) -> Reservoir # = absorb(r, cand, w, 1.0, u) absorb(r, y, w, m_add, u) -> Reservoir # Chao's weighted reservoir sampling finalize_ris(r, phat_y) -> Reservoir # W = w_sum / (M \u00b7 p\u0302(y)) finalize_mis(r, phat_y) -> Reservoir # W = w_sum / p\u0302(y) phat_at(scene, hit, y, mode) -> float # the DI target hook unit_jacobian(scene, y, from_hit, to_hit) -> float # constantly 1.0 mis_weights_at(scene, streams, y, target_mode, bias_mode, domain=DI_DOMAIN, "},{"u":"reference/03-graph.html","t":"pt/graph/ \u2014 the render graph","q":"Reference","h":"Resources, passes, the five compile stages, the execution plan. Reference pt/graph/ \u2014 the render graph Resources, passes, the five compile stages, the execution plan. Source: docs/architecture/reference/03-graph.md information-oriented Frostbite-style: declare \u2192 compile \u2192 execute , as three separate objects. assemble_di_graph(scene) -> RawGraph declare: no memory exists yet compile_graph(raw, ...) -> ExecutionPlan order \u00b7 cull \u00b7 lifetimes \u00b7 alias \u00b7 guard plan.run(ctx, only=...) execute: walk a schedule, no reasoning Module Lines Role resource.py 74 ResKind , ResourceDesc , ResourceHandle (SSA) pass_.py 55 PassDecl and DIRTY_DOMAINS \u2014 the whole pass model builder.py 263 GraphBuilder , RawGraph , assemble_di_graph compile.py 290 CompileContext , compile_graph , the five stages plan.py 155 ExecutionPlan , PlanStep , Lifetime , dirty propagation The whole layer is ~860 lines and has no Taichi import on the reasoning path \u2014 field allocation is injected, which is why tests/test_graph.py exercises ordering, culling, lifetimes, aliasing and the boundary contract with a FakeField stub and no GPU. Resources \u2014 resource.py # class ResKind(Enum): RAY_BUF, HIT_BUF, RADIANCE, MOMENT, RESERVOIR, PATH_RESERVOIR, AOV, FRAMEBUFFER @dataclass(frozen= True ) class ResourceDesc: kind: ResKind; shape: tuple; dtype: str = \"f32\" ; transient: bool = True @property def key( self ): # aliasing bucket \u2014 only exact matches may share memory return ( self .kind, tuple( self .shape), self .dtype, self .transient) @dataclass(frozen= True ) class ResourceHandle: id: int; name: str; desc: ResourceDesc; version: int = 0 def written( self ) -> ResourceHandle: # a write yields a NEW SSA version return replace( self , version= self .version + 1 ) Contract \u2014 the transient/persistent split is load-bearing. Persistent resources ( reservoir_prev , reservoir_temporal , reservoir_curr , hits_prev , radiance_accum , moment2 ) survive across frames and are never aliased . The distinction recurs consistently in four places, and all four must agree: Where Behaviour for persistent resources _resolve_producers may be read at version == 0 with no producer _lifetimes Lifetime(-1, n) \u2014 live forever _assign_slots never share a slot default_keep_alive never culled That first row is what makes ReSTIR temporal reuse legal at all : reading last frame's reservoir is an unproduced read, and the exemption is stated precisely enough that nothing else slips through it. zero_persistent() is the only way to clear them. Note on aliasing. The key bucket requires an exact descriptor match, which is deliberately conservative \u2014 a smarter allocator would alias compatible-but-unequal descriptors. On the real scenes no aliasing actually fires : every transient is either an AOV or the framebuffer, both kept alive, and hits and primary_rays sit in different buckets. The mechanism is exercised only by tests/test_graph.py::TestAliasing::test_aliasing_happens_when_lifetimes_are_disjoint , which builds a synthetic 4-pass graph. Passes \u2014 pass_.py # The entire pass model is 55 lines: DIRTY_DOMAINS = frozenset({ \"scene\" , \"camera\" , \"spp\" , \"exposure\" , \"aov\" }) @dataclass(frozen= True ) class PassDecl: name: str reads: tuple = () writes: tuple = () params: dict = field(default_factory=dict) build: Callable = None # (CompileContext) -> ExecuteFn invalidated_by: frozenset = frozenset() cost_hint: float = 0.0 def __post_init__( self ): # a typo'd dirty domain is a compile error unknown = set( self .invalidated_by) - DIRTY_DOMAINS if unknown: raise ValueError(...) Contract \u2014 the referential-transparency boundary. A pass may only touch resources it declared; its declared outputs are a deterministic function of its declared inputs, params and seed. The contract lives here , not inside the kernels, which mutate fields freely. Declaring \u2014 builder.py # class GraphBuilder: def create( self , name, desc) -> ResourceHandle # id = len(self._resources) def import_persistent( self , name, desc) -> ResourceHandle # rejects transient descs def add_pass( self , decl) -> PassDecl def build( self ) -> RawGraph class RawGraph: resources; passes; scene def resource_by_id(rid); def resource_by_name(name) assemble_di_graph(scene, kernels= None ) -> RawGraph assemble_di_graph is the single place the Uniform \u2192 RIS \u2192 ReSTIR-DI \u2192 ReSTIR-GI progression is expressed. It validates depth/strategy pairings at declare time, creates resources, then branches only on the shade node. DI and GI share the ReSTIR branch verbatim, differing in three values: gi = strat == int(Strategy.RESTIR_GI) prefix = \"ReSTIR.GI.\" if gi else \"ReSTIR.\" resv_desc = ResourceDesc(ResKind.PATH_RESERVOIR if gi else ResKind.RESERVOIR, (N,), \"struct\" , transient= False ) ReSTIR GI is not a fourth graph . tests/test_graph.py::TestRestirGiGraph asserts pass-by-pass that it is the DI graph with a different payload. The declared ReSTIR schedule: Pass reads writes invalidated_by {prefix}InitialAndTemporal hits, reservoir_prev, hits_prev reservoir_temporal camera, scene {prefix}Spatial hits, reservoir_temporal reservoir_curr camera, scene {prefix}CopyToCurrent reservoir_temporal reservoir_curr (when spatial is off) {prefix}Resolve hits, reservoir_curr radiance_accum, moment2, aov_reservoir camera, scene, spp Contract \u2014 spatial reuse reads one buffer and writes another. Read-modify-write on reservoir_curr is a race the moment the pass runs in parallel: each pixel would see an arbitrary mixture of updated and stale neighbours. One extra buffer removes the hazard rather than documenting it. Note \u2014 the reuse passes omit spp . This is a true fact about the declaration and it is what progressive_steps() was designed around. It does not mean those passes are skipped when the sample index advances: dirtiness also flows along data edges, and hits is rewritten every sample by Intersect , so in practice dirty_steps({\"spp\"}) marks every step of a ReSTIR graph. A declaration is a lower bound on what re-runs, not the last word. Compiling \u2014 compile.py # compile_graph(raw, scene_device= None , make_field= None , keep_alive= None ) -> ExecutionPlan Five stages, in Frostbite's order: Stage Function Job A _resolve_producers (res_id, version) \u2192 producing pass . Double write = error; unproduced read = error except version == 0 and not desc.transient B _toposort Kahn's algorithm, ties broken by declaration index C _cull backward reachability from default_keep_alive = {\"framebuffer\"} \u222a AOVs \u222a persistents D _lifetimes \u2192 _assign_slots \u2192 _allocate_fields [first_write, last_read] , greedy interval colouring per desc.key , one physical field per slot E build loop a CompileContext per pass \u2192 decl.build(cctx) \u2192 PlanStep Contract \u2014 determinism of stage B. Ties broken by declaration index, not by hash order, because stable buffer assignment and RNG stream layout depend on it. Contract \u2014 the injection seam. make_field and kernels are both injected. This is what makes the graph testable without a GPU and is the seam a port would reuse. CompileContext # What a build_* closure sees \u2014 scoped to exactly one pass, so field capture and declaration checking happen at the same moment: class CompileContext: def __init__( self , scene, scene_device, fields_by_name, allowed, params, pass_name) def field_by_name( self , name): if name not in self ._allowed: raise GraphError( f \"pass {self._pass_name!r} touched undeclared resource {name!r} \u2014 \" f \"it declared {sorted(self._allowed)}. Add it to reads/writes, \" f \"or the graph cannot reason about lifetimes and dirtiness.\" ) return self ._fields[name] def has( self , name) -> bool allowed is {h.name for h in (decl.reads + decl.writes)} . Guarded by tests/test_graph.py::TestBoundaryContract . The plan \u2014 plan.py # @dataclass(frozen= True ) class Lifetime: first: int; last: int; def overlaps(other) -> bool @dataclass(frozen= True ) class PlanStep: name; index; pass_index; exec_fn; invalidated_by; reads; writes; params @dataclass(frozen= True ) class ExecutionPlan: steps; fields_by_id; fields_by_name; physical; resources; lifetimes; slot_of; culled; scene field_by_name(name) has_field(name) resource_by_name(name) step_by_name(name) data_edges() # (producer_step, consumer_step) pairs, derived from resource ids dirty_steps(changed) # seeded from invalidated_by \u2229 changed, then transitive closure progressive_steps() # == dirty_steps({\"spp\"}) run(ctx, only= None ) zero_persistent() describe() Three distinct meanings of \"run less work\" , easily confused: Mechanism When Scope stage-C culling compile time permanent \u2014 the pass is not in the plan dirty_steps(changed) per interaction which steps a UI change forces progressive_steps() steady state what a new sample needs dirty_steps is what the shell calls on an exposure or AOV change: only = engine.plan.dirty_steps({ \"exposure\" , \"aov\" }) engine.plan.run(engine.ctx, only=only) describe() output # pt describe cornell_restir_gi : ExecutionPlan: 6 steps, 13 resources -> 13 physical buffers schedule: 0: GeneratePrimaryRays reads[-] writes[primary_r"},{"u":"reference/04-kernels.html","t":"pt/kernels/ \u2014 the Taichi layer","q":"Reference","h":"The execute layer, the device structs, the constraints that fail unhelpfully. Reference pt/kernels/ \u2014 the Taichi layer The execute layer, the device structs, the constraints that fail unhelpfully. Source: docs/architecture/reference/04-kernels.md information-oriented The fast twin. Mutates fields freely; the contract lives at the pass boundary ( 03-graph ). Held to agreeing with pt/core/ per sample . Module Lines Role _common.py 620 every @ti.func leaf: RNG, intersection, ONB/Lambert, light sampling, targets, reconnection restir.py 502 build_restir_temporal , build_restir_spatial , build_reservoir_copy , build_restir_resolve restir_gi.py 507 build_restir_gi_temporal , build_restir_gi_spatial , build_restir_gi_resolve runtime.py 197 Taichi init, thread guards, field allocation, the device structs shade_di.py 127 build_shade_uniform , build_shade_ris shade_gi.py 100 build_shade_gi_uniform scene_device.py 108 SceneDevice \u2014 upload SemanticIR into read-only fields intersect.py / primary_rays.py / tonemap.py 203 the rest of the graph The build_* thunk protocol # Every kernel module exports factories with one shape: def build_< pass >(cctx: CompileContext) -> Callable[[RenderContext], None ] The factory captures the fields its pass declared, defines a @ti.kernel , and returns an execute(ctx) closure the plan calls each frame. Contract \u2014 nothing shell-mutable may be a compile-time capture. Camera, sample index, seed, bias mode, spatial radius and the reproject flag are kernel arguments , never captured. Anything captured requires a full Engine.rebuild() to change; anything passed is free. This is exactly why set_seed costs nothing and with_resolution costs ~5s. def build_restir_temporal(cctx): sd = cctx.scene_device hits = cctx.field_by_name( \"hits\" ) # captured: declared resources M = int(cctx.params.get( \"M\" , 8 )) # captured: compile-time params @ti.kernel def run(sample: ti.i32, seed: ti.i32, temporal_on: ti.i32, bias_mode: ti.i32, reproject_on: ti.i32, p_eye: ti.math.vec3, ...): # arguments: per frame ... Device structs \u2014 runtime.py # Reservoir = ti.types.struct( y_p=ti.math.vec3, y_light=ti.i32, valid=ti.i32, w_sum=ti.f32, M=ti.f32, W=ti.f32) # M is float so capping can be fractional PathReservoir = ti.types.struct( y_p=ti.math.vec3, # x2, the reconnection vertex y_n=ti.math.vec3, # normal at x2, for the geometry term after a shift y_L=ti.math.vec3, # L(x2 -> x1), emission at x2 excluded y_pdf=ti.f32, # solid-angle pdf as drawn at x1 y_prim=ti.i32, valid=ti.i32, w_sum=ti.f32, M=ti.f32, W=ti.f32) Contract \u2014 these mirror core/types.py field for field , so the two implementations can be diffed per field rather than only per pixel. Two structs rather than one payload union, deliberately: Taichi structs are fixed-layout, so a union would cost the DI path the space, and the \"the Jacobian is 1 because we store a world-space point\" comments scattered through the DI code would become only conditionally true. The reuse algebra is not duplicated \u2014 only the payload. See core/reservoir.ReuseDomain . Runtime services # init_runtime(prefer_gpu= False , debug= False , force= False ) # idempotent unless force owner_thread() # the thread that called init_runtime check_allocation_thread(what= \"field\" ) -> None # fail early and legibly is_initialized() -> bool; current_arch() place(builder, field, shape) make_field(desc, builder= None ) # ResourceDesc -> field; the only allocator the graph calls Taichi constraints worth knowing # Four threading rules and two syntax rules that fail unhelpfully. All are enforced or worked around in runtime.py and engine.py . Constraint What a violation looks like ti.init() binds the LLVM context to the calling thread; only that thread may allocate fields . Launching kernels and to_numpy() elsewhere is fine. Assertion failure: std::this_thread::get_id() == main_thread_id_ from llvm_context.cpp \u2014 no mention of threads owning fields Field materialization is deferred until first access, so allocating on the right thread is not enough \u2014 it must be touched there. The same assertion, but at first render rather than construction, so building appears to have succeeded The default root FieldsBuilder is finalized on first materialization; no field can be created afterwards. TaichiRuntimeError: FieldsBuilder finalized when a second engine is built Writing to a field forces materialization, illegal while a caller-owned FieldsBuilder is open. Field builder \u2026 is not finalized , raised from inside an innocuous data upload Any kernel launch validates that no builder is open \u2014 including one launched by another thread . A rebuild on the main thread kills the render worker mid-frame with a bare TaichiRuntimeError Two more, unrelated to threading and both liable to bite while editing: from __future__ import annotations breaks Taichi. PEP 563 stringifies annotations, and Taichi reads @ti.kernel / @ti.func argument annotations as live type objects. No module under pt/kernels/ may use it. Every one carries a note. ti.template() does not thread a scalar through a @ti.func . It passes fields by reference, not local scalars, so a stateful RNG whose counter is \"advanced\" by a callee silently returns the same number every draw \u2014 and the output still looks like plausible noise. This is why the RNG is stateless and indexed. How the inlined algebra corresponds # The kernels are combine_streams with N fully expanded. In build_restir_temporal , the two-stream merge evaluates p\u0302 for both samples at both shading points ( ph_t_q , ph_c_q , ph_t_t , ph_c_t ), which is mis_weights_at 's density(i) loop unrolled: if bias_mode == _NAIVE: w_prev = ph_t_q * pr.W * m_prev # unnormalized 1/M form else : den_t = m_c * ph_t_q + m_prev * ph_t_t w_prev = (m_prev * ph_t_t / den_t) * ph_t_q * pr.W den_c = m_c * ph_c_q + m_prev * ph_c_t out.w_sum = out.w_sum * ph_c_q / den_c # canonical re-weighted too mis_merged = 1 Contract \u2014 finalize is selected by an explicit flag, not inferred. mis_merged exists because \"no history was found\" and \"history was found and weighted\" are different states that can leave M looking the same: if mis_merged == 1 : out.W = out.w_sum / ph # finalize_mis else : out.W = out.w_sum / (out.M * ph) # finalize_ris Contract \u2014 pairwise-mis is not a third branch in the temporal kernel. With exactly two streams it reduces to the balance heuristic term for term, so it shares the mis path. Measured bit-identical. The GI spatial kernel is the fullest mirror, factoring the MIS denominator into a @ti.func whose body is mis_weights_at 's density summed: @ti.func def mis_denominator(idx, sample, seed, y_p, y_n, y_L, y_hit, canon_M, rad) -> ti.f32: \"\"\"`sum_j M_j * p_hat_j(y) * J(y's domain -> j)`.\"\"\" Contract \u2014 the two Jacobian directions are different calls. Inside the denominator, J(y_hit \u2192 stream i) ; at the absorb site, J(stream i \u2192 q) . The DI kernels contain no Jacobian factor at all \u2014 the \u00d7 1 is elided, which is the concrete meaning of DI_DOMAIN.jacobian = unit_jacobian . Contract \u2014 usable() tests M > 0 , not valid . This is the kernel spelling of combine_streams 's empty-stream rule; has_sample() is a separate predicate consulted only at the absorb site. See 07-invariants . Previous pt/graph/ \u2014 the render graph Next Engine, shell and CLI"},{"u":"reference/05-runtime.html","t":"Engine, shell and CLI","q":"Reference","h":"Lifecycle, threading, the command surface. Reference Engine, shell and CLI Lifecycle, threading, the command surface. Source: docs/architecture/reference/05-runtime.md information-oriented Everything that renders \u2014 CLI, validation harness, tests, the GUI worker \u2014 goes through Engine . Engine \u2014 pt/engine.py (365 lines) # @dataclass class RenderContext: \"\"\"Per-frame state handed to every pass thunk.\"\"\" scene: SemanticIR; scene_device: object seed: int = 0 ; frame: int = 0 ; spp_index: int = 0 exposure: float = 0.0 ; aov: str = \"beauty\" bias_mode: int = int(BiasMode.MIS) spatial_radius: float = 30.0 ; reproject: bool = False prev_camera: object = None # None == \"no usable history\" Lifecycle # Engine(scene, prefer_gpu= False , init= True ) _build() is where the Taichi ownership discipline lives, and the order is forced: self ._fb = ti.FieldsBuilder() # per-engine builder, NOT the default root self .scene_device = SceneDevice( self .scene, builder= self ._fb) self .plan = compile_graph(assemble_di_graph( self .scene), self .scene_device, make_field= lambda d: make_field(d, self ._fb)) self ._snode_tree = self ._fb.finalize() self .scene_device.upload() # only after finalize self ._advance = self ._make_advance() self ._materialize() # touch every field on this thread self .reset() Contract \u2014 building an Engine costs ~5s. Taichi recompiles every kernel per engine, because each owns its own FieldsBuilder . Never construct engines in a loop ; use the setters below. The validation harness threads an engine= parameter through for exactly this reason. Contract \u2014 close() releases the SNode tree. Taichi has a finite number, so a GUI strategy switcher would exhaust them otherwise. rebuild(scene= None ) # close() + _build(); needed only for topology changes close() Change Cost resolution, strategy, max_bounces , spatial on/off rebuild() \u2014 ~5s seed, camera, exposure, AOV, bias mode, reproject a setter \u2014 free Setters \u2014 no recompile, all chainable # set_camera(cam) set_exposure(ev) set_aov(name) set_seed(seed) set_bias_mode(mode) set_reproject(on) set_bias_mode accepts \"naive\" | \"mis\" | \"pairwise-mis\" or a BiasMode . Each setter rewrites self.scene through the with_* IR edits and re-points ctx.scene , so the two never diverge. The history roll \u2014 _make_advance # Returns a @ti.kernel , or None when the plan has no reservoirs. Python cannot swap field references because the pass closures captured them at build time, so the copy happens on device: gi = int( self .scene.integrator.strategy) == int(Strategy.RESTIR_GI) src = \"reservoir_temporal\" if gi else \"reservoir_curr\" # ! GI rolls PRE-spatial @ti.kernel def advance(): for i in range(n): prev[i] = curr[i] hits_prev[i] = hits[i] This is the device twin of core/render._render_restir 's rollover and must not drift from it . The reasoning, and the numbers, are in 02-core . Two distinct resets # reset() # zero_persistent(): accumulator AND reservoirs reset_accumulation(restart_sequence= True ) # only radiance_accum and moment2 Contract \u2014 reset_accumulation is the methodological core. ReSTIR's claim is about a single warm frame , and consecutive frames are correlated through the reservoirs, so comparing accumulated means understates ReSTIR \u2014 it can even lose to plain RIS. Variance must be measured per frame. Contract \u2014 clearing the accumulator must not rewind the RNG. restart_sequence=False exists for exactly one case: a reprojecting camera drag, where the image is cleared every frame. Rewinding the sample counter would hand every drag frame the same sub-pixel jitter and \u2014 because sample_light_point(li, u, v) is deterministic \u2014 literally the same M points on the same M lights, so the canonical stream stops contributing new information the instant the drag begins. Hence two counters that coincide everywhere except that path: Property Meaning Engine.spp samples in the accumulator ctx.spp_index the RNG sample counter Cooking and outputs # render_sample(only= None ) # plan.run(ctx, only) -> _advance() -> record prev_camera -> bump render_sample_radiance() # -> (H,W,3) for THAT sample alone, by differencing render(spp, on_progress= None ) framebuffer() # (H,W,3) in [0,1], tonemapped radiance() # (H,W,3) linear mean radiance \u2014 what validation compares variance() # (H,W) variance OF THE MEAN, i.e. Var/N aov_image(name); reservoirs(which= \"curr\" ); describe() ctx.prev_camera records the camera the frame was actually rendered with, captured before the run. The shell \u2014 pt/shell/ # Module Lines Role app.py 459 DearPyGui application, layout, rebuild orchestration render_worker.py 390 the render thread and its command queue camera_controller.py 144 free-fly camera capture.py 107 write_png , write_npy , write_image , read_image , dump_artifacts viewports/ 695 debug3d (264), pathtree_inspector (173), raster (159), ris_panel (84) Contract \u2014 the thread split. The main thread owns ti.init and all field allocation . The worker only runs an already-compiled plan. App._rebuild(scene) constructs the new Engine(scene, init=False) on the main thread, hands it to the worker, and only then calls old.close() . Because an open FieldsBuilder invalidates kernel launches anywhere , a rebuild must park the worker \u2014 block until it confirms it is idle \u2014 before allocation begins. Asking it to freeze is not enough; the rebuild has to know it has stopped. Command enum: CAMERA ENGINE EXPOSURE AOV BIAS REPROJECT SPP_CAP RESTART FREEZE CAPTURE SNAPSHOT STOP . Camera commands are coalesced. Camera moves branch on reprojection \u2014 reset_accumulation(restart_sequence=False) when ctx.reproject , else a full reset() . The CLI \u2014 pt/cli.py (637 lines) # argparse with set_defaults(func=cmd_*) . A shared common(p, spp_default=None) installs the scene-override flags, and one funnel applies them: _load(args) # entirely through SemanticIR's immutable edits so every subcommand has identical override semantics and nothing mutates a scene in place. Subcommand Shape render _load \u2192 Engine \u2192 render(spp) \u2192 write_image ; -o default out/render.png gui App(...).start() ; --frames N renders N UI frames then exits (headless verification) describe print(Engine(_load(args)).describe()) edn print(to_edn(_load(args))) \u2014 round-trip and diff the lowering dump render, then dump_artifacts(eng.plan, dir) \u2014 works off the plan's resource table, no pass cooperation flip the only subcommand not using common ; takes two image paths, auto-selects HDR/LDR validate ~350 lines, six sections; defaults many_lights , --spp 400 , --ref-spp 2000 Common flags: --res W H , --strategy , --M , --bounces , --bias , --seed , --gpu , --spp . Previous pt/kernels/ \u2014 the Taichi layer Next validation/ \u2014 the harness"},{"u":"reference/06-validation.html","t":"validation/ \u2014 the harness","q":"Reference","h":"The chain of trust and every statistic hanging off it. Reference validation/ \u2014 the harness The chain of trust and every statistic hanging off it. Source: docs/architecture/reference/06-validation.md information-oriented A top-level package alongside pt , not under it, because it is a peer of the renderer rather than part of it. ~2,204 lines. The framing. Low variance is not correctness: a biased ReSTIR produces a clean, beautiful, wrong image. Changes to the estimator are argued with numbers, never with renders. The chain of trust # closed form \u2500\u2500\u25ba uniform MC (1 bounce) \u2500\u2500\u25ba RIS \u2500\u2500\u25ba ReSTIR DI \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u25ba uniform MC (2 bounce) \u2500\u2500\u25ba ReSTIR GI \u2502 \u25b2 \u2514\u2500\u2500 quadrature, different parameterization \u2500\u2500\u2518 Each link is checked against the one to its left. di_uniform is the root because it is the simplest, most obviously unbiased estimator here, and it is validated against a closed form. Why the per-sample twin diff is not enough. It is the strongest test in the project and it has a blind spot worth naming: the two implementations are supposed to compute the same thing, so a wrong pdf, a double-converted Jacobian or a doubled cosine makes them agree exactly as well as being right does. Closed form covers that gap for DI. One bounce further there is no closed form, so the indirect term is anchored against a deterministic quadrature in a different parameterization , which never forms a pdf at all and so cannot share the mistake. Measured: MC and quadrature agree to 1.6% or better at three shading points, one on a sphere. Modules # Module Lines Role analytic.py 208 closed-form anchors \u2014 re-implements its own vector math anchor.py 164 applies the closed form to pixels indirect.py 197 deterministic quadrature, the GI anchor reference_cache.py 202 converged uniform-MC references, content-hash keyed bias.py 179 the bias detector convergence.py 242 convergence curves, equal-time, per-frame variance temporal.py 303 camera-path harness for reprojection gi.py 271 the differenced indirect term flip.py 581 perceptual difference, anchored against NVlabs analytic.py deliberately duplicates _sub/_add/_dot/_cross/_len/_norm/_scale so it cannot share a bug with pt.core.vecmath . The same move, one level up, is what flip.py 's anchoring does. References \u2014 reference_cache.py # CACHE_VERSION = 1 cache_dir() -> str # $PT_REF_CACHE or .ref_cache get_converged_reference(scene, spp= 4096 , seed= None , force= False , on_progress= None ) cached_reference(scene, seed= 0 ) # never renders; for the UI thread reference_noise_floor(scene, spp= 4096 , seed_a= 0 , seed_b= 99991 ) -> float estimator_noise(scene, spp, seed_a= 0 , seed_b= 99991 ) -> float # == 2 * variance clear_cache() -> int; cache_report() Contract \u2014 strategy is deliberately absent from the fingerprint. _fingerprint hashes the camera basis, prims, materials, lights, seed, spp, max_bounces and \"estimator\": \"uniform-mc-converged\" . Asking for \"the reference for a ReSTIR scene\" therefore structurally returns the uniform-MC image \u2014 you cannot validate an estimator against itself. max_bounces is present because a two-bounce scene's reference is two-bounce uniform MC. Contract \u2014 the noise floor is not optional. reference_noise_floor is the MSE between two independent references. Without it a detector either cries wolf on the reference's own noise or compares against zero and flags everything. Bias below this is undetectable and must not be reported. The bias detector \u2014 bias.py # bias_report(converged_img, reference, ref_noise_floor, estimator_noise= 0.0 , alpha= 0.01 , margin= 2.0 , decorrelation_stride= 1 , min_sign_samples= 30 ) -> BiasReport Three tests, because each has a blind spot alone: Test Statistic Blind spot it covers / has Excess over expected noise expected = (estimator_noise + ref_noise_floor)/2 ; biased if excess > margin\u00b7expected The only one that stays valid under correlation, since E[MSE] is an average Per-pixel sign test two-sided binomial on mean-luminance error, subsampled [::stride] Assumes independent pixels, which reuse violates Localization per_pixel_sq > floor + 4\u00b7std \u2192 hotspot mask Diagnostic only; reuse bias concentrates at discontinuities Contract \u2014 E[MSE(X, Y)] = vx + vy + bias\u00b2 . Comparing against the reference's noise floor alone charges the estimator's own variance to bias: uniform MC at 400 spp against a uniform reference at 4000 spp reports as badly biased, though it is the reference's estimator. Contract \u2014 the sign test needs decorrelation and reports its own power. ReSTIR's neighbouring pixels literally share samples, so a per-pixel binomial test is wildly over-confident and flags every configuration including provably unbiased ones. Subsampling beyond the reuse radius fixes it, but too large a stride leaves no power \u2014 a 32px radius leaves 9 usable pixels at 96\u00d796 \u2014 so sign_test_n and sign_test_usable are always reported, and the test is skipped below MIN_SIGN_SAMPLES = 30 . BiasReport is a dict with .biased and .summary() ; 17 keys including verdict , mse , expected_noise , excess_ratio , sign_test_{p,n,usable} , directional , biased_pixel_fraction , bias_hotspot_mask . Efficiency \u2014 convergence.py # mse(a, b); rmse(a, b) convergence_curve(scene, checkpoints, reference, engine= None , on_progress= None ) estimate_slope(curve, drop_floor_ratio= None ) slope_verdict(curve, noise_floor= 0.0 , flat_threshold=- 0.3 , tail_fraction= 0.5 , floor_margin= 2.0 ) equal_time_comparison(scenes: dict, reference, budget_seconds= 5.0 , warmup_samples= 1 ) frame_variance(scene, warmup= 0 , trials= 8 , pct= 90.0 , engine= None , ...) -> float Contract \u2014 slope_verdict fits the tail, not the whole curve. An unbiased estimator keeps falling near -1 ; a biased one flattens onto its bias\u00b2 plateau. It needs only the curve to have turned over, so it fires earlier than the excess test. Fitting the whole curve dilutes the evidence, because early checkpoints fall steeply either way. Contract \u2014 variance is measured per frame, at the 90th percentile. Which statistic you choose decides whether you see the effect at all: the mean per-pixel variance is firefly-dominated and moves under 5% across estimators; the median samples pixels that were never noisy, where RIS genuinely loses. At p90 \u2014 where perceptible noise lives \u2014 the ordering is clean: RIS 1.9\u00d7 better than uniform, ReSTIR 1.5\u00d7 better than RIS. The indirect term \u2014 gi.py # The most consequential module in the harness, because whole-image comparison cannot see GI defects at all. SHARED_DIRECT_TERM = (int(Strategy.UNIFORM), int(Strategy.RESTIR_GI)) require_shared_direct_term(scene) -> None one_bounce_image(scene, spp, seed= None ) -> np.ndarray render_indirect(scene, spp, seed= None , engine= None , warmup= 0 , on_progress= None ) converged_indirect_reference(scene, spp= 4096 , seed= 0 , on_progress= None ) indirect_noise_floor(scene, spp= 4096 , seed_a= 0 , seed_b= 99991 ) -> float indirect_estimator_noise(scene, spp, ...) -> float indirect_verdict(scene, spp, reference, noise_floor, total_reference= None , ...) -> dict indirect_convergence_curve(scene, checkpoints, reference, seed= None , engine= None ) indirect_fraction(scene, spp= 4096 , seed= 0 ) -> float energy_ratio(img, reference) -> float Contract \u2014 differencing is exact, not statistical, and only for two strategies. SHARED_DIRECT_TERM is (UNIFORM, RESTIR_GI) \u2014 the only strategies whose vertex-0 draws are uniform MC's. For those, vertex 0 keeps its RNG stream, so subtracting the one-bounce image cancels the direct term rather than averaging it away. require_shared_direct_term refuses anything else, and also refuses max_bounces < 1 . Do not roll the subtraction by hand. Why it is needed: two bounces are only ~13% brighter than one on the Cornell box, so a completely broken indirect term still lands inside the 2%-of-pixels tolerance a whole-image comparison can afford. Contract \u2014 warm-up is not optional for a reuse estimator. A ReSTIR run becomes the estimator under test over roughly m_cap frames, so an accumulation started at frame 0 averages in the path tracer it began as. Measured on cornell_restir_gi at 96px, warm-up 0/8/24/64/128/256 \u2192 mis 1.0037/0.9966/0.9919/0.9873/0.9872/0.9870. From cold it reads 1.004 \u2014 above truth, and indistinguishable from the uniform control at 1.0009 \u00b1 0.0004. The reading settles by 64, which is the --warmup default. render_indirect implements the discard with reset_accumulation() , which clears the image while keeping reservoirs. The control that keeps the discard honest is the no-reuse row measured with the same warm-up : both passes off at M=1 is the two-bounce path tracer sample for sample, and it must read 1.000 (measured 1.0004 \u00b1 0.0027). Contract \u2014 use the indirect noise floor, not the whole-image one. The direct term cancels stream-for-stream and takes its variance with it: 1.4e-06 vs 5.8e-03 on cornell_restir_gi at 48px. Scoring a differenced image against the whole-image floor would call every GI estimator clean. Contract \u2014 energy resolves what MSE cannot. On the"},{"u":"reference/07-invariants.html","t":"Invariants","q":"Reference","h":"Every cross-cutting contract, where it is enforced, and which test guards it. Reference Invariants Every cross-cutting contract, where it is enforced, and which test guards it. Source: docs/architecture/reference/07-invariants.md information-oriented Every cross-cutting contract, where it is enforced, and which test fails if it breaks. If you are changing the estimator, this is the checklist. The governing invariant # Every estimator lives twice, and the twins agree per sample. Enforced by tests/test_kernels.py::TestRestirGiKernels::test_agrees_with_the_reference_per_pixel and its DI siblings Tolerance REL_TOL = 5e-3 , MAX_DISAGREE_FRACTION = 0.02 , plus p99 < 1e-3 and means within 2e-3 Breaks when you change core/ or kernels/ without the other The DI/GI tests are parametrized over mis , naive and pairwise-mis \u2014 including naive precisely because it is the wrong estimator. The kernel and the reference must agree about what the wrong answer is, or a downstream bias measurement would be measuring a kernel bug. Three assertions of decreasing locality, the same shape in all three twin tests: rel = np.abs(ker - ref) / np.maximum(np.abs(ref), 1e-3 ) assert float((rel.max(axis= 2 ) > REL_TOL).mean()) < MAX_DISAGREE_FRACTION assert np.percentile(rel, 99 ) < 1e-3 assert ker.mean() == pytest.approx(ref.mean(), rel= 2e-3 ) RNG # Invariant Enforced Guarded by The hash is bit-identical between core/rng.py and kernels/_common.py constants duplicated verbatim TestRngParity ti.random() appears nowhere convention \u2014 (grep) Vertex 0 keeps BOUNCE_SHADE at any depth rng.shade_bounce TestMultiBounce , and every validation/gi.py result One dimension per draw; no two draws share (bounce, dim) the layout tables TestGiRestirIdentity ReSTIR GI at M=1 , reuse off \u2261 two-bounce uniform PT, per sample gi_cand_dim aliases bsdf_dim(0, \u00b7) TestGiRestirIdentity A path reservoir cannot coexist with >2 bounces elaborate.py \u2192 GI_BOUNCE_EXACTLY_TWO TestElaborate Trap. Adding a draw means allocating a dimension in both files, or two \"independent\" draws silently become the same number. It looks exactly like plausible noise. The reuse algebra # Invariant Enforced Guarded by \u03a3\u1d62 m\u1d62(y) = 1 for any y in the combined support, under all three rules mis_weights_at TestMisWeights (parametrized 3 modes \u00d7 4 stream indices) The estimator uses m\u1d62(y\u1d62) , which does not sum to one \u2014 and need not _mis_weights TestMisWeights DI_DOMAIN.jacobian \u2261 1 exactly unit_jacobian TestReuseDomain::test_di_jacobian_is_exactly_one A per-stream Jacobian preserves the partition of unity mis_weights_at TestReuseDomain::test_partition_of_unity_survives_a_per_stream_jacobian A wrong Jacobian moves the answer domain is a value, not a subclass TestReuseDomain::test_a_varying_jacobian_changes_the_mis_weights naive still pays the Jacobian \u2014 it is not a weighting choice combine_streams TestReuseDomain::test_naive_still_pays_the_jacobian J(a\u2192b)\u00b7J(b\u2192a) = 1 and the direction is right reconnection_jacobian TestReconnection::test_reciprocity + the hand-computed asymmetric case Trap \u2014 reciprocity does not pin a Jacobian's direction. J(a\u2192b)\u00b7J(b\u2192a) == 1 holds just as well if the whole ratio is inverted, and the identity case has the same blind spot. What closes it is arithmetic done by hand on an asymmetric configuration: a vertex twice as close must give J = 4 , not 1/4 . Both numbers are asserted, on both implementations, rather than one being derived from the other. Empty streams # Two separate halves, and missing either is expensive. Half Rule Cost of missing it Inside the merge combine_streams absorbs (None, w=0, m_add=s.res.M) reference W off from the kernel's by a clean factor of two Choosing streams the code deciding which neighbours become streams must not filter on having a sample the canonical stream counted twice; 25% bright at K=3 Guarded by TestEmptyStreamConfidence , including test_an_empty_spatial_neighbour_still_dilutes_the_canonical . Kernel side: usable() tests M > 0.0 , not .valid . Measured, indirect energy at K=1/3/5: dropping empties 1.086 / 1.201 / 1.284 ; counting their confidence 1.002 / 1.006 / 1.005 . Total image energy read a healthy 0.9953 throughout. And the rule inverts for the temporal pass. Spatial streams are admitted on M > 0 ; temporal streams on y is not None . A spatial neighbour is fresh this-frame candidates, so its confidence belongs in the denominator regardless; an empty history carries an M already spent. Treating them the same took the indirect term from 0.993 to 0.939 . The graph boundary # Invariant Enforced Guarded by A pass touches only declared resources CompileContext.field_by_name TestBoundaryContract No double writes to one (resource, version) _resolve_producers TestCompile Unproduced reads are errors, except version == 0 and not transient _resolve_producers TestCompile Toposort ties break by declaration index _toposort TestCompile Persistent resources are never aliased and never culled _assign_slots , default_keep_alive TestAliasing invalidated_by \u2286 DIRTY_DOMAINS PassDecl.__post_init__ TestDeclare Spatial reuse reads reservoir_temporal , writes reservoir_curr assemble_di_graph TestRestirGiGraph ReSTIR GI is the DI graph with a different payload assemble_di_graph TestRestirGiGraph (pass by pass) Trap \u2014 read-modify-write across a parallel pass is a race. Reading and writing reservoir_curr in place means each pixel sees an arbitrary mixture of updated and stale neighbours. Removed, not documented. Frame ordering and history # Invariant Enforced Notes Every pixel completes pass 1 before any starts pass 2 _render_restir ; graph buffer split a correctness requirement, not cosmetics Reservoirs and hits roll together _make_advance , _render_restir reservoir_prev[i] was built at hits_prev[i] GI rolls pre-spatial ; DI rolls post-spatial _make_advance , _render_restir the two must not drift -1 from reprojected_pixel means no history, never a clamped fallback _render_restir The temporal gate is plane distance, not eye distance accept_reprojection dolly 20% breaks the eye-distance test Measured for the roll policy on cornell_restir_gi , indirect energy at m_cap 1/2/5/20/60: History from post-spatial 0.985 0.981 0.973 0.955 0.939 pre-spatial 0.991 0.990 0.989 0.990 0.993 copies per sample (post) 1.35 1.48 1.83 3.24 6.27 E[M] is 46.5 against 46.8 at m_cap 20 \u2014 the fix holds every confidence fixed and moves only sample identity. Engine and threading # Invariant Enforced Only the init_runtime thread allocates fields check_allocation_thread Every field is touched on the owning thread Engine._materialize Each engine owns its own FieldsBuilder ; close() releases the SNode tree Engine._build / close A rebuild parks the worker before allocating App._rebuild Nothing shell-mutable is a compile-time capture the build_* convention Clearing the accumulator does not rewind the RNG reset_accumulation(restart_sequence=False) Engine.spp (accumulator) \u2260 ctx.spp_index (RNG) on the drag path Engine The DSL # Invariant Enforced Guarded by elaborate(read(to_edn(ir))) == ir writer.py parametrized round-trip over every scene Unknown keys are hard errors analyze._warn_unknown_keys TestAnalyze Indices are assigned in source order _build_symbol_tables TestLowering All errors are reported at once, not the first Diagnostics.check TestElaborate Macro output inherits the call site's span macro._mk TestMacros Measurement # These are not code invariants but protocol ones. Violating them produces confident wrong answers, which is worse than a crash. Rule Why Discard 64 warm-up frames before measuring a reuse estimator's energy a ReSTIR run becomes the estimator over ~ m_cap frames; from cold the deficit is invisible Warm the no-reuse control identically; it must read 1.000 otherwise the discard itself is suspect Measure ReSTIR variance per frame , never accumulated consecutive frames are correlated through the reservoirs Energy may be accumulated (it is a mean) \u2014 after the warm-up discard On the indirect term, trust energy over MSE bias\u00b2 on 11\u201318% of an image never clears both variances Aggregate over the frame; per-pixel probes are useless heavy-tailed enough to show fake bias on a DI control Score a differenced image against the indirect noise floor 1.4e-06 vs 5.8e-03 \u2014 the whole-image floor calls everything clean FLIP ranks and localizes; it does not detect bias a converged biased estimator scores well When a measurement settles something, add it to ARCHITECTURE.md 's Findings worth keeping and reference it from the code that depends on it. Recording the hypotheses that were eliminated is worth as much as the answer \u2014 that file does this deliberately, and finding 23 alone records three eliminations plus one non-finding. Each finding is a ### heading with a stable anchor. A new one also needs an entry in docs/site/findings.json giving it a tag set and a status; the documentation build asserts the two stay in bijection, so nothing can be added or renamed without being classified. Previous validation/ \u2014 the harne"},{"u":"reference/semantics.html","t":"Semantics of the estimator","q":"Reference","h":"What the estimator *is*, stated precisely enough for a reimplementation to satisfy. Reference Semantics of the estimator What the estimator is , stated precisely enough for a reimplementation to satisfy. Source: docs/architecture/semantics.md information-oriented What the estimator is , stated precisely enough to check. This is the document a reimplementation must satisfy; reference/ says where each piece lives and porting-to-racket.md says what to do with it. Notation: p\u0302 is the target function, M a confidence, W a contribution weight, m\u1d62 an MIS weight, J a Jacobian. Scene and target mode are suppressed where constant. 1. The compositional structure # Eight layers, each defined in terms of the one above. # Object Definition 1 Payload y LightSample (a point on a light) or PathSample (a path suffix through one reconnection vertex) 2 Reservoir (y, w_sum, M, W) \u2208 (Payload \u222a {\u22a5}) \u00d7 \u211d\u22650 \u00d7 \u211d\u22650 \u00d7 \u211d\u22650 3 Stream (Reservoir, Hit) \u2014 a reservoir plus the shading point that defines its target 4 ReuseDomain (p\u0302, J) \u2014 the only payload-dependent data in the algebra 5 Combination combine : Hit \u00d7 [Stream] \u00d7 [u] \u00d7 Rule \u00d7 ReuseDomain \u2192 Reservoir 6 Passes initial+temporal , spatial , resolve \u2014 the same signatures for both payloads 7 Frame loop carried state (reservoir_prev, hits_prev, cam_prev) 8 Determinism rand : (pixel, sample, bounce, dim, seed) \u2192 [0,1) , a pure hash Layer 4 is the whole trick. Exactly two inhabitants exist, and everything else is written once. 2. Payloads and measure # Both payloads store a world-space point . They differ in which measure their density lives in , and that difference \u2014 not the storage \u2014 is what decides whether reuse needs a Jacobian. LightSample PathSample Stores p, wi, dist, radiance, pdf_area, pdf_solid_angle, light_id, cos_light p (=x\u2082), n (=n\u2082), radiance (=L(x\u2082\u2192x\u2081)), pdf_solid_angle, prim_id Density in area at the sampled point solid angle at the vertex it was traced from (x\u2081) View-dependent? no yes \u21d2 Jacobian \u2261 1 the reconnection Jacobian DI_DOMAIN.jacobian \u2261 1 not because the payload is a world-space point \u2014 the GI payload is one too \u2014 but because a light sample's pdf is stored in area measure, which is view-independent. Re-deriving wi and dist from a stored point is exact ( light_sample_at ), which is what makes that identity hold rather than approximately hold. 3. Streaming RIS # A reservoir summarizes an arbitrarily long candidate stream in O(1). Absorb (Chao's weighted reservoir sampling), with an explicit confidence increment: absorb((y, s, M, W), y', w, m, u) = (y, s, M + m, W) if w \u2264 0 or w = NaN (y', s + w, M + m, W) if u < w / (s + w) (y, s + w, M + m, W) otherwise update(r, y, w, u) = absorb(r, y, w, 1, u) \u2014 a fresh candidate carries confidence 1. Property (survivor law). After streaming candidates with weights w\u1d62 , the held sample is y\u1d62 with probability w\u1d62 / \u03a3\u2c7c w\u2c7c . With w\u1d62 = p\u0302(y\u1d62)/p(y\u1d62) the survivor is distributed \u221d p\u0302 . Finalize \u2014 two forms, and which one is correct depends on whether the m\u1d62 have already been paid : finalize_ris(r, p\u0302(y)) : W = w_sum / (M \u00b7 p\u0302(y)) candidates i.i.d. from one stream finalize_mis(r, p\u0302(y)) : W = w_sum / p\u0302(y) streams already carry m\u1d62 Property. finalize_ris at M = 1 collapses to 1 / p_source , so RIS is a strict generalization of plain importance sampling. Worth asserting before trusting larger M . Trap. No 1/M in finalize_mis : the m\u1d62 already sum to one over the contributing streams, so dividing by M as well double-normalizes and darkens the result. In the kernels the choice is carried by an explicit mis_merged flag rather than inferred from M , because \"no history was found\" and \"history was found and weighted\" are different states that can leave M looking the same. 4. The reuse domain # ReuseDomain = (p\u0302, J) p\u0302 : Scene \u00d7 Hit \u00d7 Payload \u00d7 TargetMode \u2192 \u211d\u22650 J : Scene \u00d7 Payload \u00d7 Hit_from \u00d7 Hit_to \u2192 \u211d\u22650 as |d(to) / d(from)| Transformation law. A density transforms with the inverse Jacobian; a contribution weight , being a reciprocal density, with the forward one. Hence the two directions appear in different places and are not interchangeable: Site Call Why MIS denominator J(y, y_hit \u2192 stream i) carry y 's density into stream i's domain, so the balance heuristic compares like with like Absorb J(y, stream i \u2192 q) carry stream i's W into the merge target's domain The two inhabitants: DI_DOMAIN = (phat_at, \u03bb_.1.0) GI_DOMAIN = (gi_phat_at, reconnection_jacobian) Target modes # Mode p\u0302 UNSHADOWED Le \u00b7 cos_light / d\u00b2 UNSHADOWED_COSINE f \u00b7 Le \u00b7 G , unshadowed \u2014 the default FULL with visibility; one ray per candidate Property. p\u0302 must be non-zero wherever the contribution is non-zero . A target that is zero where the integrand is not is biased, full stop. This is the whole content of the grazing-epsilon finding: an epsilon added to decide whether a shift exists also zeroed p\u0302 for the canonical stream's own sample. 5. Reconnection \u2014 the only non-trivial shift # The shift mapping is the identity on the sample and non-trivial only on the measure . Both payloads store world-space points; nothing is re-traced. For a stored vertex x\u2082 seen from shading points r and q : d\u03c9 = cos(\u03b8\u2082) \u00b7 dA(x\u2082) / d\u00b2 (area \u2194 solid angle) J(r \u2192 q) = |d\u03c9_q / d\u03c9_r| = (cos_q / d_q\u00b2) \u00b7 (d_r\u00b2 / cos_r) with both cosines at x\u2082, never at x\u2081 \u2014 the measure conversion foreshortens the area element . Measure choice is exclusive. Two equivalent formulations; mixing them double-counts: Parameterization Integrand pdf in J area at x\u2082 f \u00b7 G \u00b7 L area 1 solid angle f \u00b7 cos \u03b8\u2081 \u00b7 L solid angle the ratio above This implementation uses the solid-angle form. G already contains the conversion the Jacobian applies, so gi_contribution returns f \u00b7 cos(\u03b8\u2081) \u00b7 L , not f \u00b7 G \u00b7 L . Three deliberate non-choices , each of which would be a plausible mistake: No clamp on J . A grazing reconnection is a large finite number \u2014 the correct answer, and a genuine firefly. Clamping silently rewrites the estimator. No grazing threshold in p\u0302 . Refusing a shift on a threshold is unbiased (the stream leaves the MIS denominator as well as the sum, exactly as a geometric-gate rejection does). Zeroing p\u0302 is not. Strict sidedness, no epsilon. Only DIST_EPSILON = 1e-6 survives, guarding arithmetic with no answer. Precondition. Reconnection is legal only because every BSDF here is Lambertian, so L(x\u2082\u2192x\u2081) is view-independent. This is the first thing that breaks with a glossy material. 6. MIS weights # For a fixed sample y , define each stream's unnormalized density expressed in y's domain : density\u1d62(y) = M\u1d62 \u00b7 p\u0302\u1d62(y) \u00b7 J(y, y_hit \u2192 hit\u1d62) Three rules: Rule m\u1d62(y) Cost Depends on y ? NAIVE M\u1d62 / \u03a3\u2c7c M\u2c7c O(1) no MIS (generalized balance, Talbot) density\u1d62(y) / \u03a3\u2c7c density\u2c7c(y) O(N) evals per y , O(N\u00b2) per merge yes PAIRWISE_MIS each non-canonical weighted against the canonical only: m\u1d62 = (1/K)\u00b7a/(a+b) , m_c = (1/K)\u00b7\u03a3 b/(a+b) O(N) yes The proof obligation # \u2200 y in the combined support : \u03a3\u1d62 m\u1d62(y) = 1 All three rules satisfy it. NAIVE satisfies it too \u2014 which is exactly why the partition of unity is necessary but not sufficient. NAIVE 's bias comes from m\u1d62 ignoring y entirely: a sample that some stream could never have produced still pays that stream's full share. Quantifier trap. The estimator uses m\u1d62(y\u1d62) \u2014 each stream weighted at its own sample \u2014 and those do not sum to one, nor need they . The obligation is over a fixed y . mis_weights_at computes the former; _mis_weights the latter. Any formalization must keep these apart; this is the distinction a judgment form makes unambiguous and a docstring does not. Two collapses worth knowing # Same shading point \u21d2 balance \u2261 naive, exactly. If all streams share a hit then p\u0302\u1d62(y) = p\u0302(y) for all i and J = 1 , so density\u1d62(y) = M\u1d62 \u00b7 p\u0302(y) and the ratio is M\u1d62/\u03a3M\u2c7c . Consequence: temporal reuse without reprojection is unbiased under every rule , and the :bias toggle is provably inert on that pass. Measured: the three weightings agree to f32 rounding. Bias in reuse comes from combining different domains . Two streams \u21d2 pairwise \u2261 balance, term for term. 1/K' = 1 . Measured bit-identical, which is why the temporal kernel has no third branch. 7. Combination # combine(q, streams, us, rule, dom) = let m = mis_weights(streams, rule, dom) r = fold over (s\u1d62, m\u1d62, u\u1d62): if s\u1d62.y = \u22a5 : absorb(r, \u22a5, 0, s\u1d62.M, 0) -- confidence still counts else : absorb(r, s\u1d62.y, w\u1d62, s\u1d62.M, u\u1d62) where w\u1d62 = p\u0302(q, s\u1d62.y) \u00b7 s\u1d62.W \u00b7 J(s\u1d62.y, s\u1d62.hit \u2192 q) \u00b7 (m\u1d62 if rule \u2260 NAIVE else s\u1d62.M) in finalize(r, p\u0302(q, r.y), ris if rule = NAIVE else mis) Three properties this must preserve: Empty streams still carry confidence. absorb(r, \u22a5, 0, s\u1d62.M, 0) \u2014 and the caller must not filter them out either. Both halves are required; see 07-invariants . The Jacobian is not an MIS weight and does not cancel against one. NAIVE still pays it. Leaving it out is a second, independent bias. E[w_sum] is preserved by a reuse pass. This is the estimator's own internal quantity and the decisive diagnostic \u2014 it was E[w_sum] , read straight out of the reservoir buffers, that identified the empty-stream defe"},{"u":"reference/conformance.html","t":"Conformance vectors","q":"Reference","h":"The frozen golden values a port must reproduce exactly, and why the RNG comes first. Reference Conformance vectors The frozen golden values a port must reproduce exactly, and why the RNG comes first. Source: docs/architecture/conformance/README.md information-oriented Frozen golden values for the parts of the system a reimplementation must reproduce exactly , starting with the one that gates everything else. python docs/architecture/conformance/verify_conformance.py # RNG, check this repo python docs/architecture/conformance/gen_conformance.py # step 1 \u2014 regenerate python docs/architecture/conformance/gen_scene_conformance.py # step 2 python docs/architecture/conformance/gen_geometry_conformance.py # step 3 python docs/architecture/conformance/gen_reservoir_conformance.py # step 4 python docs/architecture/conformance/gen_reconnect_conformance.py # step 5 python docs/architecture/conformance/gen_graph_conformance.py # step 6 File Step What it pins rng_vectors.json 1 hash4 , rand1 , the whole dimension layout scene_ir.json 2 every bundled scene's elaborated SemanticIR geometry.json 3 intersection, camera, lights, shading, per-sample di_uniform coverage_scene.edn 3 a fixture scene , reaching branches no bundled scene does reservoir.json 4 absorb , both finalizes, the three MIS rules, combine_streams , di_ris , and the ReSTIR frame loop per sample reconnect.json 5 the shift, the Jacobian's hand-derived laws, gi_phat_at , gi_uniform , the GI identity and the GI frame loop graph.json 6 the declared graph, the five compile stages, describe() verbatim, and dirty_steps over all 32 domain subsets frameloop.json 7 camera paths, the reprojection map, per-frame radiance along a moving camera, the warm-up rewind, and the warmed indirect energy table Why the RNG comes first # porting-to-racket.md step 1 is the RNG, and it is not reorderable: every later verification in that document is a per-sample diff , and a per-sample diff requires identical draws. Until a port reproduces these values it cannot be checked against anything except statistics \u2014 and statistics on ~11% of an image will not see a defect losing a sixth of the indirect term. What is covered \u2014 the RNG # rng_vectors.json \u2014 2,240 vectors, ~97 KB, plain JSON so it needs no Python to consume. Section Contents hash4 [pixel, sample, bounce, dim, seed, expected_u32] rand1 the uniform extraction, positionally parallel to hash4 layout bounce ids, strides, and every *_dim accessor tabulated The sweep is chosen for coverage, not volume : each axis at its extremes, a power-of-two edge, one interior value, and every BOUNCE_* id. A port that reproduces the boundaries and fails in the interior is not a failure mode a pure integer hash has. verify_conformance.py additionally checks two things the table cannot express: Injectivity over runtime-scaled blocks. No two draws may share (bounce, dim) . Candidate and tap blocks are M -wide, so a fixed offset can collide for some M and not others \u2014 checked over M \u2208 (1, 2, 8, 32) . The two deliberate invariants. gi_cand_dim(0, U/V) == bsdf_dim(0, U/V) , the alias that makes ReSTIR GI at M=1 with reuse off draw the same numbers as the two-bounce uniform path tracer, so the two are comparable per sample; and shade_bounce(0) == BOUNCE_SHADE , without which differencing against a one-bounce render stops being exact and every GI measurement silently changes meaning. What is covered \u2014 geometry, camera, lights, shading # geometry.json \u2014 ~910 KB over four scenes. The sections are ordered so a failure localizes , which is the point of having more than one: Section Contents What only it can catch epsilons EPS , shadow_epsilon and scatter_epsilon tabulated at seven distances a bounce ray taking the shadow ray's relative term \u2014 nothing in a DI render calls scatter_epsilon at all bsdf INV_PI , onb on axis-aligned and diagonal normals, lambert_sample onb 's sign branch at n_z == 0 , which is what every Cornell wall has primitives the three intersection routines called directly, 26 hand-chosen rays EPS , and the dot(u, v) terms of the quad solve \u2014 see below dispatch intersect_prim selecting on kind three correct intersectors wired to the wrong kinds pick_light uniform selection swept across [0, 1] the clamp to n - 1 rays primary ray and closest hit, adjacent camera basis vs. intersection, told apart projection project_point / reproject_pixel at every hit an inverse that is approximate rather than exact light_samples area sampling over a (u, v) grid, plus the same point re-derived from a different shading point the sphere branch's poles; that pdf_area does not depend on where you look from occlusion shadow ray, its epsilon, the limit, G , all three p\u0302 modes the epsilon that cancels itself di_uniform the complete estimator, per pixel per sample , three samples everything, but only after the above have said where coverage how many hits, of which kinds, how many occluded a fixture that silently stopped reaching a branch coverage_scene.edn is a fixture scene rather than a shipped one, because the nine scenes in pt/scenes/ are chosen to make estimators differ and that leaves four branches of pt/core/ with no coverage at all: a sphere area light, a two-sided light, a plane as a shadow caster, and a quad with non-perpendicular edges. It lives here so adding it does not change scene_ir.json or pt edn 's listing; both implementations load it by path. What is covered \u2014 the reuse algebra # reservoir.json \u2014 ~950 KB over five configurations, in the order a defect propagates: Section Contents What only it can catch absorb Chao's rule on integer payloads , no scene involved a streaming bug masquerading as a p\u0302 bug; the two rejection cases, where a non-positive or NaN weight adds confidence but keeps the incumbent finalize finalize_ris and finalize_mis tabulated side by side confusing them \u2014 the difference is one factor of M , and it darkens the image by exactly that gates neighbor_offsets , accept_neighbor , accept_reprojection , over taps and partners across the frame reusing the spatial gate for reprojection. On taps alone the two agree trivially; only distant partners on the same plane separate them mis all three rules, \u03a3 m\u1d62(y) at a fixed y , and \u03a3 m\u1d62(y\u1d62) recorded as not summing to one a port \"fixing\" the second into a partition of unity, which breaks the estimator to satisfy a requirement that never existed di_ris initial RIS per pixel per sample the resampling weight w = p\u0302 / p_source frames the three-pass loop per pixel per frame, both reservoir buffers, plus a whole-frame checksum a temporal bug and a spatial bug produce the same wrong pixel; the buffers tell them apart, and the checksum keeps the strided pixel dump honest bias_demo appears three times, once per weighting, because it is the scene built to make the 1/M rule visibly wrong: if a port's three modes agree there, its MIS weighting is not doing anything. Measured first-frame energy at 24\u00d724 \u2014 naive 911.1, mis 957.6, pairwise 956.7. Two things this fixture deliberately does not pin. It never runs initial RIS at a pixel whose primary ray missed: the renderer does not, and doing so reaches materials[-1] , which Python resolves to the last material and a conforming host rejects \u2014 pinning that would freeze an accident. And the property \u03a3\u1d62 m\u1d62(y) = 1 is not a table at all; it is quantified over configurations, so it lives in port/racket/test/mis-properties.rkt under random generation. That is what turned up PAIRWISE_MIS 's conditional normalization, which no fixture generated from a scene could have shown. What is covered \u2014 reconnection # reconnect.json \u2014 ~730 KB over two GI scenes. The smallest section matters most: Section Contents What only it can catch jacobian_laws hand-built inputs touching no scene , carrying the hand-derived answer and the Python's an inverted Jacobian. Reciprocity J(a\u2192b)\u00b7J(b\u2192a) = 1 and the identity case both hold for the inverse, so only J = 4, not 1/4 pins the direction reconnect every (traced-from, evaluated-at) pixel pair, including same-pixel a cosine taken at x1 instead of x2; a grazing epsilon that breaks the identity shift first, silently discarding the sample the pixel actually traced candidates trace_path_candidate and the initial path reservoir the secondary NEE drawn from the wrong RNG stream gi_merge combine_streams under GI_DOMAIN , streams built at different shading points the Jacobian hook itself \u2014 direct lighting's is identically 1, so nothing in step 4 can show it doing work gi_uniform the two-bounce path tracer per pixel per sample the throughput chain, and the bounce ray's own epsilon identity both estimators' columns, plus the residual and its eps/d bound a measure convention that is self-consistent and wrong frames the GI loop, both buffers, plus a whole-frame checksum finding 23 \u2014 GI rolls the pre-spatial reservoir, DI the post-spatial one The identity is not bit-exact, and the fixture records why rather than tolerating it. The bounce ray leaves from x1 + eps\u00b7n , so the path trace"},{"u":"reference/cli.html","t":"Command reference","q":"Reference","h":"Every `pt` subcommand and its options, captured from the tool itself. Reference Command reference Every pt subcommand and its options, captured from the tool itself. information-oriented Captured from pt 0.1.0 by docs/site/capture_cli.py . Regenerate it after changing pt/cli.py . usage: pt [-h] {render,gui,describe,edn,dump,flip,validate} ... An introspectable Direct-Lighting / RIS / ReSTIR DI path tracer positional arguments: {render,gui,describe,edn,dump,flip,validate} render render an image headlessly gui launch the interactive shell describe print the compiled execution plan edn print the scene as canonical EDN dump dump every cooked buffer to .npy flip perceptual (FLIP) difference between two images validate run the correctness harness options: -h, --help show this help message and exit pt render # usage: pt render [-h] [--res W H] [--strategy {uniform,ris,restir-di,restir-gi}] [--M M] [--bounces BOUNCES] [--bias {naive,mis,pairwise-mis}] [--seed SEED] [--gpu] [--spp SPP] [-o OUT] [scene] positional arguments: scene bundled scene name or path to an .edn file options: -h, --help show this help message and exit --res W H --strategy {uniform,ris,restir-di,restir-gi} --M M RIS candidate count --bounces BOUNCES path depth (1 = direct lighting; restir-gi requires 2; deeper is uniform only) --bias {naive,mis,pairwise-mis} --seed SEED --gpu --spp SPP -o OUT, --out OUT pt gui # usage: pt gui [-h] [--res W H] [--strategy {uniform,ris,restir-di,restir-gi}] [--M M] [--bounces BOUNCES] [--bias {naive,mis,pairwise-mis}] [--seed SEED] [--gpu] [--frames FRAMES] [scene] positional arguments: scene bundled scene name or path to an .edn file options: -h, --help show this help message and exit --res W H --strategy {uniform,ris,restir-di,restir-gi} --M M RIS candidate count --bounces BOUNCES path depth (1 = direct lighting; restir-gi requires 2; deeper is uniform only) --bias {naive,mis,pairwise-mis} --seed SEED --gpu --frames FRAMES render N UI frames then exit (headless verification) pt describe # usage: pt describe [-h] [--res W H] [--strategy {uniform,ris,restir-di,restir-gi}] [--M M] [--bounces BOUNCES] [--bias {naive,mis,pairwise-mis}] [--seed SEED] [--gpu] [scene] positional arguments: scene bundled scene name or path to an .edn file options: -h, --help show this help message and exit --res W H --strategy {uniform,ris,restir-di,restir-gi} --M M RIS candidate count --bounces BOUNCES path depth (1 = direct lighting; restir-gi requires 2; deeper is uniform only) --bias {naive,mis,pairwise-mis} --seed SEED --gpu pt edn # usage: pt edn [-h] [--res W H] [--strategy {uniform,ris,restir-di,restir-gi}] [--M M] [--bounces BOUNCES] [--bias {naive,mis,pairwise-mis}] [--seed SEED] [--gpu] [scene] positional arguments: scene bundled scene name or path to an .edn file options: -h, --help show this help message and exit --res W H --strategy {uniform,ris,restir-di,restir-gi} --M M RIS candidate count --bounces BOUNCES path depth (1 = direct lighting; restir-gi requires 2; deeper is uniform only) --bias {naive,mis,pairwise-mis} --seed SEED --gpu pt dump # usage: pt dump [-h] [--res W H] [--strategy {uniform,ris,restir-di,restir-gi}] [--M M] [--bounces BOUNCES] [--bias {naive,mis,pairwise-mis}] [--seed SEED] [--gpu] [--spp SPP] [--dir DIR] [scene] positional arguments: scene bundled scene name or path to an .edn file options: -h, --help show this help message and exit --res W H --strategy {uniform,ris,restir-di,restir-gi} --M M RIS candidate count --bounces BOUNCES path depth (1 = direct lighting; restir-gi requires 2; deeper is uniform only) --bias {naive,mis,pairwise-mis} --seed SEED --gpu --spp SPP --dir DIR pt flip # usage: pt flip [-h] [-o OUT] [--raw RAW] [--mode {auto,ldr,hdr}] [--tonemap {aces,reinhard,linear}] [--exposures EXPOSURES] [--start-exposure START_EXPOSURE] [--stop-exposure STOP_EXPOSURE] [--ppd PPD] [--distance DISTANCE] [--width WIDTH] [--monitor-px MONITOR_PX] [--match-resolution] reference test positional arguments: reference .npy linear radiance or .png sRGB test the image under test, same kind and shape options: -h, --help show this help message and exit -o OUT, --out OUT write the magma error map here ('' to skip) --raw RAW also save the raw [0,1] error field as .npy --mode {auto,ldr,hdr} auto: HDR for .npy radiance, LDR for .png (default) --tonemap {aces,reinhard,linear} tone curve for the HDR sweep, or for LDR from radiance --exposures EXPOSURES stops in the HDR sweep (0 = derived from the reference) --start-exposure START_EXPOSURE --stop-exposure STOP_EXPOSURE --ppd PPD pixels per degree (default: 67, the published viewing conditions) --distance DISTANCE metres to the display --width WIDTH display width in metres --monitor-px MONITOR_PX display width in pixels --match-resolution treat the image as filling the display, rather than assuming a 4K monitor showing it 1:1 pt validate # usage: pt validate [-h] [--res W H] [--strategy {uniform,ris,restir-di,restir-gi}] [--M M] [--bounces BOUNCES] [--bias {naive,mis,pairwise-mis}] [--seed SEED] [--gpu] [--spp SPP] [--ref-spp REF_SPP] [--anchor-stride ANCHOR_STRIDE] [--stride STRIDE] [--budget BUDGET] [--checkpoints CHECKPOINTS [CHECKPOINTS ...]] [--no-flip] [--flip-maps DIR] [--ppd PPD] [--variance-trials VARIANCE_TRIALS] [--warmup WARMUP] [--temporal] [--temporal-frames TEMPORAL_FRAMES] [--magnify MAGNIFY] [scene] positional arguments: scene bundled scene name or path to an .edn file options: -h, --help show this help message and exit --res W H --strategy {uniform,ris,restir-di,restir-gi} --M M RIS candidate count --bounces BOUNCES path depth (1 = direct lighting; restir-gi requires 2; deeper is uniform only) --bias {naive,mis,pairwise-mis} --seed SEED --gpu --spp SPP --ref-spp REF_SPP --anchor-stride ANCHOR_STRIDE pixel grid for closed-form anchors --stride STRIDE pixel stride for the sign test (0 = derive from the reuse radius); must exceed it or the test is skipped --budget BUDGET equal-time seconds --checkpoints CHECKPOINTS [CHECKPOINTS ...] --no-flip skip the perceptual (FLIP) section --flip-maps DIR also write a magma FLIP error map per estimator --ppd PPD pixels per degree for FLIP (default: 67) --variance-trials VARIANCE_TRIALS independent seeds for the per-frame variance section (<2 skips it) --warmup WARMUP frames of reuse to run and discard before measuring (reuse strategies only) --temporal also test temporal reprojection over an A/B camera path --temporal-frames TEMPORAL_FRAMES camera-path length for --temporal --magnify MAGNIFY how far the history camera is dollied back for --temporal; sets how many pixel footprints the reprojected point moves Previous Conformance vectors Next Scene catalogue"},{"u":"reference/scenes.html","t":"Scene catalogue","q":"Reference","h":"The bundled scenes, their integrator configuration, and what each exists to exercise. Reference Scene catalogue The bundled scenes, their integrator configuration, and what each exists to exercise. information-oriented Every scene in pt/scenes/ , elaborated from source by docs/site/capture_cli.py \u2014 including each scene's own header comment, so this page cannot drift from what the scenes say about themselves. A scene loads by bare name ( pt render cornell_restir_gi ) or by path. The catalogue # scene transport sampling M bounces spp resolution lights prims analytic_spheres direct lighting :uniform 1 1 512 256&times;256 1 3 bias_demo direct lighting :restir-di 8 1 128 256&times;256 12 17 cornell direct lighting :uniform 1 1 256 512&times;512 1 8 cornell_gi global illumination :uniform 1 2 256 512&times;512 1 8 cornell_restir direct lighting :restir-di 32 1 128 512&times;512 1 8 cornell_restir_gi global illumination :restir-gi 1 2 128 512&times;512 1 8 gi_bias_demo global illumination :restir-gi 1 2 128 256&times;256 1 18 many_lights direct lighting :restir-di 8 1 64 256&times;256 16 23 reproject_demo direct lighting :restir-di 8 1 128 192&times;192 12 17 sampling is the :light-sampling axis and transport is :type ; they are independent, and only some pairings mean anything. See the estimator matrix . What each scene is for # analytic_spheres # The analytic validation scene. Configured for :uniform at :M 1 with target :unshadowed-cosine and :bias :mis . bias_demo # The scene that makes 1/M reuse visibly biased. Configured for :restir-di at :M 8 with target :unshadowed-cosine and :bias :mis . cornell # The Cornell box, authored in the EDN DSL. Configured for :uniform at :M 1 with target :unshadowed-cosine and :bias :mis . cornell_gi # The Cornell box again, with one indirect bounce. Configured for :uniform at :M 1 with target :unshadowed-cosine and :bias :mis . cornell_restir # The Cornell box with ReSTIR DI. Configured for :restir-di at :M 32 with target :unshadowed-cosine and :bias :mis . cornell_restir_gi # The Cornell box with ReSTIR GI: one indirect bounce, resampled. Configured for :restir-gi at :M 1 with target :unshadowed-cosine and :bias :mis . gi_bias_demo # The scene that makes 1/M path reuse visibly biased. Configured for :restir-gi at :M 1 with target :unshadowed-cosine and :bias :mis . many_lights # Many scattered lights \u2014 the regime RIS and ReSTIR actually exist for. Configured for :restir-di at :M 8 with target :unshadowed-cosine and :bias :mis . reproject_demo # The scene that makes 1/M temporal reuse measurably biased. Configured for :restir-di at :M 8 with target :unshadowed-cosine and :bias :mis . Previous Command reference"},{"u":"explanation/architecture.html","t":"Architecture","q":"Explanation","h":"The design in depth: the two load-bearing ideas, the layers, and what is deliberately out of scope. Explanation Architecture The design in depth: the two load-bearing ideas, the layers, and what is deliberately out of scope. Source: ARCHITECTURE.md understanding-oriented A Direct-Lighting \u2192 RIS \u2192 ReSTIR DI path tracer built as a lowering pipeline : a symbolic scene description is transformed through progressively more concrete representations until it reaches executable kernels, and every intermediate is a first-class, inspectable artifact. cornell.edn text \u2502 read spanned s-expression forms \u25bc forms pt/dsl/reader.py \u2502 macro-expand user + builtin macros, spans preserved \u25bc forms pt/dsl/macro.py \u2502 analyze shape checking, per-field source spans \u25bc SceneAST level 2 pt/dsl/ast.py \u2502 elaborate resolve \u00b7 default \u00b7 normalize \u00b7 stitch \u00b7 expand \u25bc SemanticIR level 2.5 pt/dsl/ir.py \u2502 declare passes state their reads and writes \u25bc RawGraph level 3 pt/graph/builder.py \u2502 compile order \u00b7 cull \u00b7 lifetimes \u00b7 alias \u00b7 guard \u25bc ExecutionPlan level 4 pt/graph/plan.py \u2502 execute walk a schedule; no reasoning \u25bc Taichi kernels pt/kernels/ \u2502 cook \u25bc artifacts: radiance, AOVs, reservoirs, path trees Each arrow is a pure transform. Any level can be dumped and diffed ( pt edn , pt describe , pt dump ). The two load-bearing ideas # 1. Functional core, imperative shell \u2014 with the boundary in the right place. pt/core/ is genuinely pure: no I/O, no globals, no mutation of inputs, f64 throughout. pt/shell/ owns threads, windows, input and files. But the Taichi kernels in pt/kernels/ mutate fields freely, because pretending otherwise would make them slow and awkward for no benefit. The contract lives at the pass boundary instead: a pass may only touch resources it declared, and its declared outputs are a deterministic function of its declared inputs, params and seed. graph/compile.py enforces this by handing each pass a scoped CompileContext \u2014 reaching for an undeclared buffer raises when the graph is built, not on some later frame. 2. Counter-based RNG is what makes everything else possible. Every stochastic decision is a pure hash of (pixel, sample, bounce, dim, seed) . ti.random() is banned. Three consequences the design leans on hard: the pure reference and the Taichi kernel draw the same numbers , so they can be diffed per sample rather than merely statistically; clicking a pixel re-derives the path that was actually traced , so the inspector shows evidence rather than a plausible re-roll; the worker can be interrupted and restarted at any point and still produce identical images, which is what makes camera motion cheap. The dimension layout in core/rng.py is a documented contract with kernels/_common.py , and it allocates one stream per path vertex : vertex 0 keeps BOUNCE_SHADE , so a two-bounce render draws exactly the numbers a one-bounce render draws at its first vertex, and deeper vertices get ids of their own rather than an offset inside the candidate block \u2014 whose width is M * CAND_STRIDE for a runtime M , so any fixed offset would collide for some M and the collision would be invisible. Two correlated draws look exactly like two independent ones. Layers # pt/dsl/ \u2014 the front end # EDN surface syntax with source spans threaded through every stage , so a semantic error found three phases later still underlines the exact keyword: error[UNKNOWN_MATERIAL]: geometry :s_left references unknown material :m_wihte --> cornell.edn:41:74 | 41 | {:id :s_left :prim :sphere :center [185 120 169] :material :m_wihte} | ^^^^^^^^ help: did you mean :m_white? Validation is layered, not duplicated : analyze catches shape errors (is this a 3-vector? is :radius present?), elaborate catches semantic ones (does that material exist? is this light bound to an infinite plane?). Errors are collected and raised once, so four typos report four errors. The elaborate pass does five jobs, kept mentally separate because they fail differently: resolve names to dense indices, default omitted fields, normalize derived quantities (camera basis, quad normals and areas), stitch lights to their geometry in both directions, and expand the integrator keyword into the flags the graph builder switches on. :type (which transport) and :light-sampling (how it is sampled) are separate axes, and elaborate is the only place that knows which of their combinations mean anything. :direct-lighting requires :max-bounces 1 ; :global-illumination requires two or more; multi-bounce resampling is refused there , with a span, rather than surfacing as a graph-build failure or \u2014 far worse \u2014 as a render that quietly traces fewer bounces than it was asked for. Those two constraints are each other's converse, which is what lets the depth be the single source of truth: writer.py re-derives :type from :max-bounces instead of the IR carrying a second copy that could disagree with the first. Index assignment is source-ordered , never hash- or alphabetical-ordered. That is load-bearing: it makes every downstream buffer layout, RNG stream and ReSTIR reservoir index a deterministic function of the source text. pt/core/ \u2014 the pure reference # A complete, readable, obviously-correct implementation of the whole renderer in f64 Python. It is slow, and that is the accepted price of being checkable. It serves three roles at once: the correctness oracle the kernels are diffed against, the engine behind click-to-inspect , and the thing simple enough to validate against closed-form ground truth. integrators/gi_uniform.py is the multi-bounce member of that family: NEE at every path vertex, cosine-weighted BSDF sampling between them, and Le added at the primary hit only. It shares _shared.nee_sample with di_uniform , so \"GI at one bounce is direct lighting\" is true through the same function calls rather than by careful transcription \u2014 the two agree bit for bit, not in the mean. reservoir.py is where ReSTIR is either right or silently wrong, so the three combination rules ( naive , mis , pairwise-mis ) are spelled out there rather than folded into a helper. That file holds one copy of the reuse algebra for two payloads. A ReuseDomain is the whole difference between reusing a light sample and reusing a path: a target function, and a shift mapping's Jacobian. Everything else \u2014 Chao's update, the three weightings, the partition-of-unity argument, the rule that a stream which found nothing still carries its confidence \u2014 is payload-agnostic and must stay that way; a second copy is where a fix would reach one estimator and not the other. The payloads are separate types ( LightSample , PathSample ) and separate device structs, so the statements that a light reservoir's Jacobian is 1 because it stores a world-space point stay true verbatim rather than becoming conditionally true. The seam is testable before the second payload uses it: injecting a deliberately wrong Jacobian must move the answer, while DI_DOMAIN 's must be exactly 1 \u2014 which is how a refactor that quietly dropped the factor would be caught, since every DI test would otherwise still pass. reconnect.py is the path payload's half of that: the shift mapping and its Jacobian. Connecting a stored vertex to a new shading point is an exact re-derivation, as it is for a light sample \u2014 a point does not change when you look at it from elsewhere \u2014 so the only real content is the change of measure: dw = cos_2 dA(x2) / d ^ 2 => J(r -> q) = (cos_q / d_q ^ 2) * (d_r ^ 2 / cos_r) with both cosines at x2 . Three plausible-looking mistakes, each producing a clean and wrong image, are worth naming because the code is written to make them hard: using the cosine at x1 (that one belongs to the integrand, not the change of variables, and reads as a shading error); inverting the ratio (a contribution weight is a reciprocal density, so it takes the forward Jacobian \u2014 backwards is a factor of (d/d')^4 that does not shrink with sample count); and clamping instead of rejecting. J diverges as cos_r -> 0 , which is a real firefly source, but clamping it silently rewrites the estimator, whereas refusing the reconnection does not \u2014 provided the refusal also removes that stream from the MIS denominator, which it does because both paths call the same function. Reconnection reuses the stored radiance as if it were view-independent. For a Lambertian x2 that is exact , and every material here is Lambertian; it is also the first thing a glossy BSDF breaks, which would need the suffix re-evaluated rather than merely re-weighted. This is the same view-independence argument the temporal-reprojection finding already leans on. pt/graph/ \u2014 the render graph # Frostbite's declare \u2192 compile \u2192 execute, with SSA-versioned virtual resources. Compile runs rarely and does all the reasoning; execute runs every frame and does none. assemble_di_graph is the single place the DL \u2192 RIS \u2192 ReSTIR progression is expressed. Uniform and RIS are one shade node; ReSTIR expands into InitialAndTemporal \u2192 Spatial \u2192 Resolve with persistent reservoirs. Ev"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-shadow-ray-epsilon-that-cancels-itself","t":"A shadow-ray epsilon that cancels itself","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"Offsetting the ray origin forward by `eps` *and* shortening the far end by `eps` makes the two cancel, putting the light's own surface exactly on the `t < limit` boundary \u2014 a comparison then decided by rounding, which f64 and f32 resolve differently. It caused **31% of pixels** to disagree between the reference and the kernel. Shortening by twice the offset, with a scene-scale-relative term, took that to 0.2%."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#titemplate-does-not-thread-a-scalar-counter","t":"`ti.template()` does not thread a scalar counter","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"The natural stateful RNG \u2014 `rand(seed)` advancing a caller-local counter \u2014 fails silently in Taichi: every draw in a kernel returns the same number, and the output still looks like plausible noise. The fix was the design the architecture already called for: fully stateless, explicitly-indexed draws."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-seed-that-never-reached-the-rng","t":"A seed that never reached the RNG","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"The scene's `:seed` flowed through the whole IR and was never mixed into the hash, so two \"independent\" reference renders were byte-identical and the measured noise floor was exactly zero."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#bias-detection-needs-both-estimators-variances","t":"Bias detection needs both estimators' variances","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"`E[MSE(X, Y)] = vx + vy + bias\u00b2`. Comparing against the reference's noise floor alone charges the estimator's own variance to bias \u2014 uniform MC at 400 spp against a uniform reference at 4000 spp reports as badly biased, though it *is* the reference's estimator."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#the-sign-test-assumes-independent-pixels-reuse-violates-that","t":"The sign test assumes independent pixels; reuse violates that","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"ReSTIR's neighbouring pixels literally share samples, so a per-pixel binomial test is wildly over-confident and flags every configuration, including provably-unbiased ones. Subsampling beyond the reuse radius fixes it \u2014 but too large a stride leaves no statistical power, so `sign_test_n` is always reported."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#which-statistic-you-measure-decides-whether-you-see-the-effect","t":"Which statistic you measure decides whether you see the effect","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"For the variance ordering (uniform > RIS > ReSTIR), the *mean* per-pixel variance is dominated by a handful of fireflies and moved under 5% across estimators; the *median* samples pixels that were never noisy, where RIS genuinely loses. At the **90th percentile** \u2014 where perceptible noise lives \u2014 the expected ordering is clean: RIS 1.9\u00d7 better than uniform, ReSTIR 1.5\u00d7 better than RIS."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#restir-must-be-measured-per-frame-not-accumulated","t":"ReSTIR must be measured per frame, not accumulated","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"Consecutive frames are strongly correlated through the reservoirs, so averaging N of them is worth far fewer than N independent samples. Comparing accumulated means makes ReSTIR look *worse* than plain RIS."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#many-lights-is-not-a-light-count","t":"\"Many lights\" is not a light count","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"Sixteen lamps in a ceiling grid look like the regime resampling is for, but every floor point sees them at similar distance and angle, their target values differ by under 2\u00d7, and RIS beat uniform by ~2%. Scattering the same sixteen through the *volume* at different heights, sizes and intensities is what creates the condition."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#where-1m-reuse-actually-goes-wrong--and-why-it-took-a-purpose-built-scene","t":"Where 1/M reuse actually goes wrong \u2014 and why it took a purpose-built scene","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"The intuitive place to look for spatial-reuse bias is a geometry discontinuity. It is the wrong place: the geometric similarity gate *rejects* those neighbours, so it is already doing, crudely, the job MIS does properly. On an ordinary Cornell box `:naive` and `:mis` differed by 0.2% \u2014 under the noise floor. The bias needs neighbours that **pass** the gate \u2014 same surface, same normal, same depth \u2014 yet disagree about which samples are *possible*. Lights standing perpendicular to a flat floor produce exactly that: `cos_light` flips sign along a razor-sharp line across a plane, so two adjacent pixels are geometrically identical while one assigns zero probability to samples the other can produce. On `scenes/bias_demo.edn`, `:naive` converges to **60% of the correct energy**; `:mis` and `:pairwise-mis` land within 3%. The detection sweep is the clearest statement of what bias *is*: | spp | `naive` excess | `mis` excess | `naive` energy vs truth | |---|---|---|---| | 250 | +1.71x | \u22120.16x | 0.603 | | 600 | **+4.39x** | \u22120.03x | 0.596 | | 1200 | **+5.74x** | \u22120.32x | 0.594 | | 2000 | **+5.64x** | \u22120.33x | 0.597 | The energy deficit is *constant*; only the noise hiding it shrinks. And the biased estimator is **15x less noisy** than the correct one while being 12x further from the truth \u2014 clean, stable, and wrong. Tonemapped, the two renders are hard to tell apart, which is the whole ar"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#bias-also-shows-in-the-convergence-curves-shape-sooner-than-in-its-mse","t":"Bias also shows in the convergence curve's shape, sooner than in its MSE","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"`slope_verdict` fits the *tail* of the curve: an unbiased estimator keeps falling near -1, a biased one flattens onto its bias-squared plateau. It needs only the curve to have turned over, not full convergence, so it fires earlier than the excess-over-noise test. Fitting the whole curve instead of the tail dilutes the evidence, because early checkpoints fall steeply either way."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-scene-can-be-pathological-in-a-way-that-breaks-the-statistics","t":"A scene can be pathological in a way that breaks the statistics","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"The first version of `bias_demo` used small, intense emitters. Uniform sampling's error went heavy-tailed, a handful of fireflies dominated the MSE, one realization landed 15x above its own expectation, and the harness reported *uniform MC* as biased against a reference uniform MC had produced \u2014 with a convergence slope of **+0.71**, error rising with sample count. Nothing was biased; a statistic was being applied to a distribution it does not describe. Dialling brightness down kept the support-splitting effect and made every estimator well behaved."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#temporal-reuse-is-unbiased-under-every-mode-without-reprojection--and-that-is-informative","t":"Temporal reuse is unbiased under every mode *without reprojection* \u2014 and that is informative","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"With no reprojection the canonical and temporal streams share a shading point and therefore a target function, so the balance heuristic collapses exactly to the 1/M form: measured, the three weightings agree to f32 rounding. The `:bias` toggle genuinely does nothing to that pass. It says the bias in reuse comes from combining *different domains* \u2014 which is why it lives in the spatial pass, and why it returns the moment reprojection is turned on."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#reprojecting-a-static-scene-correctly-gives-back-the-same-shading-point-so-the-bias-has-to-come-from-somewhere-else--and-where-it-comes-from-is-magnification","t":"Reprojecting a static scene correctly gives back the same shading point, so the bias has to come from somewhere else \u2014 and where it comes from is magnification","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"This was the surprise. `wi` and `dist` are re-derived exactly from the stored world-space point, and the target function is view-independent for a Lambertian surface, so a correctly reprojected pixel lands on the point it started from. A large lateral camera slide changes nothing measurable. The residual error is the *pixel quantization*: `hits_prev[floor(projection)]` is whatever that pixel's jittered ray hit, up to one **previous-frame** footprint away along the surface. That footprint is measured in the previous frame's resolution on that surface, so it is only large when the surface was *smaller* on screen then \u2014 i.e. under magnification. Dolly the history camera back by `k` and the reprojected point moves `k` current footprints, which is spatial reuse at radius `k` arriving through the temporal pass with no MIS weight to correct it. (`validation/temporal.py`) | history magnified | `mis` energy | `naive` energy | `naive` MSE excess | |---|---|---|---| | 1x (still) | 0.9990 | 0.9987 | \u22120.10x | | 3x | 0.9994 | 0.9930 | \u22120.02x | | 6x | 1.0024 | 0.9917 | +0.02x | | 12x | 1.0044 | 0.9711 | +0.09x | | 20x | 0.9920 | **0.9445** | **+2.57x** | | 40x | 0.9976 | **0.8902** | **+7.64x** | `mis` sits on the reference at every setting while `naive` degrades smoothly, and `pairwise-mis` is *bit-identical* to `mis` here \u2014 with exactly two streams the pairwise rule reduces to the balance h"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-moving-camera-has-no-single-reference-unless-the-camera-comes-back","t":"A moving camera has no single reference, unless the camera comes back","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"Per-frame comparison against a converged reference would need one reference per pose, which is the expensive part of the harness multiplied by the frame count. Alternating between two poses and measuring only the frames at pose A gives one reference that serves every measured frame, while every measured frame's history came from pose B and was genuinely reprojected. Accumulating across the path is not an option: the mean of images taken from different viewpoints is not an image of anything, so frames are extracted individually by differencing the accumulator (`Engine.render_sample_radiance`)."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-firefly-in-the-scene-geometry-cost-the-mse-test-all-of-its-power","t":"A firefly in the scene geometry cost the MSE test all of its power","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"The first draft of `reproject_demo` inherited `bias_demo`'s layout, in which four light posts run through the back wall \u2014 putting wall points at distance ~0 from a samplable light point, where the geometric term goes as 1/d\u00b2. Single-pixel radiances reached 5000 against a scene mean near 1, the mean squared error ran 25000x the *median* squared error, and it did not fall with more frames, it wandered. The bias detector read **+0.14x excess** on an estimator losing a tenth of the scene's light. Moving the posts inside the box dropped MSE by **37x**, and the same test then read **+2.57x** and fired. The lesson is not that MSE is the wrong statistic \u2014 it is that MSE is the *fragile* one, and it fails by quietly returning \"unbiased\". Total energy, which uses the whole image as its sample, resolved the deficit either way."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#the-temporal-gate-cannot-be-the-spatial-gate","t":"The temporal gate cannot be the spatial gate","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"`accept_neighbor` compares `hit.t`, the distance from the eye. Two pixels of one frame share an eye, so that is a fair proxy for depth; two frames of a moving camera do not. Dolly by 20% and a *perfectly* reprojected static point \u2014 same position, same normal \u2014 fails the test, and all reuse is discarded exactly when it would have paid. The camera-independent test is the plane distance, `|dot(p_prev \u2212 p_q, n_q)| < 0.02\u00b7t`, which tolerates the tangential slop a floored pixel index guarantees while still catching a disocclusion."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-stream-that-found-nothing-still-has-confidence-m","t":"A stream that found nothing still has confidence M","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"Found by the reference-vs-kernel diff, and only findable that way: `combine_streams` skipped streams whose sample was `None` *including their `M`*, so on pixels where the canonical stream drew M candidates and found every one unusable, the reference's `W` was off from the kernel's by a clean factor of two. The selected sample matched, so nothing but a numeric comparison could see it \u2014 and under the 1/M rule that discarded `M` is precisely what should have been diluting the other stream."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#clearing-the-accumulator-must-not-rewind-the-rng","t":"Clearing the accumulator must not rewind the RNG","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"During a reprojecting camera drag the accumulated image is cleared every frame. Rewinding the sample counter with it would hand every frame of the drag the same uniforms \u2014 the same sub-pixel jitter and, because `sample_light_point(li, u, v)` is deterministic in `u` and `v`, literally the same M points on the same M lights. The canonical stream would stop contributing new information the instant the drag began, which is the opposite of what preserving reuse across the drag is for. `Engine.spp` is therefore the accumulated count and `ctx.spp_index` is the RNG counter; they coincide everywhere except that one path."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-per-sample-reference-vs-kernel-diff-proves-transcription-not-measure","t":"A per-sample reference-vs-kernel diff proves transcription, not measure","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"It is the strongest test in the project and it has a blind spot worth naming: the two implementations are *supposed* to compute the same thing, so a wrong pdf, a double-converted Jacobian or a doubled cosine makes them agree exactly as well as being right does. For direct lighting the closed form covered that gap. One bounce further there is no closed form, so the indirect term is anchored against a deterministic quadrature in a different parameterization \u2014 which never forms a pdf at all, and so cannot share the mistake. Measured: the Monte Carlo estimator and the quadrature agree to **1.6% or better** at three shading points, one of them on a sphere."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#differencing-beats-comparing-when-the-new-term-is-a-small-fraction-of-the-image","t":"Differencing beats comparing when the new term is a small fraction of the image","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"Two bounces are only ~13% brighter than one on the Cornell box, so a *completely broken* indirect term still lands inside the 2%-of-pixels tolerance a whole-image reference-vs-kernel comparison can afford. The fix is not a tighter tolerance: it is to make the indirect term the entire signal by subtracting the one-bounce image, which the RNG layout makes exact rather than statistical \u2014 vertex 0 keeps its stream, so the direct term *cancels* instead of being averaged away. The same trick converges the indirect anchor comparison in 8000 samples instead of millions. Another instance of the recurring lesson that which statistic you measure decides whether you can see the effect at all."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-stream-that-found-nothing-still-has-confidence-m-has-a-second-half-and-missing-it-cost-25","t":"\"A stream that found nothing still has confidence M\" has a second half, and missing it cost 25%","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"The rule was already known and already implemented *inside* `combine_streams`. What was still wrong is the step before: the code that decides which neighbours *become* streams filtered them on having a sample. Dropping an empty neighbour hands the canonical stream a weight of 1 on the taps that came up empty and `m_c < 1` on the taps that did not \u2014 the canonical gets counted twice, and the estimator runs bright in proportion to the number of neighbours. It hid in direct lighting for a simple reason: a reservoir of 8 or 32 *light* candidates is almost never empty. A path reservoir at `M = 1` is empty whenever the bounce ray escapes or its vertex is black, which on the Cornell box is about **70% of pixels**. Measured on the indirect term against a converged reference: | spatial reuse | K=1 | K=3 | K=5 | |---|---|---|---| | dropping empty neighbours | 1.086 | 1.201 | 1.284 | | counting their confidence | 1.002 | 1.006 | 1.005 | Three things about how it was found are worth keeping. The **total** image energy read 0.9953 \u2014 perfectly healthy \u2014 because the indirect term is 11% of that scene; differencing against the direct-lighting render was what made the signal visible, which is the same lesson the two-bounce work already recorded, now in its expensive form. The **per-pixel probes were useless**: a single pixel's estimate is heavy-tailed enough that a DI control showed the same \"bi"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#the-same-rule-inverted-for-the-temporal-pass-and-that-asymmetry-is-real","t":"The same rule inverted for the temporal pass, and that asymmetry is real","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"A spatial neighbour is a fresh reservoir over candidates drawn *this frame*, so its confidence belongs in the denominator whether or not it found something. The history is this pixel's own accumulated state, and an empty one carries an `M` that was already spent diluting earlier frames; feeding it back dilutes the canonical again every frame with nothing to replace it. Measured, treating the two the same way took the indirect term from 0.993 to **0.939**. The measurement stands and the asymmetry is still what the code does. The *explanation* above is not the whole story: the next finding shows the dominant effect in that number is duplicated samples, not spent confidence, and that `E[M]` is not what distinguishes the healthy configuration from the broken one."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-sample-that-travels-through-spatial-reuse-and-comes-back-is-counted-twice","t":"A sample that travels through spatial reuse and comes back is counted twice","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"The one defect where each reuse mechanism was individually within ~1% of the converged reference and the two together read **0.955**, with the deficit growing in `m_cap` \u2014 1/2/5/20/60 giving 0.985/0.981/0.973/0.955/0.939. Three explanations were measured and eliminated before the real one, and the eliminations are worth as much as the answer: * **Not a warm-up transient.** Flat across 512 frames; block 64 read 0.958 and block 480 read 0.948. There is no recovery to wait for. * **Not the randomness of `M`.** The obvious repair \u2014 always admit the history as a stream so the confidence recursion stops depending on whether a reservoir found anything \u2014 made it *worse*, 0.959 to 0.943, and degraded temporal-only reuse from 0.9975 to 0.9896. * **Not confidence accounting at all**, which is the surprise, because `M` really is being asked to mean two things (fresh candidate count, and accumulated confidence over a chain of merges). Separating the two counters cannot fix this, and the decisive measurement below is why. The mechanism is that temporal reuse makes a sample *persist* and spatial reuse *spreads* it, so the two together let one draw reach several pixels that then feed each other. Measured on `cornell_restir_gi`, the spatial pass's input buffer held only **31% distinct** reconnection vertices \u2014 3.2 copies of the average sample \u2014 where either pass alone leaves it 100% distinct. T"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#measuring-a-reuse-estimator-from-a-cold-start-hides-its-steady-state-bias-and-the-dilution-is-bigger-than-the-effect","t":"Measuring a reuse estimator from a cold start hides its steady-state bias, and the dilution is bigger than the effect","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"The natural protocol \u2014 reset, render N frames, compare the accumulated mean \u2014 is fine for a path tracer and wrong for ReSTIR, for a reason that has nothing to do with the correlation between frames that `reset_accumulation` already exists to handle. A ReSTIR run begins as something close to a path tracer and *becomes* the estimator under test over roughly `m_cap` frames, so an accumulation started at frame 0 averages two different estimators together. Measured on `cornell_restir_gi` at 96px, sweeping the number of warm-up frames discarded before a 200-spp accumulation: | warm-up frames | 0 | 8 | 24 | 64 | 128 | 256 | |---|---|---|---|---|---|---| | `mis` | 1.0037 | 0.9966 | 0.9919 | 0.9873 | 0.9872 | 0.9870 | | `naive` | 1.0010 | 0.9939 | 0.9892 | 0.9841 | 0.9842 | 0.9834 | The reading settles by 64 frames and does not move again through 256. From cold it reads **1.004 \u2014 above the truth, and indistinguishable from the uniform control at 1.0009 \u00b1 0.0004**. The whole 1.3% deficit is invisible under the protocol that looks most obviously correct. `--warmup` defaults to 64 for this reason, and the same discard is what makes the [reuse] breakdown readable. The control that keeps this honest is the no-reuse row, measured *with* the same warm-up: both passes off at `M = 1` is the two-bounce path tracer sample for sample, and it reads 1.0004 \u00b1 0.0027. So the discard is not itself creat"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#discarding-warm-up-frames-is-not-the-same-as-rewinding-the-sample-counter-and-the-reference-twin-could-only-do-the-first","t":"Discarding warm-up frames is not the same as rewinding the sample counter, and the reference twin could only do the first","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"`reset_accumulation()` clears the image, keeps the reservoirs *and* sets `spp_index` back to 0, so the measured frames draw uniforms `0 \u2026 spp-1` however long the warm-up was \u2014 which is exactly what `one_bounce_image` renders, and therefore what makes the direct term cancel **per sample** rather than averaging away over `spp`. The engine has always done this. `pt/core/render.py` did not: it took `spp` and nothing else, so the only warm-up it could express was slicing frames off the front, which leaves the measured frames on samples `warmup \u2026 warmup+spp-1` against a subtrahend on `0 \u2026 spp-1`. The residue is small and lands in the worst possible place. Measured on `gi_bias_demo` at 24px, the no-reuse row \u2014 which *is* the uniform control sample for sample \u2014 read **0.9989 against a control of 1.0099**, 0.011 apart, on a statistic whose entire job is to resolve a few percent. With the rewind the two agree to 3e-4, which is the `eps/d` reconnection residual and nothing else. Found by the Racket port, which needed the reference to reproduce a measurement only the kernels could previously make. The general form is worth more than the fix: **\"every estimator lives twice\" has to include the protocol the estimator is measured under**, not just the estimator \u2014 and a twin that cannot describe the experiment will not be noticed as long as nobody asks it to run one."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#reuse-loses-energy-that-mis-weighting-does-not-explain--open-and-now-measurable","t":"Reuse loses energy that MIS weighting does not explain \u2014 open, and now measurable","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"With the duplicated-sample fix in place and warm-up discarded, path reuse is still short of the converged indirect term, and the amount is a property of how far reuse reaches rather than of the weighting. On `gi_bias_demo`, whose twelve panels put a support boundary inside every reuse radius: | | uniform | no reuse | temporal only | spatial only | both | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | `mis` | 0.9996 | 1.0000 | 0.9740 | 0.9200 | 0.8960 | | `naive` | \u2014 | 1.0000 | 0.9740 | 0.8521 | 0.8463 | and on `cornell_restir_gi` the same shape an order of magnitude smaller \u2014 1.0009 / 1.0009 / 0.9950 / 0.9926 / 0.9863. Four things this pins down: * **The estimator is exact without reuse**, warmed, on both scenes. Whatever this is, it enters through a reuse pass. * **Temporal-only is not weighting-sensitive** \u2014 `mis` and `naive` agree to four decimals, exactly as the direct-lighting finding above says they must without reprojection \u2014 and it is *still* 2.6% short. So this is not the 1/M defect. * **MIS does real work and is not sufficient.** It recovers 0.06 of the 0.16 that 1/M loses on the spatial pass, and leaves 0.08. * **The deficit scales with the spatial radius**, at fixed resolution, `m_cap` and tap count: at 128px, radius 4/8/16/32 gives `mis` 0.9529/0.9287/0.9002/0.8960. Two hypotheses are already eliminated. It is **not a resolution artifact** of a 32px radius on a small image \u2014 64/128/2"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-metric-worth-having-is-worth-anchoring-and-the-anchor-found-the-bug","t":"A metric worth having is worth anchoring, and the anchor found the bug","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"FLIP was implemented here from the paper, and the paper's own constants left it 7% high on *every* achromatic difference. What found that was not review: it was running NVlabs' `flip-evaluator` once in a throwaway environment and diffing against it \u2014 the same move as `analytic.py` re-deriving its own vector math, one level up. The bug was in `cmax`, the distance the colour difference is normalized by. The natural derivation is the largest HyAB distance the sRGB gamut admits, green to blue, which comes to 308.12; the reference behaves as though it were 203.29. The value is now *measured* rather than derived, and measuring it took two constraints that pin it with no freedom left: on the neutral axis the response is `0.0575405 * d^0.7` across d in [1.7, 53] to six digits, which fixes `gpt/(gpc*cmax)`, and the upper branch of the piecewise redistribution then reproduces the reference at d = 80 to six digits as well. Fitting a curve to a black-box oracle is a poor way to obtain a constant and a very good way to discover that yours is wrong. What the anchor establishes, and what it does not: | | reference | here | |---|---|---| | identical images | 0.000000 | 0.000000 | | one-pixel edge shift, achromatic | 0.031368 | 0.031369 | | flat colour shift, pure chroma | 0.2782 | 0.3966 | | Cornell render, 8 spp vs converged | 0.1043 | 0.1136 | The achromatic path is **exact** \u2014 spatial CSF f"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#an-epsilon-that-keeps-a-jacobian-finite-must-not-reach-the-target-function","t":"An epsilon that keeps a Jacobian finite must not reach the target function","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"`reconnect` decides two things at once \u2014 whether a shift exists, and whether the target is non-zero \u2014 and a grazing-cosine threshold added for the first silently does the second. Refusing a *shift* is unbiased: the stream drops out of the MIS denominator as well as the sum, exactly as a geometric-gate rejection does. But the same threshold applied to `p_hat` throws away the **canonical** stream's own sample, the one the pixel actually traced, and nothing else covers it. A target that is zero where the contribution is not is biased, full stop. Found by the per-sample identity against the uniform path tracer and by nothing else \u2014 the image was fine, and every invariant test on the Jacobian still passed. Sidedness is now tested strictly, with no epsilon, and the Jacobian guards only against arithmetic with no answer. A grazing reconnection is a large finite number, which is the correct answer and a genuine firefly."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-bounce-rays-epsilon-and-a-shadow-rays-solve-different-problems-and-must-not-share-a-scale","t":"A bounce ray's epsilon and a shadow ray's solve different problems and must not share a scale","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"Reusing `shadow_epsilon` for the scattered ray looked like economy. A shadow ray's margin is scaled by its *own* length, because what it has to survive is a near-cancellation at its far endpoint; a bounce ray has no far endpoint yet, so the same formula scales it by the *primary* ray's length \u2014 ~1300 units in the Cornell box \u2014 and puts the origin 0.13 units off the surface. A bounce landing in a corner 0.37 units away then starts a third of the way along its own path. That is invisible in an image, and it is fatal to reuse, because the pdf belongs to the sampled direction while reconnection re-derives the direction from the stored vertex: the two stop being the same ray. Measured on the worst sample of the ReSTIR-GI-equals-path-tracer identity, it was **169%**. The fix is a separate constant, and its value was measured rather than argued \u2014 the two failure modes turn out not to overlap: | `SCATTER_REL` | identity error (worst) | ref-vs-kernel disagreement | mean ratio | |---|---|---|---| | `1e-4` (shadow scale) | **1.69** | 0.52% | 0.999957 | | `1e-5` | 5.4e-3 | 0.52% | 0.999957 | | `1e-6` | 5.9e-4 | 2.95% | 0.999477 | | `1e-7` | 1.0e-4 | **11.8%** | 0.996464 | `1e-5` is 300x better on the near-field error and costs *nothing at all* in reference-vs-kernel agreement; one step further and f32's own error in `hit.p` stops being negligible against the offset, so the two implementati"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#reciprocity-does-not-pin-a-jacobians-direction","t":"Reciprocity does not pin a Jacobian's direction","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"`J(a->b) * J(b->a) == 1` is the natural invariant to reach for, it catches a transposed term, and it holds just as well if the whole ratio is inverted \u2014 so it cannot tell a correct shift mapping from one that is upside down, which is precisely the error worth catching. The identity case has the same blind spot. What closes it is arithmetic done by hand for an asymmetric configuration: a vertex twice as close must give `J = 4`, not `1/4`. Both numbers are asserted, on both implementations, rather than one being derived from the other."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-read-modify-write-hazard-removed-rather-than-documented","t":"A read-modify-write hazard, removed rather than documented","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"Spatial reuse reading and writing `reservoir_curr` in place is a race the moment the pass runs in parallel: each pixel sees an arbitrary mixture of updated and stale neighbours. Reading `reservoir_temporal` and writing `reservoir_curr` costs one buffer and eliminates it."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#nine-scenes-and-55000-compared-values-still-had-two-blind-spots-and-only-mutation-testing-found-them","t":"Nine scenes and 55,000 compared values still had two blind spots, and only mutation testing found them","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"The step-3 conformance fixture compares the Racket port against the Python per ray and per sample across four scenes, and passed on the first run. Deliberately breaking the port then showed two defects it could not see at all: - **`EPS` is unreachable through a render.** Every ray a scene traces starts either at the camera, far from everything, or at a shading point already offset by `shadow_epsilon` \u2014 which is `1e-3` at minimum, an order of magnitude above `EPS = 1e-4`. So the guard that stops a ray re-hitting the surface it left never fires, and deleting it changes no pixel of any scene. It still has to be right, because bounce rays are what it exists for. - **Every quad in every bundled scene is a rectangle.** `intersect_quad` solves for `(s, r)` against a 2\u00d72 Gram matrix whose off-diagonal is `dot(u, v)`, and with rectangles that is exactly zero, so the two terms carrying it vanish identically. Corrupting one of them is a no-op on all nine scenes. Even a *sheared* quad mostly hides it: the corrupted `s` has to land outside `[0, 1]` while the correct one is inside, or the wrong solve still returns the same `t`. Both are now covered by calling the intersection routines directly with hand-chosen rays, rather than by hoping a scene reaches them. The general form is worth keeping: a fixture generated *from* the implementation tests the branches that implementation happens to tak"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#emitted_radiances-sidedness-test-is-dead-code-and-should-stay","t":"`emitted_radiance`'s sidedness test is dead code, and should stay","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"`intersect_scene` face-forwards the hit normal against the incoming ray, so `dot(n, wo) = dot(n, -dir) >= 0` for every hit any caller can construct \u2014 and every caller in both implementations gets its hit from the intersector. The `dot(n, wo) <= 0` branch that suppresses emission from a one-sided lamp's back face is therefore reachable only at exact tangency; over the coverage scene's emitter hits the smallest `dot(n, wo)` is 0.050. Inverting the test changes no pixel. It is left in place on both sides because the two implementations must read the same, and because a future intersector that stops face-forwarding would need it immediately."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#pairwise_mis-is-not-unconditionally-a-partition-of-unity-and-what-keeps-that-harmless-is-an-invariant-nothing-states","t":"`PAIRWISE_MIS` is not unconditionally a partition of unity, and what keeps that harmless is an invariant nothing states","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"The rule weights each non-canonical stream against the canonical one only: m_i(y) = (1/k) \u00b7 a_i / (a_i + b), m_c(y) = (1/k) \u00b7 \u03a3_i b / (a_i + b) Summing gives `(1/k) \u00b7 \u03a3_i (a_i + b)/(a_i + b)` = 1 \u2014 but only over the `i` where `a_i + b > 0`. A pair with `a_i + b = 0` is `0/0`, is skipped, and contributes to *neither* weight, so the total comes out `k_valid / k`. That needs the canonical stream to be blind to `y` **and** at least one neighbour to be too, which is unreachable on a scene where the canonical sees everything its neighbours see \u2014 and that is exactly the configuration `tests/test_core.py::TestMisWeights` uses (four streams on one wall, one light), which is why it has always passed. Measured over the samples the estimator actually weights, at 32\u00d732: | scene | shortfall rate | mean deficit | worst sum | |---|---|---|---| | `bias_demo` | **3.8%** | 0.51 | 0.25 | | `many_lights` | 1.8% | 0.44 | 0.50 | | `cornell_restir` | 0% | \u2014 | \u2014 | The balance heuristic is 0% on all three: it renormalizes over the whole denominator, so a stream that cannot produce `y` drops out and the rest take up the slack. **It changes no pixel, and not by luck.** `b` is `M_c \u00b7 p\u0302(streams[0].hit, y)`, and every call site passes the canonical stream's own hit as the merge target \u2014 so `b = 0` exactly when `combine_streams`' own factor `p\u0302(hit_q, y)` is 0, and `w = m_i \u00b7 p\u0302_q \u00b7 W_i \u00b7 J` is zero whatever"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#two-of-the-render-graphs-five-compile-stages-are-dead-code-on-every-shipped-scene","t":"Two of the render graph's five compile stages are dead code on every shipped scene","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"Measured across all nine bundled scenes and four configuration variants: **nothing is ever culled, and nothing is ever aliased** \u2014 `n_physical` equals the resource count in all thirteen. Culling finds nothing because every AOV and every persistent resource is kept alive by `default_keep_alive` and the graph declares no pass whose output nobody reads; aliasing finds nothing because the remaining transients have distinct descriptors, so every bucket holds one resource. The topological sort is likewise the identity on all thirteen, because the builder declares passes in dependency order already, so its tie-break decides nothing. That is not an argument for deleting them \u2014 a graph is a substrate for graphs that do not exist yet, and `assemble_di_graph` is one client. It is an argument for testing them on graphs built for the purpose. Four synthetic graphs now do: a dead pass, an aliasing chain, a diamond declared sink-first, and a persistent resource read at v0. A fifth exists solely because first-fit and last-fit slot assignment agree unless *two* slots are free at once \u2014 with one free slot the rule is untested however many aliasing graphs there are. The general lesson is the one steps 3 and 4 recorded, in its sharpest form so far: **a fixture generated from an implementation tests the branches that implementation takes**, and on a render graph with one client that can be less tha"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#describe-is-a-stronger-acceptance-test-than-it-looks","t":"`describe()` is a stronger acceptance test than it looks","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"It prints slot numbers, so requiring it character-for-character transitively pins declaration order, the topological tie-break, culling, the lifetime intervals and the greedy colouring's first-fit rule \u2014 five things with no numeric output of their own. A port that gets any of them subtly different produces a plan that behaves identically and prints differently, which is exactly the divergence worth catching: it means the two are no longer the same schedule, and every later claim about buffer reuse is about a different graph."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#restir-gi-at-m1-with-reuse-off-is-the-two-bounce-path-tracer-was-written-in-five-places-and-is-not-true","t":"\"ReSTIR GI at M=1 with reuse off *is* the two-bounce path tracer\" was written in five places and is not true","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"What `gi_cand_dim`'s alias buys is that the two *draw the same numbers*, so they can be compared per sample rather than statistically. Their outputs still differ: the bounce ray leaves from `x1 + eps\u00b7n`, so the vertex it finds is not exactly on the ray from `x1` \u2014 the path tracer evaluates the integrand at the direction it **sampled**, reuse re-derives the direction from the **stored vertex**, and the two differ by about `eps / d`. Measured at 20\u00d720 on `cornell_restir_gi`: 103 of 185 samples agree bit for bit, one exceeds 1e-3, the worst is 7.3e-3, and the totals agree to 2.7e-5. `tests/test_core.py::TestGiRestirIdentity` states it correctly and asserts the residual's *shape* \u2014 most samples exact, few above 1e-3, sums within 1e-3 \u2014 rather than a tolerance that would hide a structural error in the tail. The docstrings and the porting spec did not, and the wording invites exactly the equality assertion that then fails with no explanation attached. All five now say what the alias actually buys."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#cpythons-sum-over-floats-is-not-a-left-fold","t":"CPython's `sum()` over floats is not a left fold","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"Since 3.12 it uses Neumaier compensated summation, so `sum([0.1]*10)` is exactly `1.0` where folding left gives `0.9999999999999999`. `core/reservoir.py` uses it in two places that matter \u2014 the balance heuristic's denominator and NAIVE's `\u03a3 M_j` \u2014 and a reimplementation that folds left disagrees in the last place. The disagreement appears **only with three or more live streams**, because compensated and naive summation coincide for two, which means it is invisible in the temporal merge and shows up only under spatial reuse. It cost an afternoon to find precisely because a one-ulp difference sits under any tolerance loose enough to allow for libm. The accuracy is wanted on its own terms, independently of which language provides it: the denominator is `\u03a3_j M_j p\u0302_j(y)` over streams whose targets can span orders of magnitude \u2014 the entire premise of `many_lights` \u2014 so the naive fold's error is largest exactly where resampling matters most. The port implements Neumaier explicitly rather than inheriting it."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#a-tolerance-that-is-never-used-should-still-be-asserted-at-zero","t":"A tolerance that is never used should still be asserted at zero","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"Both conformance suites allow a `1e-12` relative tolerance for libm and then separately *require* every compared value to be exactly equal, because the two claims are different and only the second one catches a one-ulp defect. Reverting the compensated sum above is invisible to the tolerance and immediately visible to the exactness check. The split also keeps the failure legible: if the exactness check is the only one failing, the port is fine and the platform's libm differs."},{"u":"explanation/findings.html#two-independent-implementations-of-the-same-f64-arithmetic-agreed-bit-for-bit-across-55184-values","t":"Two independent implementations of the same f64 arithmetic agreed bit for bit, across 55,184 values","q":"Explanation \u00b7 finding","h":"The Racket port's acceptance check allows a `1e-12` relative tolerance for the three libm calls in the path \u2014 `tan` in the camera basis, `sqrt` in every length, `sin`/`cos` in the sphere light. Measured, the tolerance was never used: every compared value, including radiance accumulated through a shadow ray and a geometry term, was exactly equal. The tolerance stays, because a different platform libm is a real possibility and the alternative is a suite that fails for a reason it cannot explain; but the useful fact is that \"same operations in the same order\" is a stronger guarantee in practice than it sounds, and a port that needs a loose tolerance to pass has a structural difference, not a rounding one. (`port/racket/test/geometry-conformance.rkt`) ---"},{"u":"explanation/findings.html","t":"Findings worth keeping","q":"Explanation","h":"Forty things that were measured rather than assumed \u2014 including the hypotheses that were eliminated, which are worth as much as the answers. Explanation Findings worth keeping Forty things that were measured rather than assumed \u2014 including the hypotheses that were eliminated, which are worth as much as the answers. Source: ARCHITECTURE.md understanding-oriented Things that were measured rather than assumed, and that took real work to get right. Each is documented at the code that depends on it. fixed \u2014 a real defect, found and corrected open \u2014 measured, unexplained, still true of the code today method \u2014 no defect: a lesson about how to measure, or a trap avoided non-finding \u2014 a negative result worth keeping \u2014 dead code that should stay, a latent defect held shut, or a check that passed Status fixed open method non-finding Tag bias engine epsilon geometry gi graph measurement perceptual port reuse reuse-algebra rng scene-design taichi temporal testing threading twin-diff variance 40 findings A shadow-ray epsilon that cancels itself # fixed epsilon geometry twin-diff Offsetting the ray origin forward by eps and shortening the far end by eps makes the two cancel, putting the light's own surface exactly on the t < limit boundary \u2014 a comparison then decided by rounding, which f64 and f32 resolve differently. It caused 31% of pixels to disagree between the reference and the kernel. Shortening by twice the offset, with a scene-scale-relative term, took that to 0.2%. documented at core/geometry.shadow_epsilon ti.template() does not thread a scalar counter # fixed rng taichi The natural stateful RNG \u2014 rand(seed) advancing a caller-local counter \u2014 fails silently in Taichi: every draw in a kernel returns the same number, and the output still looks like plausible noise. The fix was the design the architecture already called for: fully stateless, explicitly-indexed draws. documented at kernels/_common.py A seed that never reached the RNG # fixed rng The scene's :seed flowed through the whole IR and was never mixed into the hash, so two \"independent\" reference renders were byte-identical and the measured noise floor was exactly zero. documented at core/rng.hash4 Bias detection needs both estimators' variances # method measurement bias E[MSE(X, Y)] = vx + vy + bias\u00b2 . Comparing against the reference's noise floor alone charges the estimator's own variance to bias \u2014 uniform MC at 400 spp against a uniform reference at 4000 spp reports as badly biased, though it is the reference's estimator. documented at validation/bias.bias_report The sign test assumes independent pixels; reuse violates that # method measurement reuse ReSTIR's neighbouring pixels literally share samples, so a per-pixel binomial test is wildly over-confident and flags every configuration, including provably-unbiased ones. Subsampling beyond the reuse radius fixes it \u2014 but too large a stride leaves no statistical power, so sign_test_n is always reported. Which statistic you measure decides whether you see the effect # method measurement variance For the variance ordering (uniform > RIS > ReSTIR), the mean per-pixel variance is dominated by a handful of fireflies and moved under 5% across estimators; the median samples pixels that were never noisy, where RIS genuinely loses. At the 90th percentile \u2014 where perceptible noise lives \u2014 the expected ordering is clean: RIS 1.9\u00d7 better than uniform, ReSTIR 1.5\u00d7 better than RIS. ReSTIR must be measured per frame, not accumulated # method measurement reuse Consecutive frames are strongly correlated through the reservoirs, so averaging N of them is worth far fewer than N independent samples. Comparing accumulated means makes ReSTIR look worse than plain RIS. documented at Engine.reset_accumulation \"Many lights\" is not a light count # method scene-design Sixteen lamps in a ceiling grid look like the regime resampling is for, but every floor point sees them at similar distance and angle, their target values differ by under 2\u00d7, and RIS beat uniform by ~2%. Scattering the same sixteen through the volume at different heights, sizes and intensities is what creates the condition. documented at scenes/many_lights.edn Where 1/M reuse actually goes wrong \u2014 and why it took a purpose-built scene # method scene-design reuse-algebra bias The intuitive place to look for spatial-reuse bias is a geometry discontinuity. It is the wrong place: the geometric similarity gate rejects those neighbours, so it is already doing, crudely, the job MIS does properly. On an ordinary Cornell box :naive and :mis differed by 0.2% \u2014 under the noise floor. The bias needs neighbours that pass the gate \u2014 same surface, same normal, same depth \u2014 yet disagree about which samples are possible . Lights standing perpendicular to a flat floor produce exactly that: cos_light flips sign along a razor-sharp line across a plane, so two adjacent pixels are geometrically identical while one assigns zero probability to samples the other can produce. On scenes/bias_demo.edn , :naive converges to 60% of the correct energy ; :mis and :pairwise-mis land within 3%. The detection sweep is the clearest statement of what bias is : spp naive excess mis excess naive energy vs truth 250 +1.71x \u22120.16x 0.603 600 +4.39x \u22120.03x 0.596 1200 +5.74x \u22120.32x 0.594 2000 +5.64x \u22120.33x 0.597 The energy deficit is constant ; only the noise hiding it shrinks. And the biased estimator is 15x less noisy than the correct one while being 12x further from the truth \u2014 clean, stable, and wrong. Tonemapped, the two renders are hard to tell apart, which is the whole argument for measuring instead of looking. Bias also shows in the convergence curve's shape, sooner than in its MSE # method measurement bias slope_verdict fits the tail of the curve: an unbiased estimator keeps falling near -1, a biased one flattens onto its bias-squared plateau. It needs only the curve to have turned over, not full convergence, so it fires earlier than the excess-over-noise test. Fitting the whole curve instead of the tail dilutes the evidence, because early checkpoints fall steeply either way. A scene can be pathological in a way that breaks the statistics # method scene-design measurement The first version of bias_demo used small, intense emitters. Uniform sampling's error went heavy-tailed, a handful of fireflies dominated the MSE, one realization landed 15x above its own expectation, and the harness reported uniform MC as biased against a reference uniform MC had produced \u2014 with a convergence slope of +0.71 , error rising with sample count. Nothing was biased; a statistic was being applied to a distribution it does not describe. Dialling brightness down kept the support-splitting effect and made every estimator well behaved. Temporal reuse is unbiased under every mode without reprojection \u2014 and that is informative # method reuse-algebra temporal With no reprojection the canonical and temporal streams share a shading point and therefore a target function, so the balance heuristic collapses exactly to the 1/M form: measured, the three weightings agree to f32 rounding. The :bias toggle genuinely does nothing to that pass. It says the bias in reuse comes from combining different domains \u2014 which is why it lives in the spatial pass, and why it returns the moment reprojection is turned on. documented at kernels/restir.py Reprojecting a static scene correctly gives back the same shading point, so the bias has to come from somewhere else \u2014 and where it comes from is magnification # method temporal reuse-algebra This was the surprise. wi and dist are re-derived exactly from the stored world-space point, and the target function is view-independent for a Lambertian surface, so a correctly reprojected pixel lands on the point it started from. A large lateral camera slide changes nothing measurable. The residual error is the pixel quantization : hits_prev[floor(projection)] is whatever that pixel's jittered ray hit, up to one previous-frame footprint away along the surface. That footprint is measured in the previous frame's resolution on that surface, so it is only large when the surface was smaller on screen then \u2014 i.e. under magnification. Dolly the history camera back by k and the reprojected point moves k current footprints, which is spatial reuse at radius k arriving through the temporal pass with no MIS weight to correct it. ( validation/temporal.py ) history magnified mis energy naive energy naive MSE excess 1x (still) 0.9990 0.9987 \u22120.10x 3x 0.9994 0.9930 \u22120.02x 6x 1.0024 0.9917 +0.02x 12x 1.0044 0.9711 +0.09x 20x 0.9920 0.9445 +2.57x 40x 0.9976 0.8902 +7.64x mis sits on the reference at every setting while naive degrades smoothly, and pairwise-mis is bit-identical to mis here \u2014 with exactly two streams the pairwise rule reduces to the balance heuristic term for term. A moving camera has no single reference, unless the camera comes back # method measurement temporal Per-frame comparison against a converged reference would need one reference per p"},{"u":"explanation/porting.html","t":"Porting to Racket","q":"Explanation","h":"The essential-vs-incidental split, the eight-step order of work, and the traps already hit at real cost. Explanation Porting to Racket The essential-vs-incidental split, the eight-step order of work, and the traps already hit at real cost. Source: docs/architecture/porting-to-racket.md understanding-oriented A specification for reimplementing the thinking layers of this system as #lang restir/estimator , treating the Python as the reference implementation. Scope. Port pt/dsl/ , pt/graph/ , and pt/core/ \u2014 the front end, the render graph, and the pure-f64 reference that is the denotational spec. The Taichi kernels and the converged-reference oracle stay in Python. The goal is not a faster renderer. It is a substrate where the estimator is something you write and the compiler can check \u2014 where ARCHITECTURE.md 's findings stop being commit diffs and become programs you can hold side by side. Why Racket, in one detail # The original design brief said \"use a Python EDN library.\" The shipped reader is 283 hand-written lines . The reason is spans : a stock reader gives you none, and spans are what let errors.py 's 23-code catalog point a caret at the offending form. syntax/parse provides exactly that, free \u2014 source locations threaded through macro expansion, structured error reporting, and a macro stepper that shows the lowering happening. The port's justification is not performance or elegance; it is that this design had to hand-build the one thing the host would have given it. Essential vs incidental # The distinction that governs everything below. Reproducing the incidental column is wasted work; getting the essential column wrong produces a plausible, wrong renderer. Essential \u2014 semantics Incidental \u2014 Python/Taichi artifact The RNG dimension layout, exactly ( semantics \u00a78 ) that constants are duplicated in two files p\u0302 and J as a pair of hooks , two inhabitants that ReuseDomain is a frozen dataclass Both Jacobian directions, at their two distinct sites that DI elides \u00d7 1 in the kernels Empty streams carry M , in the merge and at stream selection that usable() tests M > 0 rather than .valid Phase separation between temporal and spatial that it is expressed as two buffers GI rolls pre-spatial, DI rolls post-spatial _make_advance being a @ti.kernel Source-ordered index assignment that ids are dense Python ints \u03a3\u1d62 m\u1d62(y) = 1 , and m\u1d62(y\u1d62) not summing to one which of mis_weights_at / _mis_weights is public The two epsilons and their different scalings the exact values, if your units differ ( re-measure ) Warm-up before measuring a reuse estimator --warmup defaulting to 64 Unknown scene keys are hard errors the did_you_mean edit distance Area-measure light pdfs, solid-angle path pdfs LightSample carrying both pdf fields \u2014 FieldsBuilder ownership, thread rules, SNode trees \u2014 all Taichi-specific \u2014 ~5s engine construction cost, and every API shaped around avoiding it The single most transferable line: if your reimplementation and the Python draw the same numbers, you can diff them per sample. If they do not, you are back to statistics, and statistics on ~11% of an image will not see a defect losing a sixth of the indirect term. What Racket gives you free # Python, hand-written Racket reader.py (283) \u2014 tokenizer, parser, Span the reader; syntax-source/line/column macro.py (251) \u2014 defmacro , fixpoint, depth cap, span inheritance define-syntax , hygiene, the macro stepper parts of analyze.py (392) \u2014 arity and shape checks syntax/parse patterns with #:declare errors.py (107) \u2014 ElabError , Diagnostics , caret rendering raise-syntax-error , DrRacket arrows frozen dataclasses + replace() throughout struct with #:transparent , functional update Roughly 900 of pt/dsl/ 's 2,020 lines evaporate. elaborate.py does not \u2014 its five jobs (resolve, default, normalize, stitch, expand) are domain logic, not parsing. Do not lose the accumulate-then-raise behaviour. raise-syntax-error reports the first problem; Diagnostics reports all of them. A scene with four mistakes should report four. This needs deliberate work in Racket, and it is worth it. What becomes writable # The point of the exercise. In the Python these are hardcoded; all of them are already data in shape . Step 8 built this, and the sketches below are close to what shipped. port/racket/estimator.rkt is the language and port/racket/estimators.rkt is written in it. Three differences from the sketch are worth recording, because each was forced by the code rather than chosen: A rule needs an id , not just a name. The weighting is selected by an integer that the scene IR and the Taichi kernels both agree on, so a rule has to be addressable across the device boundary. define-mis-rule takes #:id , and registering two rules on one id is an error. define-rng-layout has to separate bounces from blocks . A bounce is a stream id; a block is the repeating group inside it. Bounce 1 is \"shading\" and the thing repeated inside it is a candidate \u2014 conflating them, as the sketch did, cannot express the layout that actually exists. The frame loop's third pass needs an adapter and the first two do not. initial+temporal and spatial have identical shapes across both payloads; resolve differs by what else is in the pixel \u2014 GI adds a next-event sample at the primary hit \u2014 so it is declared as a procedure rather than named. ( define -reuse-domain gi #:target gi-phat-at #:jacobian reconnection-jacobian ) ; |d\u03c9_q / d\u03c9_r|, both cosines at x2 ( define -mis-rule balance #:weights ( \u03bb ( streams y ) ( normalize ( for/list ( [ s streams ] ) ( density s y ) ) ) ) ) ( define -pass ReSTIR.GI.Spatial #:reads [ hits reservoir_temporal ] #:writes [ reservoir_curr ] #:dirty [ camera scene ] ) ( define -rng-layout [ bounce shade #:stride 4 #:slots ( pick u v accept ) ] [ bounce bsdf #:stride 2 #:slots ( u v ) ] [ bounce gi-cand #:stride 3 #:slots ( u v accept ) #:aliases ( bsdf 0 ) ] ) Two things a language buys that a library does not: Findings become diffs. Finding 23 \u2014 the defect that took the most work \u2014 is two lines: ( define -frame-loop restir-gi #:passes ( initial+temporal spatial resolve ) - #:history reservoir_curr ) ; a sample can travel and return + #:history reservoir_temporal ) ; duplication forced to 1.00 Obligations become checkable. The unbiasedness requirement currently lives in a docstring and a parametrized test. As a judgment it can be stated once and randomly tested against every rule anyone writes \u2014 including the quantifier distinction that prose keeps blurring: ( define -judgment-form partition-of-unity #:contract ( \u22a2 rule streams y ) [ ------------------------------------------- ( \u22a2 rule streams y ) ( side-condition ( \u2248 1.0 ( \u03a3 ( m\u1d62 rule streams y ) ) ) ) ] ) Redex can generate streams and y and look for a counterexample. Note this must be stated over a fixed y \u2014 m\u1d62(y\u1d62) , which the estimator actually uses, does not sum to one. A define-rng-layout form can likewise check injectivity statically, which is the class of bug that currently only shows up as plausible noise. Order of work # Each step ends with something checkable against the Python. # Step Done when 1 RNG. pcg , hash4 , uniform_from , the full dimension layout. \u2705 done Every value in conformance/rng_vectors.json reproduces exactly. Nothing else can be verified until this holds. 2 Front end. Reader, macros, analyze, elaborate, IR, writer. \u2705 done Every scene lowers to the same IR field-for-field ( conformance/scene_ir.json ), and to-edn round-trips all nine. 3 Geometry, camera, lights, shading. Both epsilons; area-measure pdfs. \u2705 done Every value in conformance/geometry.json reproduces: intersect_scene per ray, di_uniform per sample , over four scenes. 55,184 values, all bit-identical. 4 The reuse algebra. absorb , the two finalizes, ReuseDomain , the three MIS rules, combine . \u2705 done Every value in conformance/reservoir.json reproduces, including the ReSTIR frame loop per sample over five configurations; the \u03a3 m\u1d62(y) property holds under random generation \u2014 with the one exception it turned up, below. 5 Reconnection. The shift, the Jacobian, gi_phat_at . \u2705 done Every value in conformance/reconnect.json reproduces. J = 4, not 1/4 on the asymmetric case, checked against the hand-derived answer as well as the Python's; J(a\u2192b)\u00b7J(b\u2192a) = 1 ; and ReSTIR GI at M=1 reuse-off against two-bounce uniform \u2014 which is not bit-identical, see below. 6 The graph. Resources, PassDecl , the five compile stages, dirty propagation. \u2705 done describe matches character for character on all nine bundled scenes plus four variants; dirty_steps matches on all 32 subsets of the five domains, not just the five. Plus five synthetic graphs, because culling and aliasing are dead code on every shipped scene ( conformance/graph.json ). 7 Frame loop. Carried state, phase separation, per-frame cameras, reprojection, the warm-up rewind. \u2705 done Every value in conformance/frameloop.json reproduces \u2014 26,128, all bit-identical \u2014 and the warmed indirect energy table reproduces to the last place , including "},{"u":"explanation/racket-port.html","t":"What the Racket port showed","q":"Explanation","h":"Status of all eight steps, and the arguments the port settled by being written. Explanation What the Racket port showed Status of all eight steps, and the arguments the port settled by being written. Source: port/racket/README.md understanding-oriented A reimplementation of this project's thinking layers \u2014 the DSL front end, the render graph, and the pure reference estimator \u2014 in Racket, so that the estimator becomes something you write rather than something hardcoded. The plan, the essential-vs-incidental split, and the order of work are in docs/architecture/porting-to-racket.md . The semantics being reproduced are in docs/architecture/semantics.md . Status # Step 1 RNG \u2014 pcg , hash4 , uniform-from , rand1 / rand2 , the full dimension layout \u2705 rng.rkt 2 Front end \u2014 reader, macros, analyze, elaborate, IR, writer \u2705 edn.rkt macro.rkt elaborate.rkt ir.rkt writer.rkt 3 Geometry, camera, lights, shading \u2014 both epsilons, area-measure pdfs, di-uniform \u2705 vecmath.rkt types.rkt geometry.rkt camera.rkt bsdf.rkt lights.rkt shading.rkt integrators/ 4 The reuse algebra \u2014 absorb , both finalizes, reuse-domain , the three MIS rules, combine-streams , and the ReSTIR DI frame loop \u2705 reservoir.rkt render.rkt integrators/di-ris.rkt integrators/di-restir.rkt 5 Reconnection \u2014 the shift, the Jacobian, gi-phat-at , and the ReSTIR GI frame loop \u2705 reconnect.rkt integrators/gi-uniform.rkt integrators/gi-restir.rkt 6 The render graph \u2014 resources, pass-decl , the five compile stages, dirty propagation \u2705 graph/resource.rkt graph/pass.rkt graph/plan.rkt graph/compile.rkt graph/builder.rkt 7 The frame loop \u2014 carried state, per-frame cameras, reprojection, the warm-up rewind, warmed indirect energy \u2705 render.rkt campath.rkt 8 The estimator DSL \u2014 define-reuse-domain , define-mis-rule , define-frame-loop , define-rng-layout \u2705 estimator.rkt estimators.rkt Run the tests # raco test port/racket # everything port/racket/test/mutations.sh # break the port on purpose, ~3 min 70 mutations are caught and 6 are documented as not caught, with reasons. No installation needed \u2014 the tests require by relative path. To make (require restir/rng) work from anywhere: raco pkg install --link port/racket What step 2 showed # pt/dsl/reader.py is 283 hand-written lines \u2014 a tokenizer, a recursive-descent parser, and a Span record threaded through every node. edn.rkt replaces it with six lines of readtable configuration , because EDN is s-expressions and read-syntax attaches source, line, column and span to every node for free. Three parameters arrange the differences: #%brackets / #%braces tags to keep the vector/map distinction, and a readtable entry making , contribute nothing. That is the port's whole argument, delivered: the Python had to rebuild by hand exactly what the host already provides \u2014 and the spans it gets back are better , because they are real syntax objects that survive macro expansion. The rest of the front end does not evaporate. elaborate.rkt is 490 lines against the Python's 387, because its five jobs \u2014 resolve, default, normalize, stitch, expand \u2014 are domain logic, not parsing. One thing deliberately does not use the host's machinery: raise-syntax-error reports the first problem, while a scene with four mistakes should report four, so diagnostics accumulate explicitly and raise once. What step 3 showed # Nothing evaporates here. pt/core/ 's geometry, camera, lights and shading are domain arithmetic, and the Racket is within a few lines of the Python file for file \u2014 which is the useful negative result: the port's argument is about the front end and the estimator, not about arithmetic, and this step is where you would notice if that claim were being oversold. What it did produce is a stronger acceptance test than expected. test/geometry-conformance.rkt compares 55,184 values against conformance/geometry.json \u2014 per ray for intersect-scene , per (pixel, sample) for di-uniform , over four scenes \u2014 and every one of them is bit-identical , not within tolerance. The suite allows a 1e-12 relative tolerance for the three libm calls in the path ( tan in the camera basis, sqrt in every length, sin / cos in the sphere light); measured, it is never used. The tolerance stays for a different platform's libm, but the practical reading is that a port needing a loose tolerance has a structural difference rather than a rounding one. Two things are worth carrying into step 4: (values ...) where the Python returns a tuple. eval-contribution returns four values and nee-sample returns four; define-values at every call site is more honest than a struct, because these are genuinely positional results with no independent identity. The fixture's sections are ordered by how a failure propagates. A bad shadow epsilon leaves rays clean and fails from occlusion on; a dropped light-selection probability leaves occlusion 's geometry clean and fails light-samples . Checking only the final radiance would collapse every defect into \"the image is wrong\", which is exactly the diagnostic position this project spent the most effort escaping. What mutation testing found that the fixture did not # The step-3 fixture passed on its first run. Breaking the port on purpose \u2014 23 edits, test/mutations.sh \u2014 found two defects it could not see at all, and both were about reachability rather than arithmetic: EPS is unreachable through a render. Every ray a scene traces starts at the camera or at a shading point already offset by shadow-epsilon , which is ten times larger. Deleting the guard that stops a ray re-hitting its own surface changed no pixel of any scene. Every quad in every bundled scene is a rectangle. intersect-quad solves against a 2\u00d72 Gram matrix whose off-diagonal is dot(u, v) ; with rectangles that is exactly zero and the terms carrying it vanish. Corrupting one is a no-op on all nine scenes \u2014 and even a sheared quad hides it unless the corrupted (s, r) lands outside the unit square while the correct one is inside, since otherwise the same t comes back. Both are now pinned by calling the intersection routines directly, and coverage_scene.edn carries a sheared quad, a sphere light, a two-sided light and a plane occluder \u2014 four branches no shipped scene reaches. Twenty mutations are caught. Three are documented as not caught, which is the part worth keeping: emitted-radiance 's sidedness branch is unreachable once intersect-scene face-forwards the normal (the smallest dot(n, wo) over the coverage scene's emitter hits is 0.050), bsdf-eval 's dispatch is dead until a second material exists, and vlerp has no caller in the ported surface at all. What step 4 found # The reuse algebra ported cleanly \u2014 reservoir.rkt is 300 lines against the Python's 372, and test/reservoir-conformance.rkt compares 53,311 values across five configurations, including the whole three-pass frame loop per pixel per frame, all bit-identical. What is worth reading is the three things that were not clean. CPython's sum() over floats is not a left fold. Since 3.12 it is Neumaier compensated, so sum([0.1]*10) is exactly 1.0 where (apply + ...) gives 0.9999999999999999 . mis_weights_at uses it for the balance heuristic's denominator, and the disagreement appears only at three or more live streams \u2014 compensated and naive summation coincide for two \u2014 so it is invisible in the two-stream temporal merge and shows up under spatial reuse. vecmath.rkt implements Neumaier explicitly. Two consequences worth carrying: the accuracy is wanted on its own terms, since that denominator sums targets spanning orders of magnitude; and a 1e-12 tolerance cannot see a one-ulp defect , so both conformance suites now allow the tolerance and separately assert exactness. Python's negative list indexing has no equivalent here, and that is a feature. The first fixture ran generate_candidates at neighbour pixels whose primary ray had missed, reaching scene.materials[-1] \u2014 which in Python quietly returns the last material and produces a plausible number. Racket raised. The fix was to the fixture, not to the port: the renderer never runs initial RIS at a miss, so pinning the Python's answer there would have frozen an accident into the contract. PAIRWISE-MIS is not unconditionally a partition of unity. Running \u03a3\u1d62 m\u1d62(y) = 1 over randomly generated stream configurations \u2014 which is what step 4's acceptance actually asks for \u2014 showed the pairwise rule summing to k_valid/k whenever the canonical stream is blind to y and some neighbour is too, because that pair is 0/0 , is skipped, and contributes to neither weight. On a real bias_demo render 3.8% of the weighted samples fall short, by 0.51 on average. It changes no pixel, because b = 0 implies the merge's own p\u0302_q(y) is 0 and the contribution was zero regardless \u2014 but only as long as streams[0].hit is the merge target, which is true at every call site and enforced at none. test/mis-properties.rkt pins the counterexample, the measurement and the invariant. The Python's own test cannot see it: four streams on one wall lit by"},{"u":"tutorials/index.html","t":"Tutorials","q":"Tutorials","h":"Lessons. You follow along, something happens, and you end up able to do a thing you could not do before. Start here if you have never rendered anything. Can you teach me to\u2026?"},{"u":"how-to/index.html","t":"How-to guides","q":"How-to guides","h":"Recipes for a goal you already have. They assume you know what you are doing and skip the explaining. How do I\u2026?"},{"u":"reference/index.html","t":"Reference","q":"Reference","h":"Signatures, contracts, invariants and the semantics of the estimator, stated precisely enough to check. Consult it; do not read it through. What exactly is\u2026?"},{"u":"explanation/index.html","t":"Explanation","q":"Explanation","h":"The reasoning, the alternatives that were rejected, and the evidence log \u2014 what was measured rather than assumed, including the hypotheses that were eliminated. Why is it like this?"}];
